/* Built by tools/build-split.py from data.js — do not edit. e4a, SDLs 37, 38, 39, 40, 41, 42, 43 */
(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e4a.5acf922381.js"]={"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure of tubular epithelial cells sits at the center of the favored hypothesis for cyst formation in polycystic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Brush border microvillus array","B":"Cilium-centrosome complex","C":"Tight junction protein complex","D":"Basolateral membrane infoldings","E":"Apical water channel vesicles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins states that the currently favored hypothesis places the cilia-centrosome complex of tubular epithelial cells at the center of polycystic disease. Tubular cells carry a single nonmotile primary cilium that projects into the lumen from the apical surface and arises from a basal body derived from the centriole. Ciliopathies include polycystic kidney disease and nephronophthisis. Brush border microvilli and basolateral infoldings are absorptive and transport specializations, not the sensory cilium at the center of the cyst hypothesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: polycystic kidney disease and nephronophthisis are ciliopathies, so think primary cilium when a cystic kidney disease is described. COMLEX: cystic kidney diseases are a cause of chronic kidney disease, and family history is the key clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 1 — defective signaling in tubular epithelial cells that drives cyst development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"The nonmotile primary cilium of kidney tubular cells monitors changes in which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Luminal chloride concentration","B":"Interstitial oncotic pressure","C":"Fluid flow and shear stress","D":"Circulating hormone levels","E":"Tubular fluid acidity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The apical cilia of kidney tubule cells function as a mechanosensor that monitors changes in fluid flow and shear stress. In response, these sensors regulate ion flux and cellular behavior, including cell polarity and proliferation. It is a mechanical sensor, not a detector of luminal chloride, interstitial oncotic pressure or circulating hormones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cilium is a flow sensor, and loss of that sensing drives cystogenesis. COMLEX: cysts arise when mechanosensing, calcium flux, and signal transduction are defective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 1 — defective signaling in tubular epithelial cells that drives cyst development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Bending of the primary cilium by tubular fluid flow normally regulates flux of which ion through the polycystin complex?","choices":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Potassium","C":"Chloride","D":"Calcium","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Polycystin-1 and polycystin-2 appear to form a complex that regulates intracellular calcium in response to fluid flow, perhaps because fluid moving through the tubules causes ciliary bending that opens calcium channels. Loss of the complex alters intracellular calcium, which affects proliferation, apoptosis, and secretion. The polycystin complex is described as regulating calcium, not sodium, potassium, chloride or magnesium flux.","boardPrep":"Step 1: polycystin-2 is a calcium-permeable cation channel, and disturbed calcium signaling is the proposed cyst mechanism. COMLEX: the decrease in calcium is thought to stimulate proliferation and secretion by the cyst-lining cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 1 — defective signaling in tubular epithelial cells that drives cyst development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The polycystin complex is desc","B":"The polycystin complex is desc","C":"The polycystin complex is desc","E":"The polycystin complex is desc"}},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":1,"stem":"Mutations in which pair of genes cause almost all cases of adult polycystic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"PKD1 and PKD2","B":"PKD1 and PKHD1","C":"PKD2 and NPHP1","D":"PKHD1 and NPHP1","E":"PKHD1 and COL4A5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Almost all cases of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease result from mutations in PKD1, which encodes polycystin-1 and accounts for about 85 percent of cases, or PKD2, which encodes polycystin-2. PKHD1 is the gene of the autosomal recessive childhood form, and the nephrocystin genes cause nephronophthisis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PKD1 accounts for about 85 percent of ADPKD and PKD2 for the rest; First Aid places PKD1 on chromosome 16. COMLEX: PKD2 disease is less severe, with later renal failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — progressive cystic destruction of nephrons leads to chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"PKHD1 is the gene of the autos","D":"PKHD1 is the gene of the autos","E":"PKHD1 is the gene of the autos"}},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn delivered after a pregnancy complicated by oligohydramnios has respiratory distress from pulmonary hypoplasia and bilateral, smooth, massively enlarged kidneys. Neither parent has kidney disease. Mutations in which gene most likely cause this disorder?","choices":{"A":"PKD1","B":"PKD2","C":"PKHD1","D":"NPHP1","E":"COL4A5"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most cases of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease are caused by PKHD1, which encodes fibrocystin. Like the polycystins, fibrocystin localizes to the primary cilium of tubular cells. Most patients are compound heterozygotes. PKD1 and PKD2 cause the autosomal dominant adult form with affected parents, NPHP1 causes nephronophthisis with small kidneys, and COL4A5 causes Alport syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARPKD is PKHD1 and fibrocystin, and it is associated with congenital hepatic fibrosis. COMLEX: severe cases present at birth, and First Aid links in utero oliguric renal failure to Potter sequence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — progressive cystic destruction of nephrons leads to chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, about what percentage of patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease have hepatic cysts?","choices":{"A":"10 percent","B":"40 percent","C":"25 percent","D":"65 percent","E":"90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 40 percent of patients with ADPKD have one to several cysts in the liver, called polycystic liver disease. The cysts are usually asymptomatic and derive from biliary epithelium. Cysts occur much less frequently in the spleen, pancreas, and lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD extrarenal manifestations include liver cysts, berry aneurysms, and mitral valve prolapse. COMLEX: the diagnosis is made by radiologic imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — progressive cystic destruction of nephrons leads to chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":1,"stem":"What is the usual renal prognosis of medullary sponge kidney?","choices":{"A":"Progression to end-stage disease within 5 to 10 years","B":"Renal failure beginning between 40 and 60 years of age","C":"Death in infancy or childhood from renal failure","D":"Renal failure if bilateral but surgically curable if unilateral","E":"Usually normal renal function with a benign course"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medullary sponge kidney is a relatively common and usually innocuous structural change of multiple cystic dilations of the medullary collecting ducts, usually found radiographically in adults. Renal function is usually normal, and Robbins lists the outcome as benign. Progression to end-stage disease in 5 to 10 years describes nephronophthisis, renal failure at 40 to 60 years describes adult polycystic disease, death in infancy or childhood describes the childhood polycystic form, and surgical cure of unilateral disease describes multicystic dysplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: medullary sponge kidney is a benign cause of hematuria, urinary tract infection, and recurrent stones. COMLEX: it is discovered on excretory urography and needs no specific renal therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 3 — how medullary cystic disorders and sporadic multicystic dysplasia differ in presentation and pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":1,"stem":"With which manifestations do children with nephronophthisis present first?","choices":{"A":"Gross hematuria with recurrent flank pain","B":"Edema with heavy proteinuria and hyperlipidemia","C":"Oliguria with severe hypertension and hematuria","D":"Polyuria and polydipsia with sodium wasting","E":"Hematuria with hearing loss and lenticonus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Children with nephronophthisis present first with polyuria and polydipsia, reflecting a marked defect in the concentrating ability of renal tubules. Sodium wasting and tubular acidosis are also prominent, and the expected course is progression to end-stage kidney disease in 5 to 10 years. Gross hematuria with flank pain fits adult polycystic disease, edema with heavy proteinuria is nephrotic syndrome, oliguria with hypertension and hematuria is nephritic syndrome, and hematuria with hearing loss and lenticonus is Alport syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephronophthisis is autosomal recessive with corticomedullary cysts and shrunken kidneys, and it is the most common genetic cause of end-stage kidney disease in children and young adults. COMLEX: consider it in unexplained chronic renal failure in a child with a positive family history.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 3 — how medullary cystic disorders and sporadic multicystic dysplasia differ in presentation and pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic feature characterizes multicystic renal dysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Islands of undifferentiated mesenchyme with cartilage","B":"Basketweave lamination of the glomerular basement membrane","C":"Thickened tubular basement membranes with interstitial fibrosis","D":"Cysts lined by hyperplastic epithelium with oxalate crystals","E":"Radially arranged cylindrical dilations of collecting tubules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Although normal nephrons are present, the characteristic histologic feature of multicystic renal dysplasia is islands of undifferentiated mesenchyme, often with cartilage, and immature collecting ducts. Thickened tubular basement membranes with interstitial fibrosis describe nephronophthisis, oxalate-containing cysts describe acquired cystic disease, and radial collecting duct dilations describe the childhood polycystic form.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cartilage in a cystic kidney means dysplasia, which is sporadic and has no inheritance pattern. COMLEX: most cases are associated with ureteropelvic obstruction, ureteral agenesis or atresia, and other lower urinary tract anomalies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 3 — how medullary cystic disorders and sporadic multicystic dysplasia differ in presentation and pathology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Thickened tubular basement mem"}},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has hypertension, episodic gross hematuria, and palpable bilateral flank masses. His mother reached kidney failure in her fifties. CT shows both kidneys enlarged and replaced by innumerable cysts. Which cellular abnormality best explains enlargement of these cysts?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition in tubular basement membranes with dilation","B":"Persistent embryonic mesenchyme that fails to form functional nephrons","C":"Lost flow-induced calcium signaling that drives cell proliferation","D":"Motile ciliary overactivity that increases luminal fluid secretion","E":"Ischemic tubular necrosis with regeneration of dilated tubular segments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. Mutation of PKD1 or PKD2 leads to loss of the polycystin complex, which regulates intracellular calcium in response to flow sensed at the primary cilium. The decrease in calcium is thought to stimulate proliferation and secretion by cyst-lining epithelial cells, causing progressive cyst formation and enlargement. Tubular cilia are nonmotile, and the other options describe unrelated processes such as immune injury, dysplasia, and ischemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD is a ciliopathy in which altered calcium signaling drives proliferation and secretion by cyst epithelium. COMLEX: hypertension and hematuria are frequent early features, and progression is faster in males, in people of African descent, and with hypertension.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — cystic expansion destroys nephrons and feeds the chronic kidney disease common pathway","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tubular cilia are nonmotile, a","B":"Tubular cilia are nonmotile, a","D":"Tubular cilia are nonmotile, a","E":"Tubular cilia are nonmotile, a"}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has polyuria, polydipsia, pallor, and growth retardation, and his sister has similar disease. Ultrasonography shows small kidneys, and biopsy shows tubular basement membrane thickening with interstitial fibrosis. Genetic testing identifies a mutation in NPHP1. Where does the encoded protein normally localize?","choices":{"A":"Podocyte slit diaphragm","B":"Glomerular basement membrane","C":"Proximal tubule brush border","D":"Cilium and basal body","E":"Mitochondrial inner membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is familial juvenile nephronophthisis, the most common variant of the nephronophthisis complex. Mutations in the NPHP genes, which encode nephrocystins, affect proteins found in primary cilia, in the basal bodies attached to them, or in the centrosome from which basal bodies originate, so the disease is a ciliopathy. Nephrocystins are ciliary and centrosomal proteins, not components of the slit diaphragm, GBM, brush border or mitochondria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrocystins localize to the cilium-basal body-centrosome axis, which unites nephronophthisis with the polycystic diseases. COMLEX: the tubular concentrating defect causes polyuria and polydipsia, with salt wasting and tubular acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — nephrocystin proteins at the cilium and basal body in ciliopathy cyst pathogenesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Nephrocystins are ciliary and "}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has polyuria, polydipsia, anemia, and enlarged cystic kidneys with a rising serum creatinine. Genetic testing identifies a mutation in NPHP2, which encodes inversin. During embryogenesis this protein normally mediates which process?","choices":{"A":"Left-right axis patterning","B":"Ureteric bud branching","C":"Neural crest migration","D":"Cloacal septation","E":"Kidney ascent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The NPHP2 gene product is inversin, also called nephrocystin-2, which mediates left-right patterning during embryogenesis. Like the other nephrocystins, it is part of the cilium-centrosome system, which illustrates how ciliary defects can produce extrarenal and developmental phenotypes. Ureteric bud branching, neural crest migration, cloacal septation and kidney ascent are not the described role of inversin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inversin links a ciliopathy to left-right patterning during embryogenesis. COMLEX: nephronophthisis includes a renal-retinal dysplasia variant in which kidney disease is accompanied by ocular lesions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — ciliary proteins such as inversin and their developmental roles in ciliopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has bilateral flank pain, hypertension, and enlarged kidneys with many cysts on ultrasonography. His father had the same disease. Pathology shows that cysts involve only a minority of nephrons even though every tubular cell carries the same inherited gene defect. Which best explains why only some tubules form cysts?","choices":{"A":"Dominant negative action of the mutant protein across the epithelium","B":"Somatic mutation that disables the second allele in an epithelial cell","C":"Mitochondrial heteroplasmy that varies between individual nephrons","D":"Random X inactivation that silences the normal allele in some cells","E":"Postnatal obstruction of individual tubules by interstitial fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Although susceptibility to ADPKD is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait, both alleles of the involved gene must be nonfunctional for cyst development. Each cyst is clonal: its cells carry one inherited mutation plus an acquired somatic mutation that inactivates the second allele. Because cysts initially involve only a minority of nephrons, renal function is retained until about the fourth or fifth decade. Interstitial fibrosis blocking tubules would not explain why each cyst is clonal, with its own second mutation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD follows a two-hit model, a germline hit plus a somatic hit in individual tubular cells. COMLEX: the disease is always bilateral, and apparent unilateral cases are probably multicystic dysplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — the two-hit model of inherited and somatic mutation in ADPKD cyst formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with bilateral enlarged kidneys and a family history of kidney failure develops a sudden severe headache with neck stiffness. CT shows blood in the subarachnoid space. Which vascular lesion is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysm of penetrating arteries","B":"Cerebral arteriovenous malformation","C":"Berry aneurysm of the circle of Willis","D":"Mycotic aneurysm of a cerebral artery","E":"Dural venous sinus thrombosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease have intracranial berry aneurysms in the circle of Willis, presumably from altered polycystin expression in vascular smooth muscle. Subarachnoid hemorrhage from these aneurysms accounts for death in 4 to 10 percent of affected individuals. Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms cause hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage rather than subarachnoid blood, arteriovenous malformations are not the lesion linked to this disease, and dural sinus thrombosis is a venous event.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD is associated with berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, and hepatic cysts. COMLEX: subarachnoid hemorrhage presents as a sudden worst headache of life with meningeal signs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — extrarenal manifestations of ADPKD, including intracranial aneurysms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy has had smooth, enlarged kidneys since birth, and kidney biopsy showed radially arranged, cylindrical dilations of collecting tubules. He now has splenomegaly. Which hepatic lesion most likely explains the splenomegaly?","choices":{"A":"Macronodular cirrhosis from chronic viral hepatitis infection","B":"Hepatic vein thrombosis with centrilobular sinusoidal congestion","C":"Extensive steatosis with hepatocyte ballooning and inflammation","D":"Granulomatous inflammation surrounding the portal tracts","E":"Periportal fibrosis with biliary ductule proliferation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is autosomal recessive childhood polycystic kidney disease, in which the kidneys are enlarged and smooth and all collecting tubules show cylindrical dilation. Patients who survive infancy may develop congenital hepatic fibrosis, a bland periportal fibrosis with proliferation of well-differentiated biliary ductules. In older children the hepatic disease is the predominant concern, and portal hypertension with splenomegaly may develop. Steatosis with ballooning and inflammation is fatty liver disease, not the bland periportal fibrosis of this disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARPKD is PKHD1 with congenital hepatic fibrosis and portal hypertension. COMLEX: First Aid notes that significant oliguric renal failure in utero can lead to Potter sequence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — oligohydramnios and Potter sequence when fetal urine output fails","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has polyuria, polydipsia, pallor, and growth retardation. Ultrasonography shows small, contracted kidneys, and biopsy shows small cysts at the corticomedullary junction surrounded by chronic inflammatory cells. Which process is most responsible for his eventual renal insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Compression of tissue by expanding medullary cysts","B":"Immune complex deposits in glomerular capillaries","C":"Ischemic necrosis of the renal papillae","D":"Cortical tubular atrophy with interstitial fibrosis","E":"Obstruction at the ureteropelvic junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is nephronophthisis. Although the medullary cysts are important, Robbins states that the cortical tubulointerstitial damage, with widespread tubular atrophy, thickened tubular basement membranes, and interstitial fibrosis, is the cause of the eventual renal insufficiency. Glomerular structure is generally preserved. Ureteropelvic obstruction is not part of nephronophthisis, whose kidneys are small rather than hydronephrotic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis on biopsy in a child with unexplained renal failure and a family history suggests nephronophthisis. COMLEX: the expected course is end-stage kidney disease within 5 to 10 years.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — chronic tubulointerstitial scarring and tubular atrophy in the kidney","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Although the medullary cysts a","B":"Glomerular structure is genera"}},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has had two episodes of kidney stones and a urinary tract infection, and her creatinine is normal. Excretory urography shows cystic dilation of the papillary ducts. Histologic examination of an involved papilla would most likely show cysts lined by which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Keratinizing squamous or ciliated columnar epithelium","B":"Cuboidal or occasionally transitional epithelium","C":"Mucin-secreting columnar or goblet cell epithelium","D":"Pseudostratified columnar or simple squamous epithelium","E":"Atypical hobnail or clear cell epithelium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is medullary sponge kidney, which Robbins restricts to multiple cystic dilations of the collecting ducts in the medulla, usually discovered radiographically in adults with normal renal function. On gross inspection the papillary ducts in the medulla are dilated and small cysts may be present, and the cysts are lined by cuboidal epithelium or occasionally by transitional epithelium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recurrent stones plus medullary cysts on urography in an adult with normal renal function points to medullary sponge kidney. COMLEX: cortical scarring is absent unless pyelonephritis is superimposed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — medullary sponge kidney as a benign medullary cystic disorder, distinct from nephronophthisis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-week-old girl has a palpable left flank mass. A left nephrectomy specimen shows an enlarged, irregular kidney with cysts of variable size lined by flattened epithelium, with islands of cartilage and undifferentiated mesenchyme. The right kidney functions normally. Which additional anomaly is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","B":"Congenital hepatic fibrosis","C":"Intracranial berry aneurysm","D":"Mitral valve prolapse","E":"Renal-retinal dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is multicystic renal dysplasia, a sporadic disorder that can be unilateral or bilateral. Most cases are associated with ureteropelvic obstruction, ureteral agenesis or atresia, and other anomalies of the lower urinary tract. Unilateral disease has an excellent prognosis after nephrectomy because the opposite kidney functions normally. Hepatic fibrosis, berry aneurysms, and valve prolapse belong to the polycystic diseases, and renal-retinal dysplasia is a variant of nephronophthisis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multicystic dysplasia is a sporadic developmental disorder with cartilage and immature ducts on histology. COMLEX: unilateral dysplasia may mimic a neoplasm and lead to surgical exploration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — reciprocal induction between the ureteric bud and metanephric mesenchyme","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl37_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old man with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease enrolls in a study of his tubular cells. After fluid flow is applied, the cells show no rise in cytosolic calcium. Which downstream change most directly drives fluid accumulation inside his cysts?","choices":{"A":"Low cAMP driving ENaC sodium absorption","B":"High cGMP driving ROMK potassium secretion","C":"High cAMP driving CFTR chloride secretion","D":"High calcium driving aquaporin-2 insertion","E":"Low IP3 driving NKCC1 chloride uptake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Flow normally bends the primary cilium and opens polycystin channels, letting calcium into the cell. Without that calcium signal, adenylyl cyclase loses its calcium-mediated inhibition, so cAMP accumulates. High cAMP drives MAPK/ERK proliferation and activates apical CFTR chloride channels, so fluid is secreted into the expanding cyst. High cGMP with ROMK secretion is not part of the cyst pathway. Low cAMP with ENaC absorption runs opposite to the lecture model, in which cAMP rises. High calcium with aquaporin-2 insertion contradicts the absent calcium signal shown here. Low IP3 with NKCC1 uptake describes no step of the ciliary pathway. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 37, ciliary calcium signaling and cAMP over-activation); Robbins Ch. 20 (cystic diseases, primary cilium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of flow-induced calcium entry raises cAMP, which drives both cyst-wall proliferation and CFTR-mediated fluid secretion. COMLEX: this cAMP pathway explains why cyst growth enlarges the kidneys over decades.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 2 — the same polycystin proteins are encoded by PKD1 and PKD2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl37_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has bilateral renal cysts, and her father reached end-stage kidney disease at 74. Genetic testing shows a mutation in the less common gene for her disease. Which function does the encoded protein normally serve?","choices":{"A":"Cell-matrix adhesion receptor","B":"Ligand-gated chloride channel","C":"Calcium-permeable cation channel","D":"Apical water channel protein","E":"Basolateral sodium-potassium pump unit"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The late-onset, milder course points to a PKD2 mutation, which accounts for about 15% of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. PKD2 encodes polycystin-2, a calcium-permeable cation channel that opens when flow bends the primary cilium. A cell-matrix adhesion receptor describes polycystin-1, the PKD1 product found in about 85% of cases with faster progression. A ligand-gated chloride channel is not a polycystin; CFTR, a chloride channel, acts downstream of cAMP. An apical water channel describes aquaporin-2 in collecting duct principal cells. A basolateral sodium pump subunit belongs to Na+/K+-ATPase. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 37, PKD1 vs PKD2); Robbins Ch. 20 (polycystin-2 as a calcium-permeable channel).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PKD1 (polycystin-1, about 85%) progresses faster, and PKD2 (polycystin-2, a calcium channel, about 15%) is milder and later. COMLEX: a family history of late end-stage disease fits PKD2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 6 — ADPKD vs ARPKD case classification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl37_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease has a CT scan for flank pain. It shows enlarged cystic kidneys and multiple liver cysts. His aminotransferases and bilirubin are normal. From which cell type do these liver cysts arise?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic sinusoidal endothelium","B":"Hepatocyte cytoplasmic vacuoles","C":"Kupffer cell aggregates","D":"Biliary ductal epithelium","E":"Portal venous radicles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Polycystic liver disease affects about 40% of patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, and the cysts derive from biliary epithelium. They are usually asymptomatic, so normal liver tests fit. Hepatic sinusoidal endothelium lines vascular channels, not these cysts. Hepatocyte cytoplasmic vacuoles reflect fatty or hydropic change within cells, not cysts. Kupffer cell aggregates are macrophages and form no epithelial cysts. Portal venous radicles are vessels and give rise to no cyst lining. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 37, extrarenal manifestations of ADPKD); Robbins Ch. 20 (liver cysts of biliary origin in ADPKD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD extrarenal findings are liver cysts (about 40%), berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, and colonic diverticula. COMLEX: asymptomatic liver cysts with normal liver tests need no treatment on their own.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 6 — ADPKD and ARPKD are separated by age, gene, and liver findings","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl37_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn has bilaterally enlarged kidneys with smooth outer surfaces, and his mother had oligohydramnios. He dies of respiratory failure on day 2. Autopsy shows congenital hepatic fibrosis. Which renal cut-surface finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Radial dilation of the collecting ducts","B":"Corticomedullary cysts in shrunken kidneys","C":"Cartilage islands in disorganized stroma","D":"Scattered cysts along many nephron segments","E":"Medullary pyramid cysts with normal cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neonatal onset, smooth enlarged kidneys, Potter sequence, and congenital hepatic fibrosis indicate autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease. On cut section, the collecting ducts are uniformly dilated into elongated channels arranged at right angles to the cortical surface. Scattered cysts along many nephron segments describe autosomal dominant disease in adults. Corticomedullary cysts in shrunken kidneys describe nephronophthisis. Cartilage islands in disorganized stroma describe multicystic renal dysplasia. Medullary pyramid cysts with a normal cortex describe medullary sponge kidney. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 37, ARPKD morphology and Potter sequence); Robbins Ch. 20 (ARPKD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARPKD is PKHD1 (fibrocystin), with radially dilated collecting ducts, congenital hepatic fibrosis, and Potter sequence. COMLEX: pulmonary hypoplasia from oligohydramnios is the main cause of early neonatal death.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 1 — fetal urine output maintains the amniotic fluid needed for lung development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl37_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has polyuria, polydipsia, and night blindness. His creatinine is 3.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.3–0.7 mg/dL), and ultrasound shows small kidneys with cysts at the corticomedullary junction. Funduscopy shows retinitis pigmentosa. Which protein family is most likely mutated?","choices":{"A":"Polycystin-1 of the cell membrane","B":"Fibrocystin of collecting ducts","C":"Type IV collagen alpha-5 chains","D":"Podocin of the slit diaphragm","E":"Nephrocystins in primary cilia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Childhood polyuria with a concentrating defect, small kidneys with corticomedullary cysts, and retinitis pigmentosa indicate nephronophthisis presenting as Senior-Løken syndrome. Nephronophthisis is caused by NPHP gene mutations encoding nephrocystins, which localize to primary cilia, basal bodies, or centrosomes. Polycystin-1 mutations cause autosomal dominant disease with large kidneys in adults. Fibrocystin mutations cause autosomal recessive disease with enlarged infant kidneys. Type IV collagen alpha-5 mutations cause Alport syndrome with hearing loss, not retinal dystrophy. Podocin mutations cause steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 37, nephronophthisis and Senior-Løken syndrome); Robbins Ch. 20 (nephrocystins).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephronophthisis is the most common genetic cause of end-stage disease in children, with corticomedullary cysts and small kidneys. COMLEX: retinitis pigmentosa plus childhood renal failure suggests Senior-Løken syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 1 — nephronophthisis is a ciliopathy, like the polycystic diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl37_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has her third calcium stone in 2 years. Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL), and creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Urography shows cystic dilation of collecting ducts confined to the medullary pyramids. Which mechanism best explains her recurrent stones?","choices":{"A":"Hyperuricosuria from purine overload","B":"Hypercalcemia from a parathyroid adenoma","C":"Urease-splitting bacteria alkalinizing urine","D":"Defective proximal cystine reabsorption","E":"Urinary stasis promoting crystal growth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cystic dilation of medullary collecting ducts with normal renal function describes medullary sponge kidney, a benign, non-hereditary anomaly. Urinary stasis within the dilated ducts promotes crystal growth and predisposes to recurrent calcium stones, urinary tract infection, and hematuria. Hypercalcemia from a parathyroid adenoma is excluded by her normal serum calcium. Urease-splitting bacteria form struvite, not calcium, stones. Defective cystine reabsorption forms cystine stones. Hyperuricosuria is a recognized calcium-stone risk, but the imaging shows medullary sponge kidney, where stasis in dilated ducts is the mechanism. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 37, medullary sponge kidney); Robbins Ch. 20 (medullary sponge kidney).","boardPrep":"Step 1: medullary sponge kidney means dilated medullary collecting ducts, normal GFR, and recurrent calcium stones. COMLEX: preserved function separates it from nephronophthisis, which progresses to end-stage disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — calcium stones account for about 70% of renal calculi","isHighYield":false}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, approximately what percentage of renal calculi are calcium oxalate or calcium oxalate mixed with calcium phosphate?","choices":{"A":"About 55 percent","B":"About 70 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 25 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins lists four main types of calculi, and calcium stones, composed largely of calcium oxalate or calcium oxalate mixed with calcium phosphate, make up about 70 percent. Struvite stones are 5 to 10 percent, uric acid stones 5 to 10 percent, and cystine stones 1 to 2 percent; First Aid 2025 gives calcium stones as 80 percent, struvite as 15 percent, and uric acid as about 5 percent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium stones are the most common kidney stone, and calcium stones are radiopaque. COMLEX: most stones arise in the kidney, and a low urine volume favors supersaturation, so fluids are encouraged.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — renal stone types, urinary pH, and the metabolic or infectious triggers behind them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"A tendency to excrete urine of which pH predisposes to uric acid stones in patients who have neither hyperuricemia nor increased urinary uric acid?","choices":{"A":"A pH above 7.5","B":"A pH near 6.5","C":"A pH near 7.0","D":"A pH below 5.5","E":"A pH above 8.0"},"correct":"D","explanation":"More than half of patients with uric acid calculi have neither hyperuricemia nor increased urinary uric acid excretion. In this group a tendency to excrete urine with a pH below 5.5 is thought to predispose to stones, because uric acid is insoluble in acidic urine. In contrast to radiopaque calcium stones, uric acid stones are radiolucent. Values of 6.5 and above are less acidic, so uric acid stays more soluble.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uric acid stones are radiolucent and form in acidic urine, and First Aid treats them with urine alkalinization and allopurinol. COMLEX: uric acid stones are also common in gout and in diseases with rapid cell turnover such as leukemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — renal stone types, urinary pH, and the metabolic or infectious triggers behind them","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Values of 6.5 and above are le","C":"Values of 6.5 and above are le","E":"Values of 6.5 and above are le"}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy has had three kidney stones since age 9, and his older sister also forms stones. Urine microscopy shows flat hexagonal crystals, and a urinary cyanide-nitroprusside test is positive. Which underlying defect best explains his stones?","choices":{"A":"Defective tubular reabsorption of amino acids","B":"Excess intestinal absorption of dietary oxalate","C":"Bacterial conversion of urea to ammonia","D":"Overproduction of uric acid from purine turnover","E":"Reduced urinary excretion of citrate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cystine stones are caused by genetic defects in the renal reabsorption of amino acids, including cystine, which causes cystinuria. These stones form at low urinary pH and account for 1 to 2 percent of stones. Enteric oxalate absorption, urea-splitting infection, uric acid overproduction, and hypocitraturia are associated with other stone types.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria is a hereditary transporter defect in which cystine is poorly soluble, and First Aid notes it can form staghorn calculi, although Robbins states that staghorn calculi are frequently a consequence of infection. COMLEX: cystine stones begin in childhood and run in families.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — renal stone types, urinary pH, and the metabolic or infectious triggers behind them","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Enteric oxalate absorption, ur","E":"Enteric oxalate absorption, ur"}},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":1,"stem":"Why does glomerular filtration persist for some time even with complete obstruction of urine outflow?","choices":{"A":"Tubular water secretion replaces filtration in the blocked kidney","B":"Pelvic pressure remains below Bowman space pressure during obstruction","C":"Loops of Henle absorb the filtrate before it can accumulate","D":"Filtrate diffuses back into the renal interstitium and perirenal spaces","E":"Efferent constriction sustains filtration pressure indefinitely"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Even with complete obstruction, glomerular filtration persists for some time because the filtrate diffuses back into the renal interstitium and perirenal spaces, from where it returns to the lymphatic and venous systems. Because filtration continues, the calyces and pelvis become dilated, often markedly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydronephrosis is dilation of the pelvis and calyces with progressive atrophy due to outflow obstruction. COMLEX: relief of early obstruction leads to reversion toward normal function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — how outflow obstruction progresses from pelvic pressure to parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":1,"stem":"What is the earliest functional alteration caused by urinary tract obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Falling glomerular filtration rate","B":"Heavy proteinuria with edema","C":"Impaired concentrating ability","D":"Renal salt retention with edema","E":"Hematuria with red cell casts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The high pelvic pressure is transmitted back through the collecting ducts into the cortex, and it also compresses the medullary renal vasculature, decreasing inner medullary blood flow. The initial functional alterations are therefore largely tubular, manifested primarily by impaired concentrating ability. Only later does the GFR begin to fall. Heavy proteinuria with edema and hematuria with red cell casts are glomerular syndromes, not early effects of obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in bilateral partial obstruction the earliest manifestation is polyuria and nocturia from the concentrating defect. COMLEX: some patients also develop distal tubular acidosis and renal salt wasting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — how outflow obstruction progresses from pelvic pressure to parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Only later does the GFR begin "}},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":1,"stem":"What is the gross appearance of an advanced, long-standing hydronephrotic kidney?","choices":{"A":"Thin-walled cystic sac with obliterated pyramids","B":"Small kidney with coarse polar scars","C":"Shrunken kidney with fine granular cortex","D":"Enlarged kidney replaced by innumerable parenchymal cysts","E":"Pale wedge-shaped scar with a hyperemic border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In advanced hydronephrosis the kidney may be transformed into a thin-walled cystic structure up to 15 to 20 cm in diameter, with striking parenchymal atrophy, total obliteration of the pyramids, and thinning of the cortex. Earlier there is progressive blunting and then cupping of the pyramid apices, with cortical tubular atrophy and diffuse interstitial fibrosis. A pale wedge-shaped scar with a hyperemic border is a renal infarct, not hydronephrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pelvicalyceal dilation with cortical thinning indicates chronic obstruction. COMLEX: the kidney may be slightly to massively enlarged depending on the degree and duration of obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — how outflow obstruction progresses from pelvic pressure to parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":1,"stem":"Renal colic is typically produced by which event?","choices":{"A":"Prostatic enlargement obstructing outflow","B":"A calculus lodged in the ureter","C":"Gradual dilation of the renal pelvis","D":"Formation of a simple cortical cyst","E":"Progressive cortical atrophy of the kidney"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Calculi lodged in the ureters give rise to renal colic, which Robbins describes as spasms of severe pain, often with hematuria. Prostatic enlargement typically gives bladder symptoms, and slowly progressive dilation, cysts, and atrophy are typically silent or minimally painful.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an obstructing stone causes colicky flank pain with hematuria, and First Aid describes pain radiating to the groin. COMLEX: renal colic is a classic presentation of urolithiasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 3 — clinical presentations of obstruction: pain quality, hematuria, and silent disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Prostatic enlargement typicall"}},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":1,"stem":"Most of the early symptoms of hydronephrosis are produced by which factor?","choices":{"A":"Uremia from a falling glomerular filtration rate","B":"Cortical scarring of the kidney","C":"The underlying cause of the obstruction","D":"The interstitial inflammatory reaction","E":"Hyperkalemia from tubular dysfunction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acute obstruction may provoke pain attributed to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule, but most of the early symptoms are produced by the underlying cause. Calculi lodged in the ureters give rise to renal colic, and prostatic enlargement gives rise to bladder symptoms. Uremia, hyperkalemia, cortical scarring and interstitial inflammation come with later progressive damage, after the GFR begins to fall, rather than producing the early symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the presenting symptoms of obstruction point to its cause, such as stones or prostatic enlargement. COMLEX: ultrasonography is a useful noninvasive technique for diagnosing obstructive uropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 3 — clinical presentations of obstruction: pain quality, hematuria, and silent disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":1,"stem":"Why can unilateral complete or partial hydronephrosis remain silent for long periods?","choices":{"A":"The obstructed kidney raises its filtration rate","B":"Pain fibers are absent from the renal capsule","C":"Filtration ceases so no distention can occur","D":"Collateral channels drain urine around the block","E":"The unaffected kidney maintains adequate function"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Unilateral complete or partial hydronephrosis may remain silent for long periods because the unaffected kidney can maintain adequate renal function, and it is sometimes first recognized on imaging. Filtration continues in the obstructed kidney, which is why the pelvis and calyces dilate, and Robbins attributes acute obstructive pain to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule. No collateral channel carries urine past the block; filtrate that backs up returns through the interstitium to lymphatics and veins while the pelvis dilates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: unilateral obstruction may not raise creatinine, whereas bilateral obstruction does. COMLEX: in the first few weeks, relief of obstruction leads to reversion to normal function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 3 — clinical presentations of obstruction: pain quality, hematuria, and silent disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections has flank pain and fever. CT shows a branching stone occupying the renal pelvis and calyces, and urine culture grows Proteus mirabilis. Which mechanism best explains formation of this stone?","choices":{"A":"Insolubility of uric acid in persistently acidic urine","B":"Bacterial urea splitting with alkaline urine","C":"Excess absorption of dietary calcium from the intestine","D":"Inherited defect of renal tubular amino acid reabsorption","E":"Hyperoxaluria from intestinal oxalate overabsorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Magnesium ammonium phosphate, or struvite, stones form largely after infection by urea-splitting bacteria such as Proteus and some staphylococci. These convert urea to ammonia, and the resulting alkaline urine precipitates magnesium ammonium phosphate. They form some of the largest stones, and staghorn calculi occupying large portions of the renal pelvis are frequently a consequence of infection. Intestinal overabsorption of calcium or oxalate produces calcium oxalate stones, which are not tied to urease-producing infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urease-positive bacteria such as Proteus produce ammonia and alkaline urine, and First Aid treats struvite by eradicating infection and surgically removing the stone. COMLEX: stones both cause obstruction and predispose to superimposed infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — urinary stasis converts infection into ascending pyelonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with gout has colicky right flank pain and hematuria. A plain abdominal radiograph shows no radiopaque stone, and his urine pH is 5.2. Which best explains formation of his stone?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial urease splitting urea to ammonia","B":"Excess intestinal absorption of calcium","C":"Genetic defect in amino acid reabsorption","D":"Low solubility of uric acid in acidic fluid","E":"Enteric hyperabsorption of dietary oxalate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uric acid stones are common in patients with hyperuricemia such as gout, and uric acid is insoluble in acidic urine. In contrast to radiopaque calcium stones, uric acid stones are radiolucent, which explains the negative plain film. Urease-producing infection, calcium hyperabsorption, cystinuria, and oxalate hyperabsorption produce other stone types.","boardPrep":"Step 1: radiolucent stone plus gout or acidic urine points to uric acid, and First Aid treats it with urine alkalinization and allopurinol. COMLEX: uric acid stones also occur in diseases with rapid cell turnover such as leukemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — urine alkalinization and pH trapping of weak acids","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Urease-producing infection, ca","B":"Urease-producing infection, ca","C":"Urease-producing infection, ca"}},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with gout has recurrent radiopaque stones, and a 24-hour urine collection shows hyperuricosuria. Which mechanism best explains his stone formation?","choices":{"A":"Urea-splitting infection that alkalinizes the urine","B":"Impaired tubular reabsorption of amino acids","C":"Nucleation of calcium oxalate by uric acid crystals","D":"Excess urinary pyrophosphate that inhibits crystal growth","E":"Intestinal overabsorption of dietary oxalate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Up to 20 percent of calcium oxalate stones are associated with increased uric acid secretion, called hyperuricosuric calcium nephrolithiasis, with or without hypercalciuria. The mechanism involves nucleation of calcium oxalate by uric acid crystals in the collecting ducts. Pyrophosphate is one of several inhibitors of crystal formation in urine, so an excess would not promote stones. Impaired amino acid reabsorption causes cystine stones, which are not linked to gout or hyperuricosuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium oxalate stones are the most common type and are radiopaque. COMLEX: hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia is the most frequent metabolic association, so 24-hour urine chemistry guides prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — bidirectional renal handling of uric acid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with prostatic enlargement has had anuria for 36 hours from complete bilateral outflow obstruction. After urinary catheterization his urine output rises to 400 mL per hour. Which best characterizes the urine he is now excreting?","choices":{"A":"Large volume rich in sodium chloride","B":"Small volume with maximal sodium retention","C":"Large volume of sodium-free dilute urine","D":"Normal volume with nephrotic-range protein","E":"Small volume with red cell casts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Complete bilateral obstruction of rapid onset causes oliguria or anuria and is incompatible with survival unless relieved. Robbins notes that diuresis frequently occurs after relief of complete obstruction, and it can be massive, with the kidney excreting large amounts of urine rich in sodium chloride. Nephrotic-range protein and red cell casts are glomerular findings, not features of the diuresis after relief of obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: postobstructive diuresis can cause volume and electrolyte depletion, so monitor volume status after relief. COMLEX: prostatic enlargement is a common cause of bilateral outflow obstruction in older men.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 3 — the diuretic recovery phase and its hazards","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has had left flank pain for 10 days from a stone in the left ureter, with left hydronephrosis on ultrasonography. The stone is removed. Which renal outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Permanent atrophy despite relief","B":"Progression to end-stage disease","C":"Persistent nephrotic syndrome","D":"Reversion to normal function","E":"Fixed tubular acidosis with salt wasting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the early stages of obstruction, perhaps the first few weeks, relief of the obstruction leads to reversion to normal function. Unrelieved obstruction almost always leads to permanent atrophy, and the medullary vascular defects are initially reversible. Progressive end-stage disease, fixed tubular defects, and nephrotic syndrome are not expected after early relief.","boardPrep":"Step 1: early relief of obstruction preserves renal function, whereas prolonged obstruction produces atrophy and interstitial fibrosis. COMLEX: prompt relief of obstruction is the priority.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — early versus prolonged obstruction and the fate of the hydronephrotic parenchyma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with years of untreated bladder outlet obstruction dies of an unrelated cause. Both kidneys show markedly dilated pelves, obliterated pyramids, and a thinned cortex. Which microscopic finding is expected in the cortex?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis with onion-skin arteriolar thickening","B":"Tubular atrophy with diffuse interstitial fibrosis","C":"Neutrophilic casts with cortical microabscesses","D":"Crescent formation within Bowman space","E":"Mesangial IgA deposition with hypercellularity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In chronic hydronephrosis the picture is one of cortical tubular atrophy with marked diffuse interstitial fibrosis, and obstruction triggers an interstitial inflammatory reaction even in the absence of infection. Onion-skin arteriolar lesions, microabscesses, crescents, and mesangial IgA deposits belong to malignant hypertension, acute pyelonephritis, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, and IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic obstruction ends in tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis with a thin cortex. COMLEX: in bilateral partial obstruction hypertension is common.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — obstruction and reflux causing chronic scarring of the kidney","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Onion-skin arteriolar lesions,","D":"Onion-skin arteriolar lesions,"}},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has had 3 days of gross hematuria and excruciating flank pain that radiates toward the groin, without fever. CT shows a 6-mm calculus in the proximal ureter. Which best describes the pain pattern that distinguishes his symptom from the pain of a slowly dilating hydronephrotic kidney?","choices":{"A":"A dull, constant ache","B":"Pain limited to the bladder region","C":"Spasms of severe colicky pain","D":"No pain until renal failure develops","E":"Burning pain during voiding"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Urolithiasis is manifested by spasms of severe pain, called renal colic, and hematuria, and calculi lodged in the ureters give rise to colic. In contrast, acute obstruction may provoke pain attributed to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule, unilateral hydronephrosis may remain silent for long periods, and prostatic enlargement gives rise to bladder symptoms. A dull, steady ache or silence fits slow distention of the collecting system, not the spasms of a stone lodged in the ureter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute colicky flank pain with hematuria points to a ureteral stone. COMLEX: colic is intermittent and severe, in contrast to the dull constant ache of capsular distention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — colicky ureteral pain contrasted with the dull pain of hydronephrotic capsular distention","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"In contrast, acute obstruction","D":"A dull, steady ache or silence"}},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with prostatic enlargement develops acute urinary retention. Two days later he has a dull, constant bilateral flank ache. He is afebrile, and ultrasonography shows bilateral pelvicalyceal dilation with no stones or cysts. Which best explains the flank ache?","choices":{"A":"Acute bacterial invasion of the renal pelvis and ureter","B":"Hemorrhage into a simple cyst of the renal cortex","C":"Immune complex inflammation of glomerular capillaries","D":"Ischemic infarction of a segment of the renal cortex","E":"Stretch of the capsule and collecting system"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute obstruction may provoke pain attributed to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule, so the dull constant flank ache after acute retention reflects stretch of these structures. Infection, cyst hemorrhage, glomerular inflammation, and cortical infarction are not supported by an afebrile patient with pelvicalyceal dilation and no cysts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive pain reflects stretch of the collecting system and capsule, and colicky pain suggests a stone lodged in the ureter. COMLEX: ultrasonography is a useful noninvasive technique to confirm obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — prostatic enlargement and urethral compression in benign hyperplasia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Infection, cyst hemorrhage, gl"}},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with known cervical carcinoma has flank fullness, and her creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 2.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Which is the most appropriate initial test to evaluate for urinary tract obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Renal ultrasonography","B":"Percutaneous kidney biopsy","C":"Renal arteriography","D":"Retrograde pyelography","E":"Voiding cystourethrography"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ultrasonography is a useful noninvasive technique in the diagnosis of obstructive uropathy. Robbins lists carcinoma of the cervix or uterus among tumors that can obstruct the ureters. Biopsy, arteriography, retrograde pyelography, and catheter-based cystography are invasive procedures and are not the first test for suspected obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstruction is a postrenal cause of azotemia, and imaging identifies dilated pelvicalyceal systems. COMLEX: identify and relieve obstruction early, because early relief allows return of function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — ureteral obstruction and retroperitoneal compression","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Biopsy, arteriography, retrogr"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl38_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man who had an ileal resection for Crohn disease has colicky left flank pain. He has chronic fat malabsorption. CT shows a radiopaque ureteral stone, and his urine pH is 6.0. Which crystal is most likely seen on urinalysis?","choices":{"A":"Hexagonal cystine crystals","B":"Coffin-lid struvite crystals","C":"Rhomboid uric acid crystals","D":"Envelope calcium oxalate crystals","E":"Wedge-shaped calcium phosphate crystals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fat malabsorption after ileal disease frees dietary oxalate for colonic absorption (enteric hyperoxaluria). Calcium oxalate stones, promoted by hyperoxaluria and hypocitraturia, are radiopaque and appear as envelope or bipyramidal dihydrate crystals. Hexagonal cystine crystals come from inherited cystinuria. Rhomboid uric acid crystals form in urine below pH 5.5 and are radiolucent. Coffin-lid struvite crystals require urease-positive infection and an alkaline urine. Wedge-shaped calcium phosphate crystals form in alkaline urine. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 38, calculus chemistry and hyperoxaluria); Robbins Ch. 20 (calcium oxalate stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: envelope-shaped crystals mean calcium oxalate; malabsorption raises colonic oxalate absorption. COMLEX: ask about bowel resection in a patient with recurrent calcium oxalate stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 4 — stones are classified by crystal shape, radiodensity, and urine pH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl38_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with acute leukemia develops colicky right flank pain 3 days after starting chemotherapy. His urine pH is 5.0, and serum uric acid is 12.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.5–7.2 mg/dL). Which imaging pattern is expected for his stone?","choices":{"A":"Faint on plain film, absent on CT","B":"Seen on CT but not on plain film","C":"Ground-glass density on plain film","D":"Branching staghorn on plain film","E":"Dense on both plain film and CT"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tumor lysis with acidic urine points to a uric acid stone. Uric acid stones are radiolucent, so a plain abdominal film misses them, whereas non-contrast CT detects them. Faint on plain film and absent on CT fits no stone, because CT detects all common stones. Ground-glass density on plain film describes faintly radiopaque cystine stones. A branching staghorn on plain film describes struvite stones from urease-positive infection. Dense on both studies describes radiopaque calcium stones. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 38, uric acid stones and imaging); Robbins Ch. 20 (uric acid stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: uric acid stones are radiolucent and form in urine below pH 5.5, classically after tumor lysis or with gout. COMLEX: non-contrast CT is the gold standard for suspected stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 3 — non-contrast helical CT identifies radiopaque and radiolucent stones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl38_q3","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has her second calcium oxalate stone. Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone are normal. A 24-hour urine collection shows normal calcium, normal oxalate, and low citrate excretion. Which mechanism best explains her stone formation?","choices":{"A":"Excess urinary calcium from bone resorption","B":"Bacterial urease alkalinizing the urine","C":"Persistently acidic urine favoring urate","D":"Defective reabsorption of dibasic amino acids","E":"Loss of an inhibitor of crystallization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Citrate is an endogenous inhibitor of crystallization, so hypocitraturia removes a brake on calcium stone formation even when urinary calcium and oxalate are normal. Excess urinary calcium from bone resorption is excluded by her normal urine calcium and parathyroid hormone. Bacterial urease alkalinizes urine and forms struvite stones. Persistently acidic urine favors uric acid, not calcium oxalate, stones. Defective reabsorption of dibasic amino acids causes cystinuria. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 38, hypocitraturia and loss of crystal inhibitors); Robbins Ch. 20 (calcium stones and inhibitors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium stone risk factors are hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria, and hypocitraturia. COMLEX: a 24-hour urine study identifies the metabolic cause in recurrent stone formers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 4 — the metabolic pathways of calcium oxalate stones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl38_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":3,"stem":"Patient A's right ureter was blocked suddenly and completely by a stone 5 days ago; CT shows only mild dilation of his right pelvis and calyces. Patient B has had a congenital ureteropelvic junction narrowing that partially and intermittently obstructs her left kidney for years; CT shows a massively dilated pelvis and calyces with a thin cortex. Which statement best explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Complete obstruction stops filtration at once, so no dilation forms","B":"Subtotal or intermittent obstruction allows progressive dilation","C":"A superimposed bacterial infection dilates patient B's kidney","D":"Patient A's mild dilation reflects reflux rather than obstruction","E":"Stone composition, not the pattern of blockage, sets dilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins describes sudden, complete obstruction as producing mild dilation of the pelvis and calyces and sometimes parenchymal atrophy, whereas subtotal or intermittent obstruction produces progressive dilation, giving rise to hydronephrosis; in advanced cases the kidney can become a thin-walled sac 15–20 cm across. Even with complete obstruction, glomerular filtration persists for some time because the filtrate diffuses back into the interstitium and perirenal spaces, so filtration does not stop at once. Interstitial inflammation occurs in hydronephrosis even without infection, and infection is not needed to explain the dilation. Patient A has a documented obstructing stone, not reflux. Patient B has no stone at all, so stone composition cannot account for her dilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstruction: sudden complete → mild dilation; subtotal or intermittent → progressive hydronephrosis with cortical atrophy, blunted then cupped papillae, and interstitial fibrosis; early functional loss is impaired concentration. COMLEX: Relief within the first few weeks usually restores normal function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — Bowman space hydrostatic pressure in the Starling balance","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Interstitial inflammation occu"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl38_q5","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has a ureteral stricture that went unrecognized for 8 months. Ultrasound shows a markedly dilated renal pelvis and calyces with a thinned cortex. Which change in the renal papillae is expected?","choices":{"A":"Enlarged papillae with mucosal polyps","B":"Sloughed necrotic papillary tips","C":"Flattened and cupped papillae","D":"Papillae replaced by cartilage islands","E":"Calcified papillae with a normal pelvis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sustained back-pressure dilates the pelvis and calyces and progressively blunts, flattens, and eventually cups the renal papillae while the cortex thins. Sloughed necrotic papillary tips describe papillary necrosis from ischemia, as in diabetes, analgesic use, or sickle cell disease. Enlarged papillae with mucosal polyps are not a feature of obstruction. Cartilage islands describe multicystic renal dysplasia. Calcified papillae with a normal pelvis do not fit a dilated collecting system. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 38, papillary flattening in hydronephrosis); Robbins Ch. 20 (hydronephrosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic obstruction causes calyceal blunting and papillary cupping, cortical thinning, and finally a thin-walled cystic kidney. COMLEX: ultrasound grades hydronephrosis by pelvocalyceal dilation and cortical thickness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 1 — strictures and extrinsic fibrosis are lower urinary tract causes of hydronephrosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl38_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has 3 weeks of a dull, constant ache in his right flank with no radiation and no colicky episodes. Ultrasound shows right hydronephrosis from a ureteropelvic junction narrowing. Visceral afferents for this pain travel mainly with which nerve?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar splanchnic nerve","B":"Greater splanchnic nerve","C":"Pelvic splanchnic nerve","D":"Genitofemoral nerve","E":"Lesser splanchnic nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dull, constant, non-radiating flank pain reflects stretch of the renal capsule from hydronephrosis, and the lecture places these capsular afferents at T10 to T11, the level of the lesser splanchnic nerve that supplies the kidney. Lumbar splanchnic nerves carry colic afferents from the lower ureter and bladder, which enter T11 to L2. Pelvic splanchnic nerves are S2 to S4 parasympathetic fibers. The genitofemoral nerve (L1, L2) carries the referred groin pain of ureteral colic, which he lacks. The greater splanchnic nerve (T5 to T9) supplies the upper abdominal viscera. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 38, clinical pain pathways; SDL 47, OPP innervation levels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capsular distension gives dull, fixed flank pain via T10 to T11, and ureteral colic gives loin-to-groin pain via T11 to L2. COMLEX: look for viscerosomatic changes at the matching paraspinal segments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 6 — kidney and upper ureter sympathetic innervation is T10 to T11","isHighYield":false}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":1,"stem":"Loss of one copy of which chromosomal region is found in nearly all clear cell renal cell carcinomas?","choices":{"A":"Chromosome 11p","B":"Chromosome 5q","C":"Chromosome 3p","D":"Chromosome 13q","E":"Chromosome 18q"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In 98% of clear cell renal cell carcinomas, whether sporadic, familial, or associated with VHL syndrome, one copy of chromosome 3 is deleted at band p25.3, the site of the VHL gene. In up to 80% the second copy is disabled by somatic mutation or silenced by hypermethylation, consistent with VHL acting as a tumor suppressor gene. The other regions are not the characteristic deletion of this tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC and clear cell histology point to chromosome 3 and VHL. COMLEX: clear cell carcinoma is the most common RCC subtype, accounting for 70% to 80% of renal cell cancers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 — molecular drivers that separate clear cell from papillary renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":1,"stem":"What is the normal function of the protein encoded by the VHL gene?","choices":{"A":"Phosphorylates HIF to enhance transcription","B":"Targets HIF for ubiquitin-mediated degradation","C":"Binds VEGF to limit its receptor activation","D":"Deacetylates histones near angiogenic genes","E":"Cleaves MYC to reduce metabolic reprogramming"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The VHL protein is part of a ubiquitin ligase complex that targets the transcription factor hypoxia-inducible factor for degradation in an oxygen-dependent fashion. When VHL is inactive, HIF stays high even under normoxic conditions and drives genes such as VEGF and IGF-1. VHL does not act on MYC; its target is HIF.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VHL loss produces pseudohypoxia with stabilized HIF, increased VEGF, and a highly vascular tumor. COMLEX: one-half to two-thirds of patients with VHL syndrome develop renal cysts and bilateral, often multiple, renal cell carcinomas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 — molecular drivers that separate clear cell from papillary renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"VHL does not act on MYC; its t"}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":1,"stem":"Gain-of-function mutation of which proto-oncogene causes familial papillary renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"KIT","B":"RET","C":"VHL","D":"WT1","E":"MET"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The familial form of papillary renal cell carcinoma is caused by gain-of-function mutations in MET, a proto-oncogene on chromosome 7. MET is also mutated in a small proportion of sporadic papillary carcinomas. VHL and WT1 are tumor suppressor genes, and KIT and RET are unrelated receptor tyrosine kinases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary RCC shows MET activation and trisomy 7 with no 3p deletion, whereas clear cell RCC shows VHL loss. COMLEX: papillary carcinomas are frequently multifocal and can be bilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 — molecular drivers that separate clear cell from papillary renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with painless hematuria and a 7-cm left renal mass has a hematocrit of 59% (reference range: 41–53%) with a normal oxygen saturation. Tumor secretion of which hormone best explains his hematocrit?","choices":{"A":"Erythropoietin","B":"Parathyroid hormone","C":"Growth hormone","D":"Antidiuretic hormone","E":"Thyroid hormone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Renal cell carcinoma is a great mimic and produces several syndromes from abnormal hormone production, including polycythemia. The polycythemia results from ectopic erythropoietin production by the tumor, and First Aid lists ectopic EPO among the paraneoplastic mediators of renal cell carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC is associated with the paraneoplastic mediators PTHrP, ectopic EPO, ACTH, and renin. COMLEX: unexplained erythrocytosis or hypercalcemia in an adult with hematuria or a flank mass should prompt renal imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 2 — paraneoplastic syndromes of renal cell carcinoma and benign renal tumor mimics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":1,"stem":"Which laboratory abnormality can result from tumor production of PTHrP in renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphatemia","B":"Hypermagnesemia","C":"Hypouricemia","D":"Hypercalcemia","E":"Hyperchloremia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypercalcemia is among the syndromes of abnormal hormone production listed for renal cell carcinoma, and First Aid names PTHrP as the tumor-derived mediator. PTHrP acts like PTH, which is phosphaturic and so lowers rather than raises phosphate, and magnesium or uric acid changes are not among the listed hormone syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: paraneoplastic hypercalcemia with low PTH points to PTHrP secretion. COMLEX: RCC can also present with fever, weight loss, and hypertension without any renal symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 2 — paraneoplastic syndromes of renal cell carcinoma and benign renal tumor mimics","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"PTHrP acts like PTH, which is ","B":"PTHrP acts like PTH, which is ","C":"PTHrP acts like PTH, which is "}},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":1,"stem":"Which benign renal tumor occurs in 25% to 50% of patients with tuberous sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Papillary adenoma","B":"Metanephric adenoma","C":"Angiomyolipoma","D":"Renal oncocytoma","E":"Medullary fibroma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiomyolipomas consist of vessels, smooth muscle, and fat and are present in 25% to 50% of patients with tuberous sclerosis, caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TSC1 or TSC2 tumor suppressor genes. Their clinical importance is largely due to susceptibility to spontaneous hemorrhage. Papillary adenomas, metanephric adenomas, oncocytomas and medullary fibromas are benign renal tumors without this tuberous sclerosis link.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tuberous sclerosis with seizures, intellectual disability, skin lesions, and renal angiomyolipomas. COMLEX: sudden flank pain and shock in a patient with tuberous sclerosis suggests hemorrhage into an angiomyolipoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 2 — paraneoplastic syndromes of renal cell carcinoma and benign renal tumor mimics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which tumor is the most common renal malignancy of early childhood, usually presenting between ages 2 and 4?","choices":{"A":"Angiomyolipoma","B":"Renal oncocytoma","C":"Chromophobe carcinoma","D":"Nephroblastoma","E":"Clear cell carcinoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nephroblastoma, also called Wilms tumor, is the most common renal malignancy of early childhood, ages 2 to 4 (First Aid; Robbins puts the peak at 2 to 5 years), and contains embryonic glomerular structures. Robbins notes that it follows renal cell carcinoma as the second most common malignant renal tumor overall. Renal cell carcinoma is the dominant kidney cancer of adults. Angiomyolipoma is a benign tumor of vessels, smooth muscle and fat associated with tuberous sclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a child with a large unilateral flank mass, hematuria, and possible hypertension is Wilms tumor until proven otherwise. COMLEX: examine for aniridia, hemihypertrophy, and genitourinary anomalies that signal a syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations and syndromes linked to pediatric renal tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Renal cell carcinoma is the do"}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":1,"stem":"Loss of which tumor suppressor gene, located on chromosome 11, causes Wilms tumor in the WAGR complex?","choices":{"A":"TP53","B":"WT1","C":"RB1","D":"APC","E":"NF2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"WAGR complex consists of Wilms tumor, Aniridia, Genitourinary malformations, and a Range of developmental delays, and is caused by deletion of WT1 on chromosome 11. TP53, RB1, APC, and NF2 are tumor suppressors not linked to this syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: WT1 deletion causes WAGR, and WT1 mutation causes Denys-Drash syndrome. COMLEX: Wilms tumor genes regulate normal renal development.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations and syndromes linked to pediatric renal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which syndrome combines Wilms tumor with macroglossia, organomegaly, and omphalocele?","choices":{"A":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","B":"Denys-Drash syndrome","C":"Tuberous sclerosis complex","D":"Hereditary leiomyomatosis syndrome","E":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome features Wilms tumor, organomegaly, macroglossia, hemihyperplasia, and omphalocele, from an imprinting defect causing genetic overexpression, which First Aid associates with WT2 mutation. Denys-Drash syndrome combines Wilms tumor with diffuse mesangial sclerosis and gonadal dysgenesis, and WAGR complex features aniridia and genitourinary malformations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: macroglossia plus omphalocele in a newborn suggests Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. COMLEX: recognize macroglossia and omphalocele at birth as a syndrome that carries a Wilms tumor risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations and syndromes linked to pediatric renal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man who smokes cigarettes has intermittent gross hematuria. CT shows a 9-cm mass in the upper pole of the right kidney. Nephrectomy reveals a yellow-gray, well-circumscribed spherical tumor with foci of hemorrhage and necrosis. Histology shows nests of cells with abundant clear cytoplasm and delicate branching vasculature, and cytogenetics reveal deletion of one copy of a gene on chromosome 3p. Which of the following best explains the tumor's growth advantage and rich blood supply?","choices":{"A":"Activated hepatocyte growth factor receptor signaling through MET","B":"Overexpressed TFE3 transcription factor after an Xp11 translocation","C":"Sustained HIF activity with VEGF and IGF-1 expression","D":"Impaired folliculin function in a tumor suppressor pathway","E":"Loss of fumarate hydratase with disrupted Krebs cycle activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Clear cell carcinoma is defined by deletion of one copy of chromosome 3p25.3, the site of the VHL gene, and the second copy is disabled by mutation or hypermethylation in up to 80% of cases. Loss of VHL leaves HIF levels high even in normoxia, driving expression of VEGF, which promotes angiogenesis, and IGF-1, which stimulates growth. MET drives papillary carcinoma, TFE3 translocation defines a distinct subtype, and folliculin and fumarate hydratase loss underlie separate hereditary syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC in a smoker with a 3p deletion is clear cell carcinoma with VHL loss and HIF stabilization. COMLEX: smoking doubles the incidence of renal cell carcinoma, and the tumor is often silent until it exceeds 10 cm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — downstream systemic consequences of clear cell renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"MET drives papillary carcinoma","D":"MET drives papillary carcinoma"}},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man whose father and paternal uncle had kidney cancer has multiple bilateral renal masses on CT. Biopsy of one lesion shows cuboidal cells in papillary formations with interstitial foam histiocytes in the papillary cores and low nuclear grade. Which of the following is the most likely genetic alteration?","choices":{"A":"Biallelic VHL loss with deletion of 3p","B":"Activating MET mutation with trisomy 7","C":"TFE3 overexpression with an Xp11 translocation","D":"Loss of TSC1 with unchecked mTOR signaling","E":"Multiple chromosome losses with extreme hypodiploidy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hereditary papillary carcinoma is autosomal dominant and shows multiple bilateral tumors with papillary histology and usually low nuclear grade. It is caused by gain-of-function mutations in MET on chromosome 7, and trisomy 7 is the most common cytogenetic abnormality of the familial form. VHL loss with 3p deletion characterizes clear cell carcinoma, and extreme hypodiploidy characterizes chromophobe carcinoma. TFE3 overexpression with an Xp11 translocation defines a separate, translocation subtype of renal cell carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary RCC equals MET, trisomy 7 and 17, and foamy macrophages in papillary cores, whereas clear cell RCC equals VHL and 3p deletion. COMLEX: papillary carcinomas account for 10% to 15% of renal cancers and are often multifocal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — MET activation in papillary carcinoma versus VHL loss in clear cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has multiple renal cysts and a known germline heterozygous loss-of-function VHL mutation. Over the next decade he develops multiple clear cell carcinomas in both kidneys. Which of the following best explains the development of these tumors?","choices":{"A":"Somatic loss or silencing of the remaining VHL allele","B":"Gain-of-function change in the remaining VHL allele","C":"Somatic activating mutation of MET in each kidney","D":"Germline loss of one HIF allele with reduced VEGF","E":"Loss of the Y chromosome in renal tubular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"VHL acts as a tumor suppressor gene. Individuals with VHL syndrome inherit one defective copy, and tumors develop when the second copy is disabled by an acquired somatic loss-of-function mutation or silenced by hypermethylation, the same event seen in up to 80% of sporadic clear cell cancers. One-half to two-thirds of patients develop renal cysts and bilateral, often multiple, carcinomas. MET activation and Y loss belong to papillary carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumor suppressor syndromes require loss of both alleles, the two-hit model. COMLEX: familial variants account for only 4% of renal cancers but have identified genes central to sporadic carcinogenesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — the two-hit model of an inherited germline mutation plus a somatic second hit, as in ADPKD","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"MET activation and Y loss belo"}},{"id":"sdl39_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man who smokes has intermittent gross hematuria, a dull right flank ache, and a 10-kg weight loss. Hemoglobin is 19.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and serum calcium is 12.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). CT shows a large solitary right renal mass with a tumor thrombus extending through the renal vein into the inferior vena cava. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma","B":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","C":"Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","D":"Renal oncocytoma","E":"Urothelial carcinoma of the pelvis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clear cell carcinoma usually occurs as a solitary unilateral lesion, and one of its striking characteristics is a tendency to invade the renal vein and grow as a solid column of cells up the inferior vena cava. Polycythemia and hypercalcemia are among its paraneoplastic syndromes. Papillary carcinomas tend to be multifocal, oncocytoma is benign, and pelvic urothelial carcinoma arises from the pelvic lining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC with erythrocytosis and hypercalcemia is a paraneoplastic pattern from EPO and PTHrP. COMLEX: with renal vein invasion or extension into perinephric fat, 5-year survival falls to approximately 60%.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — flank pain and hematuria compared across renal masses, stones, and obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man undergoes radical nephrectomy for a clear cell renal cell carcinoma that has invaded the renal vein. His oncologist explains that surveillance imaging will focus on the organ that renal cell carcinoma most often involves when it spreads to distant sites. Which organ is this?","choices":{"A":"Liver","B":"Brain","C":"Adrenal glands","D":"Lungs","E":"Bones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins gives the lungs (more than 50%) as the most common site of renal cell carcinoma metastasis, followed by bone (33%), then regional lymph nodes, liver, adrenal glands, and brain; renal cell carcinoma tends to metastasize widely before local symptoms, and 15% of new patients have radiologic evidence of metastases at presentation. Bone is the second most common site, not the first. The liver, adrenal glands, and brain are less frequent sites. Renal vein invasion lowers 5-year survival to about 60%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal cell carcinoma: classic triad of costovertebral pain, palpable mass, and hematuria in only 10%; hematuria is the most reliable clue; paraneoplastic syndromes include polycythemia, hypercalcemia, hypertension, hepatic dysfunction, Cushing syndrome, and feminization or masculinization; metastases go to lungs, then bones. COMLEX: Many tumors are now found incidentally on imaging done for other reasons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — VHL loss drives clear cell carcinoma growth and vascularity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The liver, adrenal glands, and","E":"Bone is the second most common"}},{"id":"sdl39_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has an incidental 6-cm mass in the kidney found on CT for abdominal pain. The nephrectomy specimen is a tan-to-mahogany-brown, well-circumscribed, homogeneous tumor with a central scar. Histology shows sheets of cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, small round nuclei with conspicuous nucleoli, and no perinuclear halos or prominent cell membranes. Electron microscopy shows numerous mitochondria. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Chromophobe carcinoma","B":"Clear cell carcinoma","C":"Papillary adenoma","D":"Angiomyolipoma","E":"Renal oncocytoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal oncocytoma is an epithelial neoplasm of eosinophilic cells with small, round, benign-appearing nuclei and numerous mitochondria, thought to arise from intercalated cells of collecting ducts. Grossly it is tan or mahogany brown and well circumscribed, with a central scar in one-third of cases, and it accounts for about 5% of renal neoplasms. Chromophobe carcinoma shows prominent cell membranes and perinuclear halos, although histologic distinction can be difficult. Papillary adenomas are small cortical lesions, usually under 1.5 cm, with papillary fronds, and angiomyolipoma is made of vessels, smooth muscle and fat.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mahogany brown mass with central scar and mitochondria-rich eosinophilic cells is oncocytoma. COMLEX: because it can mimic chromophobe carcinoma, resection is usually needed to exclude malignancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — how renal carcinoma subtypes are separated by histology and cytogenetics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought in with a large, palpable right flank mass and blood pressure above the 95th percentile. Examination shows absent irises bilaterally, an undescended left testis, and delayed developmental milestones. Which of the following genetic alterations most likely underlies his tumor?","choices":{"A":"Germline deletion of the WT1 gene","B":"Germline loss of the VHL gene","C":"Germline loss of the TSC2 gene","D":"Germline loss of the folliculin gene","E":"Germline activation of the MET gene"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The triad of aniridia, genitourinary malformations such as an undescended testis, and developmental delay with a renal mass in a child is WAGR complex, caused by deletion of WT1 on chromosome 11. Wilms tumor presents most often as a large, palpable unilateral flank mass with hematuria and possible hypertension. VHL, TSC2, folliculin, and MET alterations cause adult-type or unrelated renal syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aniridia plus a Wilms tumor points to WAGR and WT1 deletion. COMLEX: recognize the syndromic features, because a genetic syndrome changes the evaluation of a child with a renal mass.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — WT1 makes the metanephric mesenchyme competent to signal the ureteric bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has a large Wilms tumor removed after preoperative chemotherapy produced little shrinkage. Microscopy shows the triphasic pattern but also cells with large, hyperchromatic, pleomorphic nuclei and abnormal mitoses. Her oncologist warns that this histologic feature carries an adverse prognosis. Which genetic alteration most likely accompanies this feature?","choices":{"A":"Germline deletion of the WT1 gene","B":"Gain-of-function beta-catenin mutation","C":"Loss of miRNA-processing DROSHA or DICER1","D":"Inactivating mutation of the TP53 gene","E":"Loss of imprinting with gene overexpression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 5% of Wilms tumors are anaplastic, with large, hyperchromatic, pleomorphic nuclei and abnormal mitoses; anaplasia correlates with TP53 mutations and the emergence of resistance to chemotherapy, because loss of p53 removes pro-apoptotic signaling after DNA damage, and it is perhaps the most critical determinant of adverse prognosis. Germline WT1 deletion underlies the WAGR syndrome tumors. Beta-catenin gain-of-function mutations occur in about 10% of sporadic Wilms tumors without this link to anaplasia. DROSHA, DGCR8, and DICER1 alterations are associated with predominantly blastemal histology. Loss of imprinting with gene overexpression is the mechanism of Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wilms tumor: triphasic (blastemal, stromal, epithelial); anaplasia (about 5%) = TP53 mutation, chemoresistance, worse prognosis; nephrogenic rests are precursors (adjacent to 25–40% of unilateral and nearly all bilateral tumors). COMLEX: Nephrogenic rests in the resected kidney call for long-term surveillance of the contralateral kidney.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — WT1 and mesenchymal-to-epithelial conversion in normal nephrogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has a markedly enlarged tongue, bowel herniated through the abdominal wall at the umbilicus, and enlargement of the right arm and leg compared with the left. Ultrasonography shows enlarged kidneys and liver. The child is at increased risk for a renal malignancy. Which of the following underlying mechanisms is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Contiguous deletion of chromosome 11 including WT1","B":"Germline missense mutation of WT1","C":"Imprinting defect causing gene overexpression","D":"Germline loss of function mutation of VHL","E":"Germline loss of function mutation of TSC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Macroglossia, omphalocele, organomegaly, and hemihyperplasia define Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, caused by an imprinting defect causing genetic overexpression, which First Aid associates with WT2 mutation, and it carries a risk of Wilms tumor. Contiguous chromosome 11 deletion with WT1 loss is WAGR complex, and WT1 mutation causes Denys-Drash syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beckwith-Wiedemann equals imprinting defect, macroglossia, omphalocele, hemihyperplasia, and Wilms tumor risk. COMLEX: hemihyperplasia and an abdominal wall herniation on newborn examination should prompt consideration of this syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome among the germline alterations that predispose to Wilms tumor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Contiguous chromosome 11 delet"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl39_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with cerebellar hemangioblastomas has bilateral renal masses. A resected tumor shows nests of cells with abundant clear cytoplasm separated by a delicate branching vascular network. Which process most directly produces the clear cytoplasm?","choices":{"A":"Mitochondrial packing of the cytoplasm","B":"HIF-driven glycogen and lipid storage","C":"Mucin accumulation in apical vacuoles","D":"Hydropic swelling from ischemic injury","E":"Perinuclear halo from fixation shrinkage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hemangioblastomas with bilateral renal tumors suggest von Hippel-Lindau disease and clear cell carcinoma. Without VHL protein, HIF-1 escapes degradation and, with MYC, shifts metabolism toward aerobic glycolysis and glycogen and lipid storage, which gives the cytoplasm its clear appearance. Mitochondrial packing makes the granular eosinophilic cells of oncocytoma. Mucin accumulation in apical vacuoles describes glandular adenocarcinomas, not renal cell carcinoma. Hydropic swelling from ischemia is reversible cell injury, not a tumor phenotype. A perinuclear halo is the feature of chromophobe carcinoma. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 39, VHL-HIF pathway and clear cytoplasm); Robbins Ch. 20 (clear cell carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VHL loss on 3p stabilizes HIF-1, which drives VEGF (hypervascularity) and lipid- and glycogen-rich clear cells. COMLEX: bilateral renal tumors in a young patient suggest a hereditary syndrome such as VHL disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 5 — clear cell carcinoma genetics and histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl39_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":3,"stem":"A 54-year-old man whose father had kidney cancer has multiple bilateral renal masses on CT. Biopsy shows papillary architecture, and germline testing shows an activating mutation of a receptor tyrosine kinase gene on chromosome 7. Which ligand normally activates this receptor?","choices":{"A":"Vascular endothelial growth factor","B":"Insulin-like growth factor 2","C":"Hepatocyte growth factor","D":"Platelet-derived growth factor","E":"Epidermal growth factor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hereditary papillary renal cell carcinoma is caused by germline gain-of-function mutations in MET on chromosome 7, and MET is the receptor for hepatocyte growth factor (scatter factor). Papillary tumors are often multifocal and bilateral. Vascular endothelial growth factor is a HIF target in clear cell carcinoma and acts on its own receptors. Insulin-like growth factor 2 is overexpressed in Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. Platelet-derived growth factor is another HIF target in clear cell carcinoma. Epidermal growth factor acts through EGFR, which is not the papillary carcinoma driver. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 39, MET activation in papillary RCC); Robbins Ch. 20 (papillary carcinoma and MET).","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary RCC is MET (the HGF receptor) with trisomies 7 and 17, independent of 3p and VHL. COMLEX: multifocal bilateral tumors suggest papillary histology or a hereditary syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 1 — HGF and MET signaling also drive ureteric bud branching","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl39_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with a 7-cm renal mass has an alkaline phosphatase of 310 U/L (reference range: 40–129 U/L) and a prolonged prothrombin time. Imaging shows no hepatic lesions and no venous invasion, and these tests normalize after nephrectomy. Which process best explains his hepatic abnormalities?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic metastatic deposits","B":"Paraneoplastic liver dysfunction","C":"Drug-induced hepatitis","D":"Congestive hepatopathy from IVC tumor","E":"Biliary obstruction by nodes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Liver dysfunction without hepatic metastases that resolves after nephrectomy is Stauffer syndrome, a paraneoplastic feature of renal cell carcinoma. Hepatic metastatic deposits are excluded by imaging and by normalization after the kidney is removed. Biliary obstruction by nodes would leave a visible lesion and persist after nephrectomy. Drug-induced hepatitis has no culprit drug here. Congestive hepatopathy from an IVC tumor requires the venous invasion that imaging excluded. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 39, paraneoplastic syndromes of RCC including Stauffer syndrome); Robbins Ch. 20 (renal cell carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC paraneoplastics are EPO (erythrocytosis), PTHrP (hypercalcemia), renin (hypertension), ACTH (Cushing), and Stauffer syndrome. COMLEX: RCC is a great mimic, so unexplained systemic findings with a renal mass suggest it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 7 — RCC paraneoplastic syndromes in diagnostic cases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl39_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has an incidental 4-cm renal mass. The resected tumor is mahogany brown with a central stellate scar, and electron microscopy shows cytoplasm packed with mitochondria. From which cell type does this tumor arise?","choices":{"A":"Distal convoluted tubule cells","B":"Proximal tubular epithelial cells","C":"Collecting duct principal cells","D":"Juxtaglomerular granular cells","E":"Collecting duct intercalated cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A mahogany brown tumor with a central stellate scar and mitochondria-packed cells is an oncocytoma, a benign tumor arising from intercalated cells of the collecting duct. Proximal tubular epithelial cells give rise to clear cell and papillary carcinomas. Collecting duct principal cells are not the origin of oncocytoma. Juxtaglomerular granular cells give rise to the rare renin-secreting tumor. Distal convoluted tubule cells are not the source of this tumor. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 39, renal oncocytoma); Robbins Ch. 20 (oncocytoma from intercalated cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oncocytoma is benign, from intercalated cells, mahogany brown, with a central scar and abundant mitochondria. COMLEX: it mimics renal cell carcinoma on imaging, so diagnosis is often made after resection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 7 — benign mimics of renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl39_q5","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with tuberous sclerosis has sudden severe left flank pain and dizziness. Her blood pressure is 86/50 mm Hg, and CT shows a large retroperitoneal hematoma around a fat-containing renal mass. Which mechanism best explains the bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Tumor thrombus in the renal vein","B":"Vessel erosion by an infected cyst","C":"Necrosis of a clear cell carcinoma","D":"Torsion of an ectopic kidney","E":"Rupture of dysplastic tumor vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A fat-containing renal mass in tuberous sclerosis is an angiomyolipoma, made of dysplastic thick-walled vessels, smooth muscle, and mature fat. Its aneurysmal vessels can rupture into the retroperitoneum (Wunderlich syndrome). A tumor thrombus in the renal vein is typical of clear cell carcinoma and does not cause acute hemorrhage. Necrosis of a clear cell carcinoma does not fit a fat-containing mass in tuberous sclerosis. Torsion of an ectopic kidney is not suggested by the imaging. Vessel erosion by an infected cyst fits neither the mass nor the clinical picture. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 39, angiomyolipoma and Wunderlich syndrome); Robbins Ch. 20 (angiomyolipoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiomyolipoma means vessels, smooth muscle, and fat, and it occurs in 25% to 50% of tuberous sclerosis patients (TSC1 or TSC2). COMLEX: retroperitoneal hemorrhage with shock is the main hazard of large angiomyolipomas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 7 — angiomyolipoma as a benign renal mimic","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl39_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has a large flank mass. The resected tumor shows sheets of small round blue cells, abortive tubules, and a third component. Which finding best describes that third component?","choices":{"A":"Spindle stroma with rhabdomyoblasts","B":"Psammoma bodies in papillae","C":"Glomeruloid Schiller-Duval bodies","D":"Mitochondria-rich eosinophilic cells","E":"Clear cells with lipid vacuoles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Small blue blastemal cells and abortive tubules in a young child's flank mass indicate Wilms tumor, which is triphasic. The stromal component is spindle-cell mesenchyme that often shows heterologous skeletal muscle (rhabdomyoblasts), cartilage, bone, or fat. Glomeruloid Schiller-Duval bodies define yolk sac tumor. Psammoma bodies in papillae occur in papillary renal cell carcinoma. Mitochondria-rich eosinophilic cells define oncocytoma. Clear cells with lipid vacuoles define clear cell carcinoma in adults. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 39, triphasic histopathology of Wilms tumor); Robbins Ch. 20 (Wilms tumor).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wilms tumor is triphasic (blastemal, epithelial, and stromal) and is the most common renal cancer in children under 5. COMLEX: a smooth flank mass in a toddler suggests Wilms tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 5 — Wilms tumor genetics and syndromes","isHighYield":true}],"40":[{"id":"sdl40_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":1,"stem":"Which malformation places the urethral opening on the dorsal surface of the penis?","choices":{"A":"Hypospadias","B":"Phimosis","C":"Balanoposthitis","D":"Epispadias","E":"Peyronie disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Malformation of the urethral groove and canal creates an abnormal opening on the ventral surface of the penis in hypospadias or on the dorsal surface in epispadias. Hypospadias is the more common of the two and occurs in approximately 1 in 300 live male births. Phimosis is a small preputial orifice, balanoposthitis is infection of the glans and prepuce, and Peyronie disease is a fibrous penile plaque.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypospadias is ventral and more common, whereas epispadias is dorsal. COMLEX: both can accompany failure of testicular descent and urinary tract malformations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 1 — congenital urethral malformations and HPV-associated precancerous lesions of the penis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":1,"stem":"Which complication is a recognized consequence of the often constricted abnormal urethral opening in hypospadias?","choices":{"A":"Cavernosal fibrosis with painful curvature","B":"Preputial scarring with balanoposthitis","C":"Testicular torsion with hemorrhagic infarct","D":"Penile plaques with squamous dysplasia","E":"Urinary obstruction with ascending infection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The abnormal opening in hypospadias is often constricted, producing urinary tract obstruction and an increased risk of ascending infections. When the orifice lies near the base of the penis, normal ejaculation and insemination are hampered and may cause sterility. Cavernosal fibrosis describes Peyronie disease, and preputial scarring describes phimosis. Testicular torsion comes from twisting of the spermatic cord, and squamous plaques with dysplasia are penile intraepithelial neoplasia; neither follows from a narrow urethral opening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypospadias and epispadias can be associated with cryptorchidism and other urinary tract malformations. COMLEX: even isolated urethral defects carry clinical significance through obstruction and infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 1 — congenital urethral malformations and HPV-associated precancerous lesions of the penis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":1,"stem":"Which HPV type is most commonly associated with HPV-related penile intraepithelial neoplasia such as Bowen disease?","choices":{"A":"HPV 6","B":"HPV 16","C":"HPV 11","D":"HPV 1","E":"HPV 2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HPV-associated penile intraepithelial neoplasia, seen clinically as Bowen disease and bowenoid papulosis, is associated with high-risk HPV, most commonly HPV 16. High-risk types encode E6 and E7, which inactivate p53 and RB. Low-risk HPV 6 and, less often, HPV 11 cause condyloma acuminatum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high-risk HPV drives dysplasia and carcinoma, whereas HPV 6 and 11 cause benign genital warts. COMLEX: circumcision confers protection against penile cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 1 — congenital urethral malformations and HPV-associated precancerous lesions of the penis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Testicular torsion in adolescents and adults results from which anatomic defect?","choices":{"A":"Fusion of the two testes","B":"Dilated pampiniform venous plexus","C":"Bell-clapper abnormality","D":"Thickened tunica albuginea","E":"Failure of the inguinal ring to close"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In adults, torsion results from a bilateral anatomic defect that leads to increased mobility of the testes, termed the bell-clapper abnormality. Neonatal torsion occurs without an associated anatomic defect. Fusion of the testes is synorchism, a rare anomaly. A dilated pampiniform vein is a varicocele, and a thick tunica albuginea or an open inguinal ring is not the described defect; adult torsion comes from the bell-clapper mobility of the testes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bell-clapper deformity predisposes to torsion, presenting with sudden testicular pain, often without an inciting injury. COMLEX: torsion is one of the few true urologic emergencies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — torsion and cryptorchidism are contrasted by their mechanism of testicular injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A dilated pampiniform vein is ","E":"A dilated pampiniform vein is "}},{"id":"sdl40_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":1,"stem":"What is the gross appearance of a testis in advanced torsion?","choices":{"A":"Swollen dark hemorrhagic and necrotic testis","B":"Firm gray-white lobulated mass without necrosis","C":"Small firm testis with hyalinized tubular cords","D":"Golden brown circumscribed nodule under 5 cm","E":"Enlarged pale testis with diffuse abscesses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Twisting of the spermatic cord cuts off venous drainage while the thick-walled arteries remain patent, producing intense vascular engorgement and then hemorrhagic infarction. In advanced stages the swollen testis consists entirely of soft, necrotic, hemorrhagic tissue. A homogeneous gray-white lobulated mass suggests seminoma, a small firm testis with hyalinized tubules suggests cryptorchid atrophy, and a golden brown nodule suggests a Leydig cell tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: venous outflow is blocked first, giving hemorrhagic rather than pale infarction. COMLEX: the dark discoloration reflects hemorrhage and infarction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — torsion and cryptorchidism are contrasted by their mechanism of testicular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Within approximately how long after the onset of torsion can manual untwisting spare the testis from orchiectomy?","choices":{"A":"About 12 hours","B":"About 6 hours","C":"About 24 hours","D":"About 48 hours","E":"About 72 hours"},"correct":"B","explanation":"If the testis is manually untwisted within approximately 6 hours of the onset of torsion, the affected testis may be spared an orchiectomy. Longer delays allow hemorrhagic infarction to progress toward irreversible necrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: torsion is a surgical emergency with a salvage window of about 6 hours. COMLEX: First Aid lists absent cremasteric reflex and a high-riding testis as bedside findings, and orchiopexy is done bilaterally.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — torsion and cryptorchidism are contrasted by their mechanism of testicular injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Longer delays allow hemorrhagi","C":"Longer delays allow hemorrhagi","D":"Longer delays allow hemorrhagi","E":"Longer delays allow hemorrhagi"}},{"id":"sdl40_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular neoplasm is characterized by Schiller-Duval bodies?","choices":{"A":"Yolk sac tumor","B":"Embryonal carcinoma","C":"Choriocarcinoma","D":"Seminoma","E":"Teratoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In approximately 50% of yolk sac tumors, structures resembling endodermal sinuses, called Schiller-Duval bodies, are seen. They consist of a mesodermal core with a central capillary and visceral and parietal layers of cells resembling primitive glomeruli. Yolk sac tumor cells express AFP, glypican-3, and cytokeratin. Embryonal carcinoma grows in anaplastic alveolar or tubular patterns, choriocarcinoma contains trophoblasts, seminoma has clear cells with lymphocyte-rich septa, and teratoma shows tissues of several germ layers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schiller-Duval bodies and AFP mark yolk sac tumor, the most common testicular tumor in children up to 3 years. COMLEX: serum AFP is used to diagnose and monitor these tumors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — germ cell tumor subtypes are separated by their histology and by their characteristic serum markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old man has hemoptysis, multiple bilateral lung nodules, and a small hemorrhagic mass in the left testis. Serum hCG is markedly elevated, and serum AFP is normal. Which germ cell tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Embryonal carcinoma","B":"Yolk sac tumor","C":"Choriocarcinoma","D":"Seminoma","E":"Spermatocytic tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choriocarcinoma is composed of two intimately juxtaposed cell types, syncytiotrophoblasts and cytotrophoblasts, and serum hCG is invariably elevated, often markedly. Hemorrhage and necrosis are extremely common, and widespread metastasis occurs early. About 15% of seminomas contain syncytiotrophoblasts, but hCG is elevated only minimally. Marked AFP comes from yolk sac elements and is normal here, embryonal carcinoma lacks the trophoblasts that make hCG, and spermatocytic tumor affects older men and does not metastasize.","boardPrep":"Step 1: choriocarcinoma equals hCG, hemorrhagic primary, and early hematogenous spread. COMLEX: pure choriocarcinoma has a poor prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — germ cell tumor subtypes are separated by their histology and by their characteristic serum markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic pattern characterizes classic seminoma?","choices":{"A":"Lace-like network with Schiller-Duval bodies","B":"Syncytiotrophoblasts admixed with cytotrophoblasts","C":"Anaplastic cells in alveolar or tubular patterns","D":"Clear glycogen-rich cells with lymphocytic septa","E":"Cartilage, neural tissue, and squamous epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Seminoma is composed of sheets of uniform round to polyhedral cells with distinct membranes, clear cytoplasm containing glycogen, and large central nuclei with prominent nucleoli, divided into lobules by delicate fibrous septa with a lymphocytic infiltrate. Lace-like reticular growth with Schiller-Duval bodies describes yolk sac tumor, trophoblastic cells describe choriocarcinoma, alveolar or tubular anaplastic cells describe embryonal carcinoma, and multiple germ layer tissues describe teratoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma is the most common germ cell tumor, radiosensitive, with an excellent prognosis and a gray-white homogeneous cut surface. COMLEX: seminoma cells are typically positive for KIT and OCT3/4 and negative for cytokeratin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — germ cell tumor subtypes are separated by their histology and by their characteristic serum markers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lace-like reticular growth wit","E":"Lace-like reticular growth wit"}},{"id":"sdl40_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old uncircumcised man has a solitary, thickened, gray-white plaque on the penile shaft that has been present for 8 months. Biopsy shows dysplastic squamous cells with large hyperchromatic nuclei, lack of orderly maturation, and numerous mitoses, all confined to the epidermis above an intact basement membrane. In situ hybridization detects high-risk HPV 16. Which of the following best explains how this lesion can progress to invasive carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Loss of VHL with stabilized HIF signaling","B":"Isochromosome 12p with KIT activation","C":"Viral E6 and E7 inactivation of p53 and RB","D":"Loss of fumarate hydratase with Krebs cycle disruption","E":"Gain-of-function MET activation by hepatocyte growth factor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is Bowen disease, a form of HPV-associated penile intraepithelial neoplasia, which gives rise to infiltrating squamous cell carcinoma in 10% of patients. High-risk HPV encodes E6 and E7, which inactivate the p53 and RB tumor suppressor proteins, leading to genomic instability and increased proliferation, the same role played in cervical cancer. The other alterations belong to renal or germ cell tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high-risk HPV E6 and E7 inactivate p53 and RB, causing genomic instability and proliferation. COMLEX: Bowen disease on the shaft appears as a solitary plaque, whereas on the glans it looks velvety red.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — carcinoma in situ progressing to invasive carcinoma in the lower urinary tract","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old sexually active man has multiple reddish-brown papules on the penile shaft for 3 months. Biopsy shows dysplastic squamous cells resembling carcinoma in situ, and high-risk HPV 16 is detected. At 6-month follow-up, several lesions have regressed spontaneously. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Bowen disease","B":"Condyloma acuminatum","C":"Verrucous carcinoma","D":"Basaloid carcinoma","E":"Bowenoid papulosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bowenoid papulosis occurs in sexually active adults and differs from Bowen disease by younger patient age and multiple rather than solitary reddish-brown papules. It is etiologically related to HPV 16 and histologically indistinguishable from Bowen disease, but it virtually never develops into invasive carcinoma and usually regresses spontaneously. Condyloma acuminatum, caused by HPV 6 and 11, keeps orderly maturation, and verrucous carcinoma is an HPV-independent well-differentiated subtype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple papules in a young adult with HPV 16 and spontaneous regression suggest bowenoid papulosis, while a solitary plaque in an older man suggests Bowen disease. COMLEX: both are HPV-associated precursor lesions of the penile intraepithelial neoplasia group.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 1 — HPV-associated penile epithelial lesions and the clinical and histologic features that separate them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man reports 6 months of penile curvature to the left during erection and pain with intercourse. Examination reveals a firm palpable induration along the left side of the penile shaft, and the glans and foreskin are normal. Which of the following best describes the underlying lesion?","choices":{"A":"Squamous dysplasia above an intact basement membrane","B":"Collagen plaque between cavernosa and tunica albuginea","C":"Papillary epithelium with perinuclear vacuolization","D":"Candida infection of the glans and prepuce","E":"Preputial ring scarring after repeated infection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peyronie disease is characterized by hard penile plaques from collagen deposition in the connective tissue between the corpora cavernosa and the tunica albuginea. It is reactive rather than neoplastic, and clinically causes penile curvature toward the side of the lesion and pain during intercourse. Squamous dysplasia describes penile intraepithelial neoplasia, koilocytosis describes condyloma, Candida causes balanoposthitis, and preputial scarring describes phimosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyronie disease results from microvascular trauma with sclerosing chronic inflammation, giving curvature toward the plaque. COMLEX: Robbins lists surgery and collagenase injection to lyse the fibrous plaques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 4 — erectile physiology and the NO-cGMP pathway relevant to penile function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Squamous dysplasia describes p"}},{"id":"sdl40_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy is awakened from sleep by sudden severe left scrotal pain with vomiting. There was no trauma. The left testis is high-riding with a horizontal lie, and the cremasteric reflex is absent on the left. If the condition is not corrected, which of the following processes best explains the tissue damage that will develop?","choices":{"A":"Arterial occlusion with pale coagulative infarction","B":"Ascending bacterial infection with abscess formation","C":"Lymphatic obstruction with interstitial edema","D":"Venous occlusion with hemorrhagic infarction","E":"Immune vasculitis with fibrinoid necrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Twisting of the spermatic cord typically cuts off venous drainage while the thick-walled arteries remain patent, producing intense vascular engorgement followed by hemorrhagic infarction. Torsion is one of the few true urologic emergencies, and adolescent torsion usually reflects the bilateral bell-clapper abnormality with increased testicular mobility. Infarction changes progress from congestion to widespread hemorrhage with duration. Infection and lymphatic obstruction are not what cord twisting causes; the damage comes from blocked venous outflow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute severe scrotal pain with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex is torsion, and the treatment is immediate surgical exploration. COMLEX: manual untwisting within approximately 6 hours may spare the testis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — torsion as a time-critical testicular vascular emergency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Infection and lymphatic obstru"}},{"id":"sdl40_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old boy has an empty right hemiscrotum, and a testis is palpable in the right inguinal canal. He undergoes orchiopexy. His mother asks why the cancer risk remains higher after surgery and is also higher for his normally descended left testis. Which of the following best answers her question?","choices":{"A":"An intrinsic in utero defect in gonadal development","B":"Heat injury that persists after repositioning","C":"Androgen excess from prominent Leydig cells","D":"Scarring of the inguinal canal after surgery","E":"Ascending infection along the spermatic cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cryptorchid testis carries a four- to 10-fold higher risk for testicular cancer, and repositioning does not eliminate the elevated risk. Because the contralateral, normally descended testis is also at higher risk, cryptorchidism and the associated risk for germ cell neoplasm is believed to be linked to an in utero defect in gonadal cell development, as in testicular dysgenesis syndrome, and not to the abnormal anatomic position. Leydig cell androgen excess, inguinal scarring and ascending infection would not explain the raised risk in the normally descended testis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cryptorchidism raises germ cell tumor risk, and medical therapy with orchiopexy reduces but does not eliminate the risk of cancer and sterility. COMLEX: histologic changes in a malpositioned testis begin as early as 2 years of age, so early repair is recommended.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — germ cell neoplasia in situ as the precursor lesion of testicular germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Because the contralateral, nor"}},{"id":"sdl40_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man evaluated for infertility has a history of untreated bilateral cryptorchidism. Testicular biopsy shows thickened tubular basement membranes, hyalinized tubular cords, and prominent interstitial cells. Which of the following cell populations is characteristically lost from the seminiferous tubules?","choices":{"A":"Sertoli cells","B":"Spermatogonia","C":"Leydig cells","D":"Stromal cells","E":"Neoplastic germ cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the cryptorchid testis the basement membrane of the spermatic tubules thickens early, and subsequent loss of spermatogonia leaves tubules with only Sertoli cells. Scarred tubules may appear as dense cords of hyaline connective tissue with a concomitant increase in interstitial stroma. Leydig cells are spared and appear relatively prominent, which explains the interstitial cells on biopsy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cryptorchid atrophy features Sertoli-only tubules, thickened basement membranes, and spared Leydig cells. COMLEX: bilateral disease causes sterility, and repair does not fully restore spermatogenesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — primary testicular failure with hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, distinguished from secondary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has painless enlargement of the right testis. Radical orchiectomy specimen shows a bulky, homogeneous, gray-white lobulated mass without hemorrhage or necrosis. Histology shows sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm in lobules separated by fibrous septa containing lymphocytes, and no other germ cell component is identified. Preoperative serum AFP is normal and hCG is mildly elevated. Which of the following best explains the hCG elevation?","choices":{"A":"Syncytiotrophoblasts within the tumor","B":"Yolk sac elements within the tumor","C":"Eosinophilic hyaline globules within tumor cells","D":"Hyperplastic Leydig cells in the adjacent testis","E":"Teratomatous elements within the tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is a seminoma, and approximately 15% of seminomas contain syncytiotrophoblasts. In this subset serum hCG is elevated, but not to the extent seen in choriocarcinoma. Yolk sac elements produce AFP, and the eosinophilic hyaline globules of yolk sac tumor contain AFP and alpha-1-antitrypsin, not hCG; the normal AFP and the absence of other germ cell components also exclude yolk sac and teratomatous elements. Leydig cells respond to hCG but do not secrete it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma may show mildly elevated hCG from syncytiotrophoblasts with a normal AFP. COMLEX: therapy and prognosis depend on stage and histologic type, and seminoma is radiosensitive and chemosensitive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — serum tumor markers and the histologic components that produce them in germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has a painless left scrotal mass. Radical orchiectomy shows a lace-like network of cuboidal cells with structures that have a mesodermal core, a central capillary, and surrounding cells resembling primitive glomeruli. Which of the following serum markers is most likely to be markedly elevated?","choices":{"A":"hCG","B":"PSA","C":"AFP","D":"CEA","E":"CA 125"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lace-like reticular pattern with Schiller-Duval bodies identifies a yolk sac tumor, the most common testicular tumor in infants and children up to 3 years. Eosinophilic hyaline globules containing AFP are typical, and marked elevation of serum AFP is produced by yolk sac tumor. Marked hCG elevation reflects syncytiotrophoblastic elements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AFP marks yolk sac tumor and hCG marks choriocarcinoma. COMLEX: after orchiectomy, persistent AFP or hCG elevation indicates metastatic spread.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — histologic patterns that distinguish the non-seminomatous germ cell tumor subtypes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man underwent right radical orchiectomy for a mixed germ cell tumor with embryonal carcinoma and yolk sac elements. Preoperative serum AFP was markedly elevated. Eight weeks after surgery, repeat serum AFP measurements remain persistently elevated. Which of the following is the most likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"A normal postoperative inflammatory response","B":"Normal AFP production by the remaining testis","C":"Production by a new seminoma in the left testis","D":"Metastatic spread beyond the testis","E":"Persistent effect of testicular dysgenesis syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the staging of testicular germ cell tumors, persistent elevation of hCG or AFP after orchiectomy indicates metastatic spread. Markedly elevated AFP or hCG is produced by yolk sac tumor and syncytiotrophoblastic elements, respectively, and both are elevated in more than 80% of nonseminomatous germ cell tumors at diagnosis. Retroperitoneal paraaortic nodes are generally the first involved, and nonseminomatous tumors tend to metastasize earlier and more often by the hematogenous route. A postoperative inflammatory response does not keep a tumor marker raised 8 weeks after the source is removed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: serum markers are used to evaluate a testicular mass, stage the tumor, assess burden, and monitor therapy. COMLEX: about 90% of patients with nonseminomatous tumors achieve complete remission with aggressive chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — serum tumor markers as tools for staging and monitoring germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl40_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old uncircumcised man has an ulcerated, indurated mass on the glans penis. Biopsy shows invasive squamous cell carcinoma positive for HPV 16. Which factor most strongly predicts his prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Depth of HPV 16 integration","B":"Presence of phimosis at diagnosis","C":"Size of the primary lesion","D":"Circumcision status in infancy","E":"Inguinal lymph node metastasis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Penile squamous cell carcinoma spreads first to inguinal lymph nodes, and regional nodal metastasis is the most significant prognostic factor. The depth of HPV 16 integration is not a clinical prognostic measure. Phimosis is a risk factor for developing carcinoma, not a predictor of outcome. The size of the primary lesion matters less than nodal status. Circumcision status in infancy affects the risk of disease, not prognosis once it develops. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 40, invasive penile SCC and lymphatic metastasis); Robbins Ch. 21 (penile squamous cell carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: penile SCC risk factors are lack of circumcision, smegma, smoking, and HPV 16 or 18, and nodal status sets prognosis. COMLEX: examine the inguinal nodes in any patient with a penile lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — seminoma spreads to para-aortic, not inguinal, nodes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl40_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old uncircumcised man with diabetes has had repeated bouts of glans and foreskin inflammation with smegma buildup. He now has a foreskin that does not retract over the glans. Which process best explains this new finding?","choices":{"A":"Congenital fusion of urethral folds","B":"Scarring of the preputial ring","C":"Lymphatic obstruction of the shaft","D":"Venous thrombosis of the prepuce","E":"Fibrous plaque of tunica albuginea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Recurrent balanoposthitis causes inflammatory scarring of the preputial ring, producing acquired phimosis. Phimosis traps smegma and raises the risk of infection and carcinoma. Incomplete fusion of the urethral folds causes hypospadias, not phimosis. A fibrous plaque of the tunica albuginea causes Peyronie disease with penile curvature. Lymphatic obstruction of the shaft causes edema, not a fixed preputial ring. Venous thrombosis of the prepuce is not a feature of recurrent balanoposthitis. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 40, balanoposthitis and phimosis); Robbins Ch. 21 (inflammatory lesions of the penis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: balanoposthitis in uncircumcised men with poor hygiene can scar into phimosis. COMLEX: phimosis is a risk factor for penile squamous cell carcinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 1 — phimosis raises the risk of penile squamous cell carcinoma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl40_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has an orchiectomy of an intra-abdominal testis that was never brought into the scrotum. Which histologic finding is expected in the removed testis?","choices":{"A":"Hemorrhagic infarction with venous thrombosis","B":"Hyalinized tubules with spared Leydig cells","C":"Granulomas destroying seminiferous tubules","D":"Lost Leydig cells with intact spermatogenesis","E":"Interstitial plasma cells with orchitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An undescended testis at core temperature loses germ cells from about age 2. Tubular basement membranes thicken, and the seminiferous tubules hyalinize. Sertoli cells remain, and interstitial Leydig cells are spared and appear prominent. Hemorrhagic infarction with venous thrombosis describes torsion. Granulomas destroying tubules describe granulomatous orchitis. Lost Leydig cells with intact spermatogenesis reverses the actual pattern. Interstitial plasma cells with orchitis describe an inflammatory process, not cryptorchid atrophy. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 40, histological progression of cryptorchidism); Robbins Ch. 21 (cryptorchidism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cryptorchid testes show germ cell loss and tubular hyalinization with spared Leydig cells, plus a 4- to 10-fold germ cell tumor risk. COMLEX: orchiopexy at 6 to 12 months (Robbins; First Aid 2025 says before 2 years of age) lowers but does not remove the cancer and infertility risks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — germ cell tumors are the main cancer risk of cryptorchidism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl40_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy has sudden severe left testicular pain, a high-riding testis with a horizontal lie, and an absent cremasteric reflex. At surgery 3 hours later, the left testis is untwisted and viable. The surgeon also fixes the right testis. Which reason best supports fixing the right testis?","choices":{"A":"Existing atrophy of the right testis","B":"Injury spreading to the other testis","C":"Bilateral nature of the anomaly","D":"Antisperm antibodies against it","E":"Need for a cooler scrotal position"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adolescent torsion arises from the bell-clapper deformity, in which the tunica vaginalis attaches high on the cord and lets the testis hang freely. Because this defect is bilateral, surgeons fix both testes. Nothing indicates existing atrophy of the right testis. Injury does not spread to the other testis. Antisperm antibodies are not the rationale for contralateral fixation. A cooler scrotal position is not needed, because a descended testis is already at scrotal temperature. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 40, bell-clapper deformity and bilateral orchiopexy); Robbins Ch. 21 (testicular torsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: torsion occludes venous drainage while arterial inflow continues, causing hemorrhagic infarction, and surgery within about 6 hours can save the testis. COMLEX: bilateral orchiopexy is standard because the bell-clapper deformity is bilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 6 — the testes share T10 to T11 sympathetic innervation with the kidneys","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl40_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has a painless, firm right testicular mass. Radical orchiectomy shows a homogeneous gray-white tumor of uniform clear cells divided by fibrous septa with lymphocytes. Serum AFP is normal. To which site does this tumor most often spread first?","choices":{"A":"Inguinal lymph nodes","B":"Pulmonary parenchyma","C":"Hepatic parenchyma","D":"Lumbar vertebral bone marrow","E":"Para-aortic lymph nodes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Uniform clear cells with lymphocyte-rich septa and a normal AFP indicate seminoma, which spreads late and mainly through lymphatics to the retroperitoneal para-aortic nodes. Inguinal lymph nodes drain the scrotal skin and penis, not the testis. Pulmonary parenchyma is an early hematogenous target of non-seminomatous tumors such as choriocarcinoma. Lumbar vertebral bone marrow is the classic metastatic site of prostate cancer. Hepatic parenchyma is a hematogenous site for non-seminomatous tumors. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 40, lymphatic spread of seminoma); Robbins Ch. 21 (seminomas spread mainly to paraaortic nodes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma is radiosensitive, spreads late to para-aortic nodes, and does not make AFP. COMLEX: a testicular mass is removed by radical inguinal orchiectomy, not biopsied through the scrotum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 5 — seminoma versus non-seminomatous germ cell tumors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl40_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has a left testicular mass. Radical orchiectomy shows only mature cartilage, intestinal-type glands, and neural tissue, with no other germ cell elements. Serum AFP and beta-hCG are normal. Which statement best describes this tumor's behavior?","choices":{"A":"Benign and cured by excision","B":"Benign unless AFP is raised","C":"Radiosensitive like seminoma","D":"Malignant with metastatic risk","E":"Malignant in prepubertal boys"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tissues from more than one germ layer define a teratoma. In postpubertal men, teratoma arises from germ cell neoplasia in situ, is malignant, and can metastasize even when it looks mature. Benign and cured by excision describes prepubertal teratoma in children. Benign unless AFP is raised is wrong, because AFP reflects yolk sac elements, not teratoma behavior. Radiosensitive like seminoma is wrong, because non-seminomatous tumors are radioresistant. Malignant in prepubertal boys reverses the age pattern. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 40, prepubertal vs postpubertal teratoma); Robbins Ch. 21 (teratoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teratoma is benign in prepubertal children but malignant in postpubertal men, and it can undergo somatic-type malignant transformation. COMLEX: normal markers do not exclude a malignant germ cell tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 5 — seminoma, yolk sac tumor, and choriocarcinoma markers","isHighYield":false}],"41":[{"id":"sdl41_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has nocturia, hesitancy, and a weak urinary stream. Rectal examination shows a smooth, rubbery, symmetrically enlarged, nontender prostate, and serum PSA is mildly elevated. In which region of the prostate did his condition most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Peripheral zone","B":"Central zone","C":"Fibromuscular zone","D":"Transition zone","E":"Bladder trigone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins divides the prostate into peripheral, central, transition, and periurethral zones. Most hyperplasias arise in the transition zone, and the resulting nodules compress the urethra into a slit-like orifice and produce urinary obstruction. Most carcinomas instead originate in the peripheral zone. The bladder trigone is part of the bladder, not one of the prostatic zones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPH is hyperplasia, not hypertrophy, of the transition zone, and it is not premalignant. COMLEX: the transition zone lies around the urethra, so enlargement causes obstructive voiding symptoms rather than a palpable posterior nodule.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — inflammatory and hyperplastic disorders of the prostate arise from distinct mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"In benign prostatic hyperplasia, which cell population expresses the enzyme that generates dihydrotestosterone from testosterone in the prostate?","choices":{"A":"Prostatic secretory cells","B":"Prostatic basal cells","C":"Prostatic stromal cells","D":"Ejaculatory duct cells","E":"Prostatic neuroendocrine cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DHT is the main androgen in the prostate and is formed from testosterone by type 2 5-alpha-reductase. Robbins states that this enzyme is expressed primarily in stromal cells and is not expressed in prostatic epithelial cells. DHT then binds androgen receptors in stromal and epithelial cells and upregulates growth factors such as FGF and TGF-beta.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors such as finasteride shrink the gland by decreasing DHT synthesis. COMLEX: the stromal location of the enzyme explains why DHT signaling drives stromal proliferation in BPH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — inflammatory and hyperplastic disorders of the prostate arise from distinct mechanisms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins states that this enzym","B":"Robbins states that this enzym"}},{"id":"sdl41_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which form of prostatitis occurs most commonly?","choices":{"A":"Acute prostatitis with fever and chills","B":"Chronic prostatitis with negative cultures","C":"Chronic prostatitis with recurrent same-organism UTIs","D":"Granulomatous prostatitis after BCG instillation","E":"Fungal granulomatous prostatitis in immunocompromised hosts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic abacterial prostatitis, often called chronic pelvic pain syndrome, is the most common form. It resembles chronic bacterial prostatitis in symptoms, but there is no history of recurrent urinary tract infection, expressed prostatic secretions contain more than 10 leukocytes per high-power field, and cultures are uniformly negative. The other forms are less frequent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostatitis presents with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and low back pain, and chronic prostatitis may be bacterial or nonbacterial. COMLEX: expressed prostatic secretions with leukocytes but sterile cultures point to the abacterial form.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — inflammatory and hyperplastic disorders of the prostate arise from distinct mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature is present in benign prostatic glands but missing from malignant glands?","choices":{"A":"Prominent nucleoli","B":"Perineural invasion","C":"Tightly packed cells","D":"Basal cell layer","E":"Amphophilic cytoplasm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Benign prostatic glands have a basal cell layer beneath the secretory layer, and this layer is absent in adenocarcinoma. Immunohistochemical markers that stain basal cells can bring out the distinction. Malignant glands are also smaller, lined by a single uniform layer, tightly packed, without branching or papillary infoldings, and have enlarged nuclei with large nucleoli. Perineural invasion is a finding specific to carcinoma, and amphophilic cytoplasm is described in tumor cells, so neither is a benign feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of the basal cell layer is the key histologic marker of invasive prostate cancer, and AMACR is upregulated in most cases. COMLEX: high-grade PIN retains at least a partial basal layer and an intact basement membrane.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — benign hyperplasia, adenocarcinoma, and its precursor lesions are separated by histologic and molecular features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which alteration is the most common genetic event in prostate cancer?","choices":{"A":"BCR-ABL1 gene fusion","B":"COL1A1-PDGFB gene fusion","C":"TMPRSS2-ERG gene fusion","D":"PAX3-FOXO1 gene fusion","E":"EWSR1-FLI1 gene fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common genetic alteration in prostate cancer is a chromosomal rearrangement that places an ETS family gene, most commonly ERG or ETV1, next to the androgen-regulated TMPRSS2 promoter. This occurs in about half of prostate cancers and causes androgen-dependent overexpression of the ETS factor. The other fusions belong to other neoplasms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the androgen-regulated promoter explains why androgen deprivation therapy lowers expression of the driver. COMLEX: a firm posterior nodule on rectal examination or a raised PSA prompts needle biopsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — benign hyperplasia, adenocarcinoma, and its precursor lesions are separated by histologic and molecular features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which lesion is considered a precursor of prostatic adenocarcinoma and shares its predominance in the peripheral zone?","choices":{"A":"High-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia","B":"Nodular hyperplasia of the periurethral and transition zones","C":"Nonspecific granulomatous prostatitis from ruptured ducts","D":"Prostatic infarct with adjacent squamous metaplasia","E":"Chronic bacterial prostatitis with leukocytes in secretions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"High-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia (HGPIN) is the proposed precursor. Both HGPIN and cancer predominate in the peripheral zone, HGPIN is found in about 80 percent of prostates removed for carcinoma, and many molecular changes of invasive cancer are also present in it. HGPIN glands retain at least a partial basal cell layer and have an intact basement membrane, unlike malignant glands. Nodular hyperplasia arises in the transition zone and is not a premalignant lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PIN is architecturally benign acini lined by atypical cells with prominent nucleoli. COMLEX: BPH is not premalignant, which distinguishes it from HGPIN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — benign hyperplasia, adenocarcinoma, and its precursor lesions are separated by histologic and molecular features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, how is the combined Gleason score derived for a prostate tumor showing two distinct architectural arrangements of glands?","choices":{"A":"Highest grade seen in any single core","B":"Average of the pattern grades across cores","C":"Product of primary and secondary grades","D":"Fraction of tumor lacking gland formation","E":"Sum of primary and secondary pattern grades"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most tumors contain more than one pattern. A primary grade is assigned to the dominant pattern and a secondary grade to the second most frequent pattern, and the two numeric grades are added to give the combined Gleason score. A tumor with a single pattern is treated as having identical primary and secondary grades, so the number is doubled, and, according to Robbins, the score therefore ranges from 2 to 10.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gleason grade is based on glandular architecture and correlates closely with metastatic potential. COMLEX: scores are now collapsed into five grade groups that each carry a different prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Gleason grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors, and prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to bone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":1,"stem":"Which growth pattern characterizes the highest Gleason grade, grade 5?","choices":{"A":"Solid sheets and nests of infiltrating cells","B":"Uniform round glands in well-circumscribed nodules","C":"Individual discrete well-formed glands","D":"Branching glands with papillary infoldings","E":"Well-formed glands with lesser fused components"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In grade 5 tumors the cells infiltrate the stroma in cords, sheets, and solid nests, with no identifiable gland formation. Grade 1 is the opposite extreme, with uniform round glands packed into well-circumscribed nodules. Branching glands with papillary infoldings are a feature of benign glands, and the other options describe well-formed or mixed gland patterns of lower grade groups.","boardPrep":"Step 1: higher grade means loss of gland formation and worse prognosis. COMLEX: Gleason scores of 8 through 10 tend to be advanced cancers that are less likely to be cured.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Gleason grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors, and prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to bone","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Branching glands with papillar","E":"Branching glands with papillar"}},{"id":"sdl41_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which skeletal site is most frequently involved by metastatic prostatic carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Proximal femur","B":"Lumbar spine","C":"Thoracic spine","D":"Pelvic bones","E":"Rib cage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hematogenous spread of prostate cancer occurs chiefly to bone, particularly the axial skeleton, and the metastases are typically osteoblastic. Robbins lists the most commonly involved bones in descending order as lumbar spine, proximal femur, pelvis, thoracic spine, and ribs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoblastic metastases in a male strongly suggest a prostatic primary, and spinal spread is often through the Batson vertebral venous plexus. COMLEX: low back pain with raised alkaline phosphatase and PSA suggests skeletal involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Gleason grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors, and prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to bone","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins lists the most commonl","C":"Robbins lists the most commonl","D":"Robbins lists the most commonl","E":"Robbins lists the most commonl"}},{"id":"sdl41_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has had 2 days of fever, chills, and dysuria. One week ago he underwent cystoscopy. His temperature is 39.2 C. Rectal examination shows an exquisitely tender, boggy prostate. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to establish the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Prostate needle biopsy","B":"Serum PSA level","C":"Repeat cystoscopy","D":"Urine culture","E":"Semen analysis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fever, chills, dysuria, and an exquisitely tender, boggy prostate after urethral instrumentation indicate acute bacterial prostatitis, usually from E. coli, other gram-negative rods, enterococci, or staphylococci reaching the gland by reflux of contaminated urine. Robbins states the diagnosis is established by urine culture and clinical features. Biopsy of a gland with suspected acute prostatitis is contraindicated because it may lead to sepsis, and PSA is also raised by prostatitis so it does not confirm the cause. Repeat cystoscopy is the kind of instrumentation that seeds the infection, and semen analysis does not identify the organism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. coli is the most common cause of acute bacterial prostatitis in older men, and in young men consider Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae. COMLEX: avoid needle biopsy and vigorous manipulation of an acutely inflamed prostate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — ascending infection of the lower urinary tract through reflux or stasis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has nocturia, hesitancy, and a weak urinary stream. Rectal examination shows a smoothly enlarged, firm, nontender prostate. After 6 months of a new medication, his symptoms improve and ultrasonography shows a decrease in prostate volume. Which mechanism best explains the reduction in gland size?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of prostatic smooth muscle by alpha-1 blockade","B":"Blockade of androgen receptors in prostatic epithelium","C":"Blockade of estrogen receptors on prostatic stromal cells","D":"Inhibition of bacterial growth within residual urine","E":"Blocked testosterone conversion to dihydrotestosterone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The presentation is benign prostatic hyperplasia, in which DHT-driven growth factors increase stromal cell proliferation and decrease epithelial cell death. Robbins notes that 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors physically shrink the prostate by decreasing DHT synthesis. Alpha-adrenergic blockers relieve symptoms by lowering prostatic smooth muscle tone but do not reduce gland volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase, and alpha-1 antagonists such as tamsulosin relax smooth muscle. COMLEX: BPH is not premalignant, and residual urine predisposes to infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride actions on DHT and PSA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has had nocturia, difficulty starting his stream, and overflow dribbling for several years, with no episodes of acute retention. Ultrasonography shows a large post-void residual volume and a thickened bladder wall. Which change is most likely present in his bladder wall?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy with trabeculation","B":"Thinning of the muscular wall with smooth mucosa","C":"Transmural fibrosis producing a contracted bladder","D":"Yellow mucosal plaques of foamy macrophages","E":"Multifocal keratinizing squamous metaplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increased resistance to urinary outflow from prostatic enlargement leads to bladder wall smooth muscle hypertrophy and distention with retained urine. As hypertrophy progresses the muscle bundles enlarge and produce trabeculation, and over time crypts form and may become diverticula. Marked thinning of the wall without trabeculae is described in some cases of acute obstruction or terminal disease, when the bladder becomes extremely dilated. The residual urine is a common site of infection. Yellow plaques of foamy macrophages are malakoplakia, and keratinizing squamous metaplasia is a response to chronic injury such as schistosomiasis, not to outflow obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPH can lead to bladder hypertrophy, hydronephrosis, and urinary tract infections. COMLEX: sudden acute urinary retention in BPH requires emergency catheterization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — obstruction with dilation and progressive parenchymal atrophy of the upper urinary tract","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has an elevated PSA and a firm posterior prostatic nodule. On needle biopsy, a small group of crowded glands is admixed with benign glands, and the pathologist is cautious because benign mimics of cancer exist. Which finding is one of the few that is by itself specific for carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Enlarged nuclei containing prominent nucleoli","B":"Glands lined by two distinct layers of cells","C":"Nodules of glands with surrounding stromal growth","D":"Atypical cells confined within preexisting ducts","E":"Malignant glands wrapping around a nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins notes that the diagnosis of prostate cancer on needle biopsy is usually made from a constellation of architectural, cytologic, and ancillary findings, but a few findings are specific, such as perineural invasion. Enlarged nuclei with prominent nucleoli are typical of carcinoma, but atypical cells in high-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia may look cytologically identical, so they are not specific. Glands with two cell layers, an inner secretory layer and an outer basal layer, are benign. Nodules of glands with stromal proliferation describe benign prostatic hyperplasia. Atypical cells within preexisting ducts and acini are high-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia, a precursor rather than invasive carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prostate adenocarcinoma: small crowded glands with a single cell layer (no basal cells; p63 negative, AMACR positive), enlarged nuclei with prominent nucleoli; perineural invasion is specific. COMLEX: Posterior peripheral zone tumors are palpable on digital rectal examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Gleason grading of the infiltrating gland patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a transition zone nodule removed during transurethral resection. Histology shows nodules of glands lined by an inner columnar layer and an outer flattened basal layer, with papillary infoldings, separated by bland spindle-shaped stromal cells. Which statement about this lesion is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"It usually arises in the peripheral zone","B":"It commonly evolves into high-grade PIN","C":"It carries no premalignant potential","D":"It is defined by absence of basal cells","E":"It is driven by loss of androgen signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The two-layered glands with papillary infoldings and stromal proliferation in the transition zone are benign prostatic hyperplasia, which Robbins states is not a premalignant lesion. It is driven by DHT signaling through androgen receptors, and it arises in the transition zone rather than the peripheral zone. The basal cell layer is retained, whereas its absence marks adenocarcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPH is hyperplasia of the transition zone and is not premalignant. COMLEX: PSA is organ specific but not cancer specific, and BPH, prostatitis, infarction, instrumentation, and ejaculation can all raise it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — DHT dependence of benign prostatic hyperplasia and its pharmacologic inhibition with finasteride","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with metastatic prostate adenocarcinoma responded to androgen deprivation therapy, but 2 years later his PSA is rising again while his serum testosterone remains at castrate levels. Sequencing of a new metastasis shows many extra copies of the androgen receptor gene. How does this change allow the tumor to keep growing?","choices":{"A":"TMPRSS2-ERG fusion raising androgen-driven ETS output","B":"Epigenetic silencing of the GSTP1 detoxifying gene","C":"Germline BRCA2 loss that impairs DNA break repair","D":"Hypersensitivity to the low residual androgen levels","E":"Inactivation of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Prostate cancer cells depend on androgens acting through the androgen receptor, which is why castration usually induces regression, but most tumors eventually escape; Robbins lists acquisition of hypersensitivity to low androgen levels, for example through androgen receptor gene amplification, among the escape mechanisms, along with ligand-independent receptor splice variants, receptor mutations, and bypass pathways. The TMPRSS2-ERG fusion makes ETS expression androgen-dependent, so it explains dependence rather than escape. GSTP1 silencing is a frequent early epigenetic event that may enhance carcinogen genotoxicity. Germline BRCA2 loss raises the risk of early, aggressive disease but does not explain growth at castrate androgen levels. Losing 5-alpha-reductase would lower DHT and reduce, not restore, androgen signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prostate cancer genetics: TMPRSS2-ETS fusion in about half (androgen-dependent ETS expression), p27 silencing, MYC amplification, PTEN deletion, GSTP1 methylation early, and TP53 and RB loss and AR amplification late; germline risk from BRCA2, mismatch repair genes, and HOXB13. COMLEX: A rising PSA despite castrate testosterone signals castration-resistant disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling in target cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a PSA of 9.8 ng/mL (reference range: 0–4.0 ng/mL) and a firm nodule on digital rectal examination. Needle biopsy shows adenocarcinoma composed of predominantly poorly formed, fused, and cribriform glands with a lesser component of well-formed glands. The dominant pattern is grade 4 and the second most frequent pattern is grade 3. Which Gleason score and Grade Group apply?","choices":{"A":"Score 7, Grade Group 2","B":"Score 7, Grade Group 3","C":"Score 8, Grade Group 3","D":"Score 6, Grade Group 1","E":"Score 8, Grade Group 2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The primary grade of 4 and secondary grade of 3 add to a Gleason score of 7. In Robbins Table 21.7 a 4 plus 3 pattern, with predominantly poorly formed, fused, or cribriform glands and a lesser component of well-formed glands, is Grade Group 3. A 3 plus 4 tumor, with predominantly well-formed glands, is Grade Group 2, and a score of 6 or lower is Grade Group 1. A score of 8, reached by 4 plus 4, 3 plus 5, or 5 plus 3, is Grade Group 4, so neither score 8 option is correct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the primary pattern is the dominant one, so 4 plus 3 is worse than 3 plus 4 despite the same total. COMLEX: grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors and are combined to guide management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Gleason patterns are combined into grade groups that carry different prognoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has progressive low back pain. Serum alkaline phosphatase and PSA are elevated, and radiographs show dense sclerotic lesions in several lumbar spinal segments. Which route of spread best explains the pattern of spinal involvement?","choices":{"A":"Obturator and paraaortic lymphatics","B":"Hepatic portal venous drainage","C":"Batson vertebral venous plexus","D":"Direct extension into seminal vesicles","E":"Thoracic duct lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to the axial skeleton, and the metastases are typically osteoblastic, which in a male points strongly to a prostatic origin. First Aid notes that metastasis to the spine often occurs through the Batson vertebral venous plexus. Lymphatic spread goes to obturator and then paraaortic nodes, and local extension involves periprostatic tissue, seminal vesicles, and the bladder base, but none of these routes accounts for vertebral bone lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoblastic bone metastases in a man suggest prostate cancer, and lumbar spine is the most commonly involved site. COMLEX: low back pain with raised alkaline phosphatase and PSA should prompt evaluation for skeletal metastases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — hematogenous spread of prostate cancer to the axial skeleton with osteoblastic lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with metastatic prostate adenocarcinoma is started on chronic leuprolide. Which mechanism explains its therapeutic effect?","choices":{"A":"Pituitary desensitization suppresses LH and testosterone","B":"Direct inhibition of 5-alpha-reductase in prostate stromal cells","C":"Competitive blockade of androgen receptors on tumor cells","D":"Inhibition of adrenal androgen synthesis by cytochrome blockade","E":"Enhanced inhibin B release that lowers pituitary FSH"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Metastatic prostate cancer is treated with androgen deprivation, by orchiectomy or by LHRH analogs. Chronic administration of an LHRH agonist desensitizes pituitary cells expressing LHRH receptors, suppressing release of LH, which is required for testosterone production by Leydig cells. Tumor regression follows because prostate cancer cell growth and survival depend on androgens, although most tumors eventually become resistant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH stimulates the pituitary whereas continuous GnRH agonist exposure downregulates its receptors. COMLEX: androgen deprivation induces remission, and resistance to testosterone withdrawal heralds disease progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — pulsatile versus continuous GnRH stimulation and pituitary receptor down-regulation by agonists such as leuprolide in prostate cancer therapy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl41_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has fever, chills, dysuria, and perineal pain. His temperature is 39.2 °C, and on rectal examination the gland is exquisitely tender and boggy. Urinalysis shows pyuria and bacteria. Which step should be avoided during this illness?","choices":{"A":"Midstream urine culture","B":"Peripheral blood cultures","C":"Complete blood count","D":"Prostate needle biopsy","E":"Empiric antibiotic therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fever, perineal pain, and a tender, boggy prostate indicate acute bacterial prostatitis, usually from coliform bacteria refluxing from the urethra or bladder. Needle biopsy during acute infection can seed the bloodstream and cause bacteremia and septic shock, so it is avoided. A midstream urine culture identifies the organism. Peripheral blood cultures are reasonable in a febrile patient. A complete blood count helps assess systemic infection. Empiric antibiotic therapy is the treatment. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 41, acute bacterial prostatitis and biopsy contraindication); Robbins Ch. 21 (prostatitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute bacterial prostatitis is usually E. coli from urine reflux, with a tender, boggy prostate. COMLEX: avoid biopsy during acute infection because of bacteremia risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 1 — E. coli also causes ascending bacterial cystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl41_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has had three urinary tract infections in a year, each with the same Escherichia coli strain. Between episodes he has low back and perineal pain and no catheter history. Expressed prostatic secretions show 25 leukocytes per high-power field (reference range: 10 or fewer) and grow E. coli. Which factor best explains his relapses?","choices":{"A":"Plasmid-mediated antibiotic resistance","B":"Poor antibiotic penetration of prostate","C":"Reinfection from a sexual partner","D":"Vesicoureteral reflux into the kidney","E":"Biofilm on an indwelling catheter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Relapsing infection with the same organism and positive cultures of prostatic secretions indicates chronic bacterial prostatitis. Most systemic antibiotics penetrate the prostate poorly, so bacteria persist in the gland and reseed the urinary tract. Plasmid-mediated resistance would cause failure during treatment, whereas the lecture attributes relapse to a prostatic reservoir. Reinfection from a partner does not explain why prostatic secretions keep growing the same strain. Vesicoureteral reflux would cause upper tract infection, not prostatic leukocytosis. Biofilm on a catheter is excluded by his lack of catheter use. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 41, chronic bacterial prostatitis); Robbins Ch. 21 (prostatitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic bacterial prostatitis means recurrent UTIs with the same organism and positive expressed-secretion cultures; chronic pelvic pain syndrome has leukocytes but negative cultures. COMLEX: prolonged antibiotic courses are needed because drug penetration into the prostate is poor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — BCG is the most common cause of granulomatous prostatitis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl41_q3","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has nocturia and a weak stream, and his prostate is smooth, rubbery, and enlarged. Researchers studying his transition-zone tissue find that dihydrotestosterone acts first on stromal cells. Which paracrine step most directly drives epithelial proliferation?","choices":{"A":"Stromal FGF stimulating epithelial growth","B":"Epithelial TGF-beta driving fibrosis","C":"Stromal VEGF driving gland angiogenesis","D":"Epithelial IGF-2 driving stromal growth","E":"Stromal ERG fusion activating epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In benign prostatic hyperplasia, DHT made by type 2 5-alpha-reductase binds androgen receptors in stromal cells. The stromal cells then secrete fibroblast growth factors such as FGF-2 and keratinocyte growth factor, which stimulate epithelial proliferation. Epithelial TGF-beta driving fibrosis reverses the direction; the lecture describes stromal TGF-beta as a fibroblast mitogen that also regulates epithelial apoptosis. Stromal VEGF is not the paracrine driver described. Epithelial IGF-2 is linked to Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, not BPH. ERG fusion is a feature of prostate cancer, not hyperplasia. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 41, stromal-epithelial crosstalk in BPH); Robbins Ch. 21 (benign prostatic hyperplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in BPH, DHT acts on stromal cells, which release FGFs that drive epithelial growth in the transition zone. COMLEX: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors shrink the gland by removing this DHT stimulus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride lower intraprostatic DHT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl41_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a PSA of 9.8 ng/mL (reference range: below 4.0 ng/mL). Needle biopsy shows small, crowded glands with enlarged nuclei and prominent nucleoli, and staining for p63 is negative around them. Which additional immunostain finding best supports carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Positive AMACR in gland cells","B":"Positive HMW keratin in basal cells","C":"Positive p63 in basal cells","D":"Positive PSA in stromal cells","E":"Negative PSA in glandular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Small crowded glands with prominent nucleoli and absent p63 staining suggest adenocarcinoma, and AMACR (alpha-methylacyl-CoA racemase) is upregulated in most prostatic adenocarcinomas, serving as a positive marker alongside basal cell loss. Positive p63 in basal cells would indicate a benign gland or HGPIN, and the glands here are p63-negative. Positive high-molecular-weight cytokeratin in basal cells would likewise mark a retained basal layer. Positive PSA in stromal cells is not a recognized finding. Negative PSA in glandular cells does not support prostatic carcinoma. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 41, AMACR and basal cell markers); Robbins Ch. 21 (AMACR in prostatic carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostate cancer lacks a basal layer (negative p63 and HMWK) and is AMACR-positive. COMLEX: HGPIN keeps its basal layer, which separates it from invasive carcinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Gleason grading uses gland architecture, not nuclear atypia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Positive high-molecular-weight"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl41_q5","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a firm, gritty nodule felt on digital rectal examination, and his PSA is 7.2 ng/mL (reference range: below 4.0 ng/mL). Biopsy confirms adenocarcinoma. In which prostatic region did this tumor most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Periurethral transition zone","B":"Central zone near ducts","C":"Anterior fibromuscular stroma","D":"Posterior peripheral zone","E":"Bladder neck muscle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 70% of prostatic adenocarcinomas arise in the outer peripheral zone, classically posteriorly, which is why early tumors are palpable as firm nodules on rectal examination. The periurethral transition zone is the site of benign prostatic hyperplasia. The central zone near the ejaculatory ducts is not the usual origin. The anterior fibromuscular stroma is not reachable by rectal examination and is not the typical site. Bladder neck muscle is not prostatic glandular tissue. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 41, anatomic localization of adenocarcinoma vs BPH); Robbins Ch. 21 (prostatic carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cancer arises in the posterior peripheral zone and BPH in the transition zone. COMLEX: because BPH is central, it compresses the urethra and causes symptoms early, whereas peripheral cancer often does not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — BPH arises in the transition zone and compresses the urethra","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl41_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer has low back pain. Radiographs show dense sclerotic foci in the lumbar vertebrae, and his alkaline phosphatase is 420 U/L (reference range: 40–129 U/L). Which tumor-derived signal most directly produces these lesions?","choices":{"A":"RANKL activating osteoclasts","B":"PTHrP-driven bone resorption","C":"Endothelin-1 stimulating osteoblasts","D":"Osteoprotegerin blocking osteoblasts","E":"IL-6 recruiting osteoclast precursors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prostate cancer metastases are characteristically osteoblastic. Tumor cells in the marrow secrete endothelin-1, bone morphogenetic proteins, FGFs, and TGF-beta, which stimulate osteoblasts to lay down dense sclerotic bone, raising alkaline phosphatase. RANKL activating osteoclasts produces lytic, not sclerotic, lesions. PTHrP-driven bone resorption also produces lysis and hypercalcemia. IL-6 recruiting osteoclast precursors is a lytic pathway, as in myeloma. Osteoprotegerin blocking osteoblasts is wrong, because osteoprotegerin is a RANKL decoy that inhibits osteoclasts. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 41, osteoblast stimulation cascade); Robbins Ch. 21 (osteoblastic bony metastases).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostate bone metastases are osteoblastic, spread through the valveless Batson plexus, and favor the lumbar spine. COMLEX: new back pain in an older man with prostate cancer suggests vertebral metastasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Batson plexus spread explains the lumbar vertebral predilection","isHighYield":false}],"42":[{"id":"sdl42_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in infants and children?","choices":{"A":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","B":"Bifid ureter with duplicated pelvis","C":"Obstructing calcium oxalate calculi","D":"Ureteral diverticulum formation","E":"Extrinsic compression by a pelvic tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in infants and children. Cases presenting early in life preferentially affect males and are bilateral in 20 percent of cases. Ureteral duplication and diverticula are congenital anomalies that are often of little clinical significance, and calculi and pelvic tumors are causes of obstruction in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UPJ obstruction is usually attributed to abnormal organization of smooth muscle bundles or excess collagen at the junction. COMLEX: in adults, UPJ obstruction is more common in females and is most often unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 1 — obstructive ureteral lesions and inflammatory bladder lesions produce distinct clinical and morphologic pictures","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":1,"stem":"Which ureteral disorder features storiform scar tissue encasing the ureters, abundant IgG4-positive plasma cells, and an initial response to corticosteroids?","choices":{"A":"Ureteral duplication with reflux","B":"Ureteral urothelial carcinoma","C":"Congenital ureteral diverticulum","D":"Sclerosing retroperitoneal fibrosis","E":"Obstructing ureteral calculus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sclerosing retroperitoneal fibrosis is a fibrotic proliferative inflammatory process that encases retroperitoneal structures and causes hydronephrosis. Microscopy shows storiform fibrous tissue with lymphocytes, plasma cells that are frequently IgG4-positive, and eosinophils. At least a subset is related to IgG4-related disease, initial treatment is corticosteroids, and resistant cases require stents or ureterolysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: retroperitoneal fibrosis is a cause of hydronephrosis, and drug exposures such as ergot derivatives and beta-blockers are among its recognized associations. COMLEX: it occurs in middle to late age and is more common in males.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 1 — obstructive ureteral lesions and inflammatory bladder lesions produce distinct clinical and morphologic pictures","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":1,"stem":"In a woman with suspected interstitial cystitis (bladder pain syndrome), what is the main role of a bladder biopsy?","choices":{"A":"To confirm increased submucosal mast cells","B":"To exclude urothelial carcinoma in situ","C":"To identify the causative bacterial organism","D":"To measure the depth of the Hunner ulcers","E":"To detect metaplasia within von Brunn nests"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Interstitial cystitis is a disorder of unknown etiology, most frequent in women, with intermittent severe suprapubic pain, frequency, urgency, hematuria, and dysuria for more than 6 weeks without infection or another identifiable cause; its microscopic findings are nonspecific, and Robbins states that the main role of biopsy is to rule out carcinoma in situ, which clinically mimics it. Submucosal mast cells are often increased, but the finding is nonspecific and its significance uncertain. By definition no infection is present. Hunner ulcers mark the late ulcerative phase, but biopsy is not done to measure them. Von Brunn nests with metaplasia describe cystitis cystica et glandularis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Interstitial cystitis: women, suprapubic pain with urgency and frequency, cystoscopic fissures and punctate hemorrhages (glomerulations), Hunner ulcers in the late phase, possible contracted bladder from transmural fibrosis. COMLEX: Exclude carcinoma in situ before treating bladder pain empirically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — urothelial carcinoma in situ, the precursor of invasive bladder cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"By definition no infection is "}},{"id":"sdl42_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":1,"stem":"Which molecular changes characterize non-muscle-invasive papillary urothelial carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Inactivating TP53 and RB mutations","B":"MET proto-oncogene activation","C":"FGFR3 and RAS gain of function","D":"VHL loss with stabilized HIF-1 alpha","E":"TMPRSS2-ERG gene rearrangement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Non-muscle-invasive papillary cancers often have gain-of-function alterations that increase growth factor receptor signaling, such as amplification of the FGFR3 gene and activating mutations of RAS and PI 3-kinase. These tumors frequently recur but progress to muscle-invasive cancer in only about 20 percent of cases. TP53 and RB inactivation characterizes the flat CIS pathway to muscle-invasive disease. VHL loss with HIF stabilization belongs to clear cell renal cell carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary bladder tumors follow the FGFR3 and RAS pathway, whereas flat CIS and muscle-invasive tumors follow the TP53 and RB pathway. COMLEX: FGFR3 is also a target for antibody-drug conjugates in advanced disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — urothelial carcinoma behavior is defined by its molecular pathway, precursor lesion, and depth of invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":1,"stem":"From which lesion do most muscle-invasive bladder cancers develop?","choices":{"A":"Papillary urothelial hyperplasia","B":"Urothelial carcinoma in situ","C":"Exophytic urothelial papilloma","D":"Inverted urothelial papilloma","E":"Cystitis cystica et glandularis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most muscle-invasive bladder cancers develop by progression from flat urothelial carcinoma in situ (CIS), and TP53 and RB mutations occur early in CIS. Papillary urothelial hyperplasia is the origin of the noninvasive papillary tumors, which more rarely progress. Inverted papillomas are completely benign, and cystitis cystica et glandularis is a metaplastic lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CIS is cytologically malignant but confined to the epithelium, with no basement membrane invasion. COMLEX: if untreated, 50 percent to 75 percent of CIS progresses to invasive cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — urothelial carcinoma behavior is defined by its molecular pathway, precursor lesion, and depth of invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":1,"stem":"A bladder tumor is staged T2. Which layer has it invaded?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis propria","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Perivesical soft tissue","D":"Prostatic stroma","E":"Pelvic wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the AJCC staging system T2 means invasion of the muscularis propria, the detrusor muscle. T1 is invasion of the lamina propria, T3 is invasion of perivesical soft tissue, and T4 is extravesical invasion into structures such as prostatic stroma, seminal vesicles, uterus, vagina, or the pelvic wall. Invasion of the muscularis propria is the point at which survival falls most sharply, with a 30 percent 5-year mortality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: depth of invasion is the most important prognostic factor in bladder cancer. COMLEX: muscularis propria invasion is an indication for radical cystectomy or radiation therapy with chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — urothelial carcinoma behavior is defined by its molecular pathway, precursor lesion, and depth of invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which organism, endemic in Egypt and Sudan, is an established risk factor for bladder cancer?","choices":{"A":"Wuchereria bancrofti","B":"Onchocerca volvulus","C":"Enterobius vermicularis","D":"Entamoeba histolytica","E":"Schistosoma haematobium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Schistosoma haematobium infection in endemic areas such as Egypt and Sudan is an established risk factor for bladder cancer. The ova are deposited in the bladder wall and incite a chronic inflammatory response that induces progressive mucosal squamous metaplasia, dysplasia, and in some instances neoplasia. The other organisms are not associated with bladder cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic cystitis and Schistosoma haematobium are among the risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder, along with smoking and chronic stones. COMLEX: painless hematuria in a patient from an endemic region warrants cystoscopic evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — different environmental exposures predispose the bladder to distinct histologic types of carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which histologic type accounts for about 70 percent of bladder cancers associated with Schistosoma haematobium?","choices":{"A":"Invasive urothelial carcinoma","B":"Adenocarcinoma of the bladder","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma","D":"Small-cell carcinoma","E":"Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In schistosomal bladder cancer, chronic inflammation drives squamous metaplasia and dysplasia, and 70 percent of the cancers are squamous. The remainder are urothelial or glandular, and glandular tumors are the least common type. Squamous cell carcinoma makes up only 3 percent to 7 percent of bladder cancers in the United States, but it is much more frequent where urinary schistosomiasis is endemic. Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is the bladder sarcoma of infants and children, not a schistosomal carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schistosomiasis of the bladder produces squamous metaplasia that progresses to squamous cell carcinoma. COMLEX: pure squamous carcinomas are nearly always associated with chronic bladder irritation and infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — different environmental exposures predispose the bladder to distinct histologic types of carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The remainder are urothelial o"}},{"id":"sdl42_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which exposure is the most important risk factor for urothelial carcinoma of the bladder?","choices":{"A":"Cyclophosphamide therapy","B":"Chronic analgesic use","C":"Pelvic irradiation","D":"Cigarette smoking","E":"Aryl amine exposure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cigarette smoking is clearly the most important influence, increasing risk three-fold to seven-fold, and 50 percent to 80 percent of bladder cancers in males are associated with cigarettes. Industrial exposure to aryl amines such as 2-naphthylamine, heavy long-term cyclophosphamide, long-term analgesic use, and pelvic irradiation are also recognized risk factors but contribute less.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the risk factors for urothelial carcinoma are tobacco smoking, aromatic amines in dyes, and cyclophosphamide. COMLEX: painless hematuria is the most common presenting symptom of bladder cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — different environmental exposures predispose the bladder to distinct histologic types of carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-month-old boy has a prenatal history of renal pelvic dilation. Ultrasonography now shows marked dilation of the right renal pelvis and calyces with a normal-caliber ureter and a normal bladder. Which mechanism is the most likely cause of the obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous tissue encasing the retroperitoneal ureter","B":"Calculus lodged at the ureterovesical junction","C":"Neurogenic interruption of bladder innervation","D":"Disorganized smooth muscle at the ureteropelvic junction","E":"Papillary carcinoma growing within the ureteral lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in infants and children, and early presentation preferentially affects males. It is most often ascribed to abnormal organization of smooth muscle bundles or excess collagen deposition between them, and only rarely to extrinsic compression by abnormal renal vessels. Dilation limited to the right pelvis and calyces with a normal ureter and bladder localizes the obstruction to the junction, whereas retroperitoneal fibrosis and ureteral carcinoma are adult disorders and a distal calculus or neurogenic bladder would dilate the ureter or bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UPJ obstruction is a congenital cause of hydronephrosis that may be bilateral in 20 percent of early cases. COMLEX: contralateral renal agenesis and other anomalies can coexist, so evaluate the other kidney.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — mechanical progression of hydronephrosis with calyceal dilation and parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dilation limited to the right ","E":"Dilation limited to the right "}},{"id":"sdl42_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman who received a renal transplant has recurrent E. coli urinary tract infections. Cystoscopy shows soft yellow raised mucosal plaques. Biopsy shows aggregates of large foamy histiocytes containing laminated calcified inclusions. Which mechanism best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Mast cell accumulation in the bladder submucosa","B":"Downward growth of von Brunn nests into lamina propria","C":"Defective phagolysosome function in macrophages","D":"Lymphoid follicle formation within the bladder mucosa","E":"Implantation of renal tubular cells at eroded mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The yellow plaques and von Hansemann histiocytes with laminated calcified Michaelis-Gutmann bodies are diagnostic of malakoplakia. It arises in chronic bacterial infection, mostly by E. coli, and is more frequent with immunosuppression such as in renal transplant recipients. It appears to stem from acquired defects in phagocyte function, and the macrophages accumulate bacterial debris because of defective phagolysosome function. The other options describe interstitial cystitis, cystitis cystica, follicular cystitis, and nephrogenic adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: malakoplakia shows Michaelis-Gutmann bodies, which are calcified concretions in enlarged lysosomes, and von Hansemann histiocytes. COMLEX: it can mimic a bladder tumor clinically, so biopsy is needed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — chronic bacterial infection of the urinary tract with E. coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other options describe int","B":"The other options describe int","E":"The other options describe int"}},{"id":"sdl42_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with lupus nephritis is receiving high-dose cyclophosphamide and develops gross hematuria, frequency, and dysuria. Urine culture is negative and cystoscopy shows hyperemic, bleeding mucosa. With heavy long-term exposure to this drug, which neoplasm is she at increased risk of developing?","choices":{"A":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","B":"Bladder urothelial carcinoma","C":"Prostatic adenocarcinoma","D":"Penile squamous cell carcinoma","E":"Testicular germ cell tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cytotoxic agents such as cyclophosphamide may cause hemorrhagic cystitis, a noninfectious iatrogenic cystitis, and the negative culture supports this. Robbins states that heavy long-term exposure to cyclophosphamide induces hemorrhagic cystitis and increases the risk of bladder cancer. The other neoplasms are not linked to cyclophosphamide in this source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the risk factors for urothelial carcinoma include smoking, aromatic amines, and cyclophosphamide. COMLEX: hematuria in a patient with prior cyclophosphamide exposure warrants cystoscopy rather than assuming persistent cystitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — chemical and drug exposures that injure the urothelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has painless gross hematuria. Cystoscopy shows patchy mucosal reddening and granularity without an intraluminal mass. Urine cytology shows dyscohesive malignant cells, and biopsy shows cytologically malignant cells within flat urothelium. Which genetic alteration most likely occurred early in the development of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"FGFR3 amplification with RAS activation","B":"MET proto-oncogene activation","C":"VHL loss with stabilized HIF-1 alpha","D":"WT1 deletion at chromosome 11p13","E":"Inactivating mutations of TP53 and RB"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Malignant cells confined to flat urothelium define carcinoma in situ, and the lack of cohesiveness explains shedding of cells into the urine. Most muscle-invasive bladder cancers arise from flat CIS, and mutations disrupting p53 and RB are prevalent in all muscle-invasive cancers but occur early in the development of CIS. FGFR3 and RAS alterations characterize the papillary pathway, and MET, VHL, and WT1 alterations belong to renal tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the flat CIS pathway is driven by TP53 and RB loss. COMLEX: CIS appears as mucosal reddening or granularity without a mass, is often multifocal, and 50 percent to 75 percent progress to invasive cancer if untreated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — divergence of the papillary and flat precursor pathways of urothelial carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"FGFR3 and RAS alterations char","C":"FGFR3 and RAS alterations char","D":"FGFR3 and RAS alterations char"}},{"id":"sdl42_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes transurethral resection of a single papillary bladder tumor. Pathology shows a noninvasive low-grade papillary urothelial carcinoma with orderly architecture, and the tumor harbors a gain-of-function FGFR3 alteration. Which statement best describes his expected course?","choices":{"A":"Frequent recurrence with rare progression","B":"No recurrence after complete resection","C":"Progression to muscle invasion in most patients","D":"Early spread to the liver and lung","E":"Death from disease in about a quarter of patients"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancers have a high tendency to recur, up to 70 percent, and they need lifelong surveillance. Low-grade papillary tumors have a 98 percent 10-year survival rate regardless of the number of recurrences, and fewer than 10 percent progress to higher-grade lesions. Across non-muscle-invasive papillary cancers as a group, only about 20 percent progress to muscle-invasive disease, so progression is uncommon. Death in about 25 percent of cases is the figure for high-grade papillary carcinoma, not low-grade tumors. Spread to liver and lung is the risk of muscle-invasive disease, not of a noninvasive low-grade papillary tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary tumors follow the FGFR3 and RAS pathway and recur but rarely progress. COMLEX: small localized low-grade papillary tumors need only diagnostic transurethral resection, whereas lifelong cystoscopic surveillance is required.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — clinical behavior and surveillance needs of low-grade papillary urothelial tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Across non-muscle-invasive pap"}},{"id":"sdl42_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has painless hematuria. Transurethral resection shows high-grade urothelial carcinoma invading the muscularis propria, and staging imaging shows no distant metastases. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"BCG instillation","B":"Surveillance cystoscopy","C":"Radical cystectomy","D":"Repeat transurethral resection","E":"Intravesical chemotherapy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Stage determines treatment, and invasion of the muscularis propria is an indication for radical cystectomy, or radiation therapy with neoadjuvant or adjuvant chemotherapy, because of the significant potential for regional lymph node and systemic spread to liver and lung. Intravesical BCG is used for CIS and non-muscle-invasive papillary tumors that are large, high grade, multifocal, recurrent, or invade the lamina propria. Resection with surveillance is enough only for small, localized low-grade papillary tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2 invasion of the detrusor marks the shift from local therapy to radical management. COMLEX: metastatic bladder cancer responds poorly to chemotherapy, so a subset may benefit from immune checkpoint inhibitors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — depth of invasion into the bladder wall determines management of urothelial carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man from an endemic region of Egypt has a long history of chronic urinary schistosomiasis and now has hematuria. Cystoscopy shows a fungating, ulcerated bladder mass, and biopsy shows an invasive keratin-producing carcinoma. Which sequence best explains this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Aromatic amine exposure with TP53 loss in flat urothelium","B":"FGFR3 amplification with papillary urothelial proliferation","C":"Egg-induced inflammation with squamous metaplasia","D":"Von Brunn nest downgrowth with intestinal metaplasia","E":"Renal tubular cell implantation at eroded mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Schistosoma haematobium ova are deposited in the bladder wall and incite a chronic inflammatory response that induces progressive mucosal squamous metaplasia, dysplasia, and in some instances neoplasia. Seventy percent of these cancers are squamous, which explains the keratin-producing carcinoma. Aromatic amines and FGFR3 alterations promote urothelial carcinoma, intestinal metaplasia is a precursor of adenocarcinoma, and renal tubular cell implantation produces nephrogenic adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder include Schistosoma haematobium, chronic cystitis, smoking, and chronic stones. COMLEX: extensive multifocal keratinizing squamous metaplasia is a precursor to squamous dysplasia and carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — squamous metaplasia as the setting for squamous carcinoma of the bladder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Aromatic amines and FGFR3 alte"}},{"id":"sdl42_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man worked for 25 years in dye manufacturing and smokes cigarettes. He has painless gross hematuria, and cystoscopy shows a large invasive bladder tumor. Which tumor type is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Adenocarcinoma","C":"Small-cell carcinoma","D":"Urothelial carcinoma","E":"Leiomyosarcoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Urothelial neoplasms represent about 90 percent of bladder tumors. Cigarette smoking is the most important risk factor, and industrial exposure to aryl amines such as 2-naphthylamine is another, with cancer appearing 15 to 40 years after first exposure. Squamous carcinoma is associated with chronic irritation and schistosomiasis, adenocarcinoma is rare, small-cell carcinoma is an uncommon neuroendocrine variant, and leiomyosarcoma is the most common bladder sarcoma in adults but is rare.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urothelial carcinoma is associated with smoking, aromatic amines in dyes, and cyclophosphamide, and presents with painless hematuria. COMLEX: occupational history of dye or rubber industry exposure is a key element to elicit in hematuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — occupational aryl amine and tobacco exposures predispose to urothelial carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with chronic bladder stones and recurrent cystitis undergoes cystoscopic biopsy. The urothelium is extensively replaced by multifocal keratin-producing stratified epithelium. Which interpretation of this finding is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"A precursor to squamous dysplasia and carcinoma","B":"A precursor to bladder adenocarcinoma","C":"A mimic of cancer from implanted renal tubular cells","D":"A histiocytic reaction from defective phagolysosome function","E":"A normal glycogenated lining expected in adult men"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In response to chronic injury the urothelium is often replaced by a more durable squamous epithelium, and extensive multifocal keratinizing squamous metaplasia is a precursor to dysplasia and to in situ and invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Bladder schistosomiasis is the classic example of this sequence. Intestinal metaplasia, not squamous metaplasia, is the precursor of adenocarcinoma, nephrogenic adenoma is due to implanted tubular cells, and malakoplakia reflects defective phagocyte function. Keratinizing squamous epithelium is not the normal adult bladder lining; it is metaplasia after chronic injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic irritation from stones or infection promotes squamous metaplasia, dysplasia, and squamous cell carcinoma. COMLEX: glycogenated nonkeratinizing squamous epithelium is a normal finding at the trigone in females and should not be mistaken for metaplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — chronic irritation and infection induce squamous metaplasia of the bladder mucosa","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Intestinal metaplasia, not squ"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl42_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman develops gross hematuria and suprapubic pain on day 3 of her first high-dose cyclophosphamide cycle for lymphoma. She has had no transplant and no pelvic radiation. Her urine culture is negative, and her platelet count is normal. Which mechanism best explains her bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Acrolein injury to the urothelium","B":"Immune complex vasculitis of the bladder","C":"BK polyomavirus urothelial lysis","D":"Escherichia coli adherence to urothelium","E":"Radiation endarteritis of bladder vessels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatic metabolism of cyclophosphamide yields acrolein, which concentrates in urine and directly damages the urothelium, causing ulceration, vascular necrosis, and hemorrhagic cystitis. Immune complex vasculitis of the bladder is not a recognized cause here. BK polyomavirus causes hemorrhagic cystitis in immunosuppressed transplant recipients, and she has had no transplant. E. coli adherence is excluded by her negative culture. Radiation endarteritis follows pelvic radiation, which she has not had. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 42, cyclophosphamide-induced hemorrhagic cystitis); Katzung Ch. 54 (cyclophosphamide toxicity).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cyclophosphamide forms acrolein, which causes hemorrhagic cystitis; mesna and hydration protect the bladder. COMLEX: sterile hematuria during chemotherapy suggests a drug cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — chronic urothelial injury can drive metaplasia and carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl42_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has had years of hesitancy, a weak stream, and nocturia from an enlarged prostate. CT shows a 5-cm thin-walled outpouching of the bladder wall near the ureteral orifice, and the rest of the bladder wall is thickened and trabeculated. Which mechanism most likely produced the outpouching?","choices":{"A":"Congenital failure of the detrusor musculature","B":"Raised bladder pressure from outflow obstruction","C":"Erosion of the wall by an invasive carcinoma","D":"Transmural fibrosis from interstitial cystitis","E":"Defective phagocyte killing in malakoplakia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bladder diverticula are pouch-like invaginations of the wall, congenital or acquired; acquired diverticula are most often associated with prostatic hyperplasia, whose outflow obstruction raises intravesical pressure and causes outpouching of the bladder wall. Congenital diverticula come from focal failure of normal musculature or fetal urinary obstruction, which does not fit an acquired lesion in an older man with obstruction. Carcinoma can rarely arise in a diverticulum, where it tends to be more advanced because the muscularis propria is thin or absent, but carcinoma does not create the pouch. Transmural fibrosis in interstitial cystitis contracts the bladder rather than producing an outpouching. Malakoplakia forms soft yellow mucosal plaques.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acquired bladder diverticula follow outflow obstruction (prostatic hyperplasia) with raised intravesical pressure; the obstructed bladder also shows smooth muscle hypertrophy and trabeculation. COMLEX: Urinary stasis in a diverticulum predisposes to infection and stones, and tumors arising there are often advanced at diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 1 — prostatic hyperplasia causes bladder outflow obstruction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Carcinoma can rarely arise in ","E":"Malakoplakia forms soft yellow"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl42_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a papillary bladder tumor resected transurethrally. Pathology shows invasion of the lamina propria with no muscularis propria involvement. He is treated with intravesical Bacillus Calmette-Guérin. Which mechanism best explains this therapy's effect?","choices":{"A":"Local cell-mediated immune attack","B":"Inhibition of FGFR3 tyrosine kinase","C":"Direct DNA cross-linking of tumor","D":"Antibody-mediated complement lysis","E":"Hormonal suppression of growth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lamina propria invasion without muscle invasion is non-muscle-invasive disease, managed by transurethral resection followed by intravesical BCG. BCG, an attenuated strain of Mycobacterium bovis, incites a local cell-mediated immune response that destroys mucosal tumor cells. Direct DNA cross-linking describes alkylating or platinum chemotherapy. FGFR3 tyrosine kinase inhibition targets a papillary pathway mutation but is not how BCG works. Antibody-mediated complement lysis is not the BCG mechanism. Hormonal suppression is not used for urothelial carcinoma. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 42, management of non-muscle-invasive disease with BCG); Robbins Ch. 21 (urothelial carcinoma treatment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ta, Tis, and T1 tumors are treated with TURBT plus BCG; T2 (detrusor invasion) requires radical cystectomy. COMLEX: BCG instillation is also the most common cause of granulomatous prostatitis in the United States.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — BCG therapy can cause granulomatous prostatitis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl42_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old smoker has painless hematuria. Cystoscopy shows flat, velvety red patches with no papillary mass, yet urine cytology contains many malignant cells. Which property of this lesion best explains the positive cytology?","choices":{"A":"Fragile papillary fronds shedding cells","B":"Invasion of the detrusor muscle","C":"Poor cohesion of the neoplastic cells","D":"Necrosis within a bulky invasive mass","E":"Inflammation lysing benign urothelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Flat red mucosa with malignant cytology indicates urothelial carcinoma in situ, made of full-thickness anaplastic cells that lack cohesion and shed readily into urine. Fragile papillary fronds belong to papillary tumors, which are absent here. Invasion of the detrusor muscle is a staging feature and does not explain shedding from a flat lesion. Necrosis within a bulky invasive mass does not fit the absence of a mass. Inflammation does not produce malignant cells. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 42, flat carcinoma in situ pathway); Robbins Ch. 21 (urothelial carcinoma in situ).","boardPrep":"Step 1: flat CIS has TP53 and RB loss, sheds malignant cells into urine, and progresses to invasive carcinoma in 50% to 75% if untreated. COMLEX: urine cytology is especially useful for detecting high-grade flat lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — smoking is the major risk factor for urothelial carcinoma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Inflammation does not produce "}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl42_q5","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a papillary bladder tumor resected. Pathology shows high-grade urothelial carcinoma invading the lamina propria, and the sampled muscularis propria is free of tumor. Which pathologic stage applies?","choices":{"A":"Stage pTa","B":"Stage pTis","C":"Stage pT2a","D":"Stage pT1","E":"Stage pT3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Invasion of the lamina propria without extension into the muscularis propria is stage pT1. Stage pTa is a non-invasive papillary carcinoma. Stage pTis is flat carcinoma in situ. Stage pT2a means invasion of the inner half of the detrusor muscle. Stage pT3 means extension through the muscle into perivesical tissue. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 42, pathologic staging of urothelial carcinoma); Robbins Ch. 21 (staging of bladder cancer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pTa is papillary non-invasive, pTis is flat CIS, pT1 is lamina propria, and pT2 is detrusor muscle. COMLEX: pT2 is the threshold for radical cystectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — detrusor invasion (pT2) dictates radical cystectomy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl42_q6","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man who has smoked for 40 years has painless gross hematuria, and biopsy shows urothelial carcinoma. He never worked with dyes or chemicals. Which mechanism best links his smoking to this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Immune suppression by nicotine","B":"Bloodborne spread of tar particles","C":"Chronic hypoxia of the urothelium","D":"Urinary excretion of aromatic amines","E":"Schistosome eggs in the bladder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tobacco smoke contains carcinogens, including aromatic amines and nitrosamines, that are filtered by the kidneys, concentrated in urine, and stored in the bladder, damaging the urothelium. Smoking increases risk 3- to 7-fold. Bloodborne spread of tar particles is not the mechanism. Chronic hypoxia of the urothelium is not how smoking causes bladder cancer. Immune suppression by nicotine is not the described pathway. Schistosome eggs in the bladder describe Schistosoma haematobium, which leads to squamous cell carcinoma. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 42, cigarette smoking and urothelial carcinoma); Robbins Ch. 21 (risk factors for urothelial carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: smoking is the biggest risk factor for urothelial carcinoma because urinary carcinogens bathe the bladder. COMLEX: smoking cessation lowers bladder cancer risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — FGFR3/RAS and TP53/RB define the two carcinoma pathways","isHighYield":false}],"43":[{"id":"sdl43_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":1,"stem":"In adults, minimal change disease can occur in association with which malignancy?","choices":{"A":"Renal cell carcinoma","B":"Colon carcinoma","C":"Hodgkin lymphoma","D":"Melanoma","E":"Lung carcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins notes that minimal change disease in adults can be associated with Hodgkin lymphoma and, less frequently, other lymphomas and leukemias, and that it may also follow vaccination or infection or occur with NSAID therapy. Carcinomas of the lung and colon and melanoma are the malignancies associated with secondary membranous nephropathy, present in as many as 5% to 10% of adults with that disease. Renal cell carcinoma is a primary kidney tumor known for paraneoplastic syndromes, not for causing minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Minimal change disease: most common nephrotic syndrome in children, normal light microscopy, diffuse foot process effacement, selective proteinuria, excellent steroid response; adult associations include Hodgkin lymphoma and NSAIDs. COMLEX: New nephrotic syndrome in an adult with lymphadenopathy should prompt evaluation for lymphoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — the primary nephrotic podocytopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunoglobulin subclass, a poor activator of the classical complement pathway, is the principal immunoglobulin deposited in PLA2R-associated membranous nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"IgG1","B":"IgG2","C":"IgG3","D":"IgM","E":"IgG4"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins notes that IgG4, which differs from other IgG subclasses in being a poor activator of the classical complement pathway, is the principal immunoglobulin deposited in many cases of membranous nephropathy, PLA2R-associated disease in particular; how it is associated with complement activation is unclear, although C5b-9 can be found in the lesions. IgG1 and IgG3 are effective activators of the classical pathway and are not the subclass singled out. IgG2 is not the principal deposited subclass. IgM is typically trapped in the sclerotic segments of FSGS rather than deposited along the subepithelial basement membrane.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Membranous nephropathy: subepithelial deposits with spikes, granular IgG and C3, IgG4-dominant in PLA2R disease; other antigens include THSD7A, NELL1 (malignancy), and EXT1/2 (autoimmune disease). COMLEX: Antigen typing helps separate primary disease from secondary causes worth searching for.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — the primary nephrotic podocytopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which lesion is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States, particularly among people of Hispanic and African descent?","choices":{"A":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","B":"Primary membranous nephropathy","C":"Adult minimal change disease","D":"Fibrillary glomerulonephritis","E":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins identifies FSGS, primary and secondary forms together, as the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States, particularly in people of Hispanic and African descent. Idiopathic FSGS accounts for about 35 percent of adult nephrotic syndrome and 10 percent of childhood nephrotic syndrome, and Robbins notes that membranous nephropathy is the most common primary lesion in older adults. Fibrillary glomerulonephritis is an uncommon immune complex disease, not a leading cause of adult nephrotic syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS shows segmental sclerosis with hyalinosis, a poor steroid response, and progression to ESKD in at least 50 percent of patients within 10 years. COMLEX: minimal change disease is the leading cause in children, about 75 percent of childhood cases, and membranous nephropathy is the most common primary lesion in older adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — nephrotic podocytopathies: minimal change disease, FSGS, and membranous nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":1,"stem":"Which streptococcal product is thought to be the principal antigen in most cases of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis and has been localized to the hump-like deposits?","choices":{"A":"M protein of the cell wall","B":"Pyogenic exotoxin B, SpeB","C":"Streptolysin O hemolysin","D":"Hyaluronic acid capsule","E":"Lipoteichoic acid of the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins states that the preponderance of evidence suggests streptococcal pyogenic exotoxin B (SpeB) is the principal antigen in most cases; it is commonly secreted by nephritogenic strains and has been localized to the hump-like subepithelial deposits. M protein typing identifies the nephritogenic strains (types 1, 4, and 12 account for more than 90% of cases) but is not described as the deposited antigen. Streptolysin O, the hyaluronic acid capsule, and lipoteichoic acid are not the antigens localized to the deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Poststreptococcal GN: 1–4 weeks after pharyngitis or impetigo, children 6–10 years, nephritogenic group A strains (M types 1, 4, 12), SpeB antigen, low C3, granular IgG and C3, subepithelial humps. COMLEX: Most children recover with supportive care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — nephritic pathophysiology and poststreptococcal GN","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Streptolysin O, the hyaluronic"}},{"id":"sdl43_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":1,"stem":"Which category accounts for the majority of cases of crescentic (rapidly progressive) glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-GBM disease without lung involvement","B":"Goodpasture syndrome with lung hemorrhage","C":"Immune complex crescentic GN, type II","D":"C3-dominant crescentic glomerulonephritis","E":"Pauci-immune crescentic GN, type III"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins summarizes that about one-fifth of patients with crescentic glomerulonephritis have anti-GBM antibody-mediated disease without lung involvement, about one-quarter have immune complex-mediated crescentic disease, and the remainder, the majority, have the pauci-immune type, most of them with circulating ANCA. Goodpasture syndrome is the anti-GBM form with pulmonary hemorrhage, a smaller group. Immune complex crescentic disease, from postinfectious GN, lupus, IgA nephropathy, and others, accounts for about a quarter and is treated by addressing the underlying disease rather than plasmapheresis. C3-dominant crescentic disease from alternative pathway abnormalities is rare.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crescentic GN types: I anti-GBM (linear IgG; plasmapheresis), II immune complex (granular deposits; treat the underlying disease), III pauci-immune (ANCA; granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis, or renal-limited; B-cell therapies). COMLEX: Rapidly rising creatinine with an active sediment calls for urgent serology and biopsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — rapidly progressive GN and crescent formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":1,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy develops cola-colored urine and periorbital edema 2 weeks after streptococcal pharyngitis, and his serum C3 is low. Immunostaining of his kidney biopsy localizes a bacterial protein within the glomerular deposits. Which streptococcal product is thought to be the principal antigen in most cases of this disease?","choices":{"A":"M protein of the cell wall","B":"Streptolysin O","C":"Streptokinase","D":"Pyrogenic exotoxin B","E":"Hyaluronic acid capsule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins names streptococcal pyogenic exotoxin B (SpeB) as the principal antigen in most, but not all, cases of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis; it is secreted by nephritogenic strains and has been localized to the hump-like deposits. M protein is used to type the nephritogenic strains rather than serving as the main deposited antigen. Streptolysin O is the target of the antistreptolysin titer used in diagnosis, streptokinase is a fibrinolytic enzyme, and the hyaluronic acid capsule is an antiphagocytic coat; none is identified as the principal deposit antigen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: only certain group A strains are nephritogenic, and more than 90 percent of cases trace to M types 1, 4, and 12. COMLEX: the antigens are first planted in subendothelial sites, forming immune complexes in situ before the subepithelial humps appear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — immune complex pathogenesis of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"M protein is used to type the ","B":"Streptolysin O is the target o","C":"Streptolysin O is the target o"}},{"id":"sdl43_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis has persistent microscopic hematuria, and a kidney biopsy shows mesangial deposits of IgA with C3. Which mechanism best links his liver disease to the glomerular deposits?","choices":{"A":"Portal hypertension raising glomerular capillary pressure","B":"Defective hepatobiliary clearance of IgA complexes","C":"Hypoalbuminemia exposing anionic basement membrane sites","D":"Hepatic loss of complement factor H synthesis","E":"Ammonia toxicity to mesangial cell membranes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins describes secondary IgA nephropathy in liver disease, in which defective hepatobiliary clearance of IgA complexes lets them persist in the circulation and lodge in the mesangium. Raised glomerular capillary pressure drives hyperfiltration injury and sclerosis, not mesangial IgA deposition. Hypoalbuminemia does not create immune deposits, inherited factor H defects underlie atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome, and ammonia is not a recognized cause of glomerular deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA nephropathy is also more frequent in celiac disease, in which intestinal mucosal defects are seen. COMLEX: Henoch-Schönlein purpura is the systemic childhood disorder with similar mesangial IgA deposits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — multi-hit pathogenesis and secondary forms of IgA nephropathy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Hypoalbuminemia does not creat","D":"Hypoalbuminemia does not creat"}},{"id":"sdl43_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with end-stage kidney disease from biopsy-proven IgA nephropathy receives a kidney transplant from his brother. According to Robbins, which outcome regarding his original disease is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies form against collagen in the new graft","B":"The graft is spared because the defect lies in the native kidney","C":"IgA deposits frequently recur in the graft","D":"Membranous nephropathy replaces it in most grafts","E":"Thin basement membranes develop in the graft"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IgA nephropathy is driven by circulating galactose-deficient IgA1 and the immune complexes it forms, not by a defect intrinsic to the native kidney, so Robbins notes that recurrence of IgA deposits in transplanted kidneys is frequent. In about 15 percent of those with recurrent deposits, the disease runs the same slowly progressive course as primary IgA nephropathy. Antibodies to basement membrane collagen define anti-GBM disease, and membranous nephropathy and thin basement membranes are separate diseases that do not replace IgA deposits in a graft.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the circulating defect is galactose-deficient IgA1 recognized by IgG autoantibodies, which is why a new kidney is not protected. COMLEX: secondary IgA nephropathy also occurs with intestinal disease and staphylococcal infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — course and progression of IgA nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which genetic pattern typifies thin basement membrane nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Heterozygous autosomal alpha-3 or alpha-4 mutations","B":"X-linked large deletions of the alpha-5 chain","C":"Autosomal recessive mutations in nephrin","D":"Autosomal dominant uromodulin gene mutations","E":"Sporadic autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy is a related disorder associated with mutations in the genes encoding the alpha-3 or alpha-4 chains of type IV collagen. It most often has autosomal inheritance, and most patients are heterozygous for the defective gene. X-linked alpha-5 deletions cause classic Alport syndrome, nephrin mutations cause congenital nephrotic syndrome, uromodulin mutations cause autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease, and alpha-3 autoantibodies cause anti-GBM disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: benign familial hematuria with a diffusely thinned GBM points to the mild end of the type IV collagen spectrum, where a milder Alport-type presentation shows GBM widths of 150 to 225 nm compared with 300 to 400 nm in healthy adults. COMLEX: Robbins treats these as a spectrum requiring genetic counseling and clinical follow-up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 1 and Obj 2 — IgA nephropathy pathogenesis and the inherited type IV collagen basement membrane disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":1,"stem":"In ischemic acute tubular injury, sublethal endothelial injury contributes to intrarenal vasoconstriction through which change in vasoactive mediators?","choices":{"A":"More prostacyclin with less thromboxane","B":"More bradykinin with less angiotensin II","C":"More natriuretic peptide with less renin","D":"More endothelin with less nitric oxide","E":"Less adenosine with more nitric oxide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins attributes part of the intrarenal vasoconstriction of ischemic acute tubular injury to sublethal endothelial injury, which increases release of the vasoconstrictor endothelin and decreases production of the vasodilators nitric oxide and prostacyclin. The renin-angiotensin system, stimulated by low tubular sodium during hypotension, adds to the constriction. More prostacyclin, more bradykinin, more natriuretic peptide, or more nitric oxide would each favor vasodilation, and angiotensin II and renin are increased rather than decreased.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vasoconstriction reduces both glomerular blood flow and oxygen delivery to the outer medulla. COMLEX: ischemia or toxins may also lower the glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — hemodynamic disturbances in ischemic acute tubular injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"More prostacyclin, more bradyk","E":"More prostacyclin, more bradyk"}},{"id":"sdl43_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":1,"stem":"A kidney biopsy taken during recovery from ischemic acute tubular injury shows evidence of tubular epithelial regeneration. Which histologic finding represents that regeneration?","choices":{"A":"Tubular atrophy with thyroid-like colloid casts","B":"Flat cells with hyperchromatic nuclei and mitoses","C":"Interstitial eosinophils with tubulitis","D":"Calcified cells with large acidophilic inclusions","E":"Neutrophil casts with cortical microabscesses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins describes epithelial regeneration in ischemic acute tubular injury as flattened epithelial cells with hyperchromatic nuclei and mitotic figures; in time this regeneration repopulates the tubules so that no residual damage is seen. Dilated atrophic tubules with colloid-like casts mark chronic pyelonephritis, interstitial eosinophils with tubulitis mark drug-induced interstitial nephritis, large acidophilic inclusions that later calcify mark mercuric chloride toxicity, and neutrophil casts with microabscesses mark acute pyelonephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: repair depends on reversibly injured epithelial cells that proliferate and differentiate, aided by basement membranes preserved in the patchy lesions. COMLEX: interstitial edema and leukocytes within dilated vasa recta are other findings in ischemic injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — recovery of tubular function after acute tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, approximately what proportion of patients with acute tubular injury who survive the precipitating event recover renal function with supportive care?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 40 percent","C":"About 60 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins states that with supportive care 95 percent of patients who do not succumb to the precipitating event recover, because the patchy necrosis and preserved basement membranes allow the tubules to re-epithelialize. Recovery is expected after nephrotoxic injury unless the toxin has seriously damaged other organs such as the liver or heart. The high death rate belongs to the precipitating illness: in shock from sepsis, extensive burns, or multiorgan failure, mortality can exceed 50 percent, so the lower figures do not describe renal recovery in survivors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prognosis depends on the magnitude and duration of injury, and the severity of the morphologic findings often does not match the clinical severity. COMLEX: subtle tubular dysfunction may persist for months after recovery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical phases and recovery after acute tubular injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The high death rate belongs to","B":"The high death rate belongs to","C":"The high death rate belongs to","D":"The high death rate belongs to"}},{"id":"sdl43_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and a 50-pack-year smoking history has worsening hypertension and an abdominal bruit, and his creatinine rises sharply after an ACE inhibitor is started. Which lesion most likely narrows his renal arteries?","choices":{"A":"Fibromuscular dysplasia of the medial layer","B":"Cholesterol atheroemboli in arcuate arteries","C":"Fibrinoid necrosis of interlobular arteries","D":"Atheromatous plaque at the vessel origin","E":"Tumor invasion of the arterial wall"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The most common cause of renal artery stenosis, about 70 percent of cases, is narrowing at the origin of the renal artery by an atheromatous plaque, which is more frequent in males and increases with age and diabetes. Fibromuscular dysplasia is the second most frequent cause and occurs more often in younger women. Atheroemboli and fibrinoid necrosis affect intrarenal vessels and are not the usual cause of main renal artery stenosis. Tumor invasion of the arterial wall is not a cause of renal artery stenosis that the course lists, which are atheroma and fibromuscular dysplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal artery stenosis raises renin release from the ischemic kidney, causing angiotensin II mediated hypertension. COMLEX: unilateral stenosis causes 2 to 5 percent of hypertension and is potentially curable by surgery, with a cure rate of 70 to 80 percent in well-selected cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — renovascular disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, and the common pathway of chronic kidney disease progression","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fibromuscular dysplasia is the","B":"Atheroemboli and fibrinoid nec"}},{"id":"sdl43_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl develops hemolytic anemia with schistocytes, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury without any preceding diarrhea. Plasma ADAMTS13 activity is normal. A mutation in which protein is most likely?","choices":{"A":"ADAMTS13 metalloprotease","B":"Von Willebrand factor multimers","C":"Complement factor H","D":"Antithrombin III","E":"Coagulation factor VIII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"More than half of patients with atypical HUS have identifiable mutations of complement-regulatory proteins, most commonly factor H, which promotes breakdown of the alternative pathway C3 convertase and protects cells from uncontrolled complement activation. Mutations in factor I and CD46 also occur, and autoantibodies against regulators can produce the same disease. ADAMTS13 deficiency causes TTP, and von Willebrand factor, antithrombin III, and factor VIII are not the mutated proteins in atypical HUS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: atypical HUS lacks preceding diarrhea and reflects complement dysregulation, and it is separated from TTP by normal plasma ADAMTS13 levels. COMLEX: atypical HUS has a worse prognosis than typical HUS because the triggers may be genetic or chronic conditions that are difficult to treat.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — renovascular disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, and the common pathway of chronic kidney disease progression","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"ADAMTS13 deficiency causes TTP"}},{"id":"sdl43_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"Once nephron loss lowers the GFR to roughly what fraction of normal does progression to end-stage kidney disease proceed independent of the original insult?","choices":{"A":"30 to 50 percent of normal","B":"5 to 10 percent of normal","C":"60 to 75 percent of normal","D":"80 to 90 percent of normal","E":"15 to 20 percent of normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once any renal disease has destroyed enough nephrons to reduce the GFR to about 30 to 50 percent of normal, progression to end-stage kidney disease proceeds at a steady rate independent of the original insult or the activity of the underlying disease. The two major histologic features of this progressive damage are glomerulosclerosis and tubulointerstitial fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after enough nephron loss, hyperfiltration of surviving glomeruli perpetuates the decline regardless of the original cause. COMLEX: the extent of tubulointerstitial damage often correlates better with functional decline than the severity of glomerular injury, and renin-angiotensin system inhibitors are the most successful intervention to slow sclerosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — renovascular disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, and the common pathway of chronic kidney disease progression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":1,"stem":"Which cardiac abnormality occurs in about 20 to 25 percent of patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Bicuspid aortic valve","B":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","C":"Ventricular septal defect","D":"Mitral valve prolapse","E":"Coarctation of the aorta"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins reports mitral valve prolapse and other valvular anomalies in 20 to 25 percent of patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, most of them asymptomatic. Bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation are classic associations of Turner syndrome, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is a sarcomere gene disorder, and ventricular septal defect is a common isolated congenital lesion; none is the valve association listed for this disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intracranial berry aneurysms arise in the circle of Willis, and their rupture accounts for death in 4 to 10 percent of patients. COMLEX: progression of the kidney disease is faster in males, in people of African descent, and with hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — extrarenal manifestations of polycystic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":1,"stem":"Which urinary crystal shape is characteristic of magnesium ammonium phosphate stones?","choices":{"A":"Envelope","B":"Rhomboid","C":"Hexagonal","D":"Wedge-shaped prism","E":"Coffin lid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Struvite stones, composed of magnesium ammonium phosphate, form after infection with urease-producing bacteria and appear as coffin lid shaped crystals in urine. Calcium oxalate crystals are envelope shaped, uric acid crystals are rhomboid, cystine crystals are hexagonal, and calcium phosphate crystals are wedge-shaped prisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: crystal shape identifies stone type on urine microscopy, and the coffin lid shape means struvite. COMLEX: struvite forms after urea-splitting bacteria such as Proteus convert urea to ammonia, producing alkaline urine and staghorn calculi.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 2 and SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — polycystic kidney disease genetics and extrarenal features, and calculus chemistry with crystal morphology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":1,"stem":"Which liver lesion accompanies autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease in children who survive infancy?","choices":{"A":"Congenital hepatic fibrosis","B":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis","C":"Hepatocellular adenoma","D":"Portal vein thrombosis","E":"Alcoholic steatohepatitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patients with autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease who survive infancy may develop congenital hepatic fibrosis, a bland periportal fibrosis with proliferation of well-differentiated biliary ductules. In older children the liver disease becomes the predominant concern and can cause portal hypertension with splenomegaly. In almost all cases the liver also has cysts with portal fibrosis and bile duct proliferation. Primary sclerosing cholangitis, hepatocellular adenoma and alcoholic steatohepatitis are acquired liver diseases with other causes, such as oral contraceptives for adenoma and alcohol for steatohepatitis, not features of this inherited disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARPKD is caused by PKHD1 mutations encoding fibrocystin, with enlarged smooth kidneys and radially arranged collecting duct cysts. COMLEX: in adult polycystic disease the liver cysts derive from biliary epithelium, occur in about 40 percent of patients, and are usually asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 2 and SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — polycystic kidney disease genetics and extrarenal features, and calculus chemistry with crystal morphology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal cell carcinoma subtype most often shows trisomy 7 and trisomy 17?","choices":{"A":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","B":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma","C":"Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","D":"Collecting duct carcinoma","E":"Xp11 translocation renal cell carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma accounts for 10 to 15 percent of renal cancers and is not associated with chromosome 3p deletions. Its most common cytogenetic abnormalities are trisomies 7 and 17 with loss of the Y chromosome in sporadic male cases, and the familial form shows trisomy 7 with MET mutations. Clear cell tumors show 3p deletion, chromophobe tumors show multiple chromosome losses with hypodiploidy, and Xp11 tumors show TFE3 translocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary RCC is often multifocal, hemorrhagic, and cystic, with foamy macrophages in the papillary cores and sometimes psammoma bodies. COMLEX: papillary adenomas smaller than 1.5 cm share trisomies 7 and 17 and are found in 7 to 22 percent of autopsies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 and Obj 3 — molecular drivers of renal cell carcinoma subtypes and germline genetics of pediatric nephroblastoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Clear cell tumors show 3p dele"}},{"id":"sdl43_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":1,"stem":"Germline loss-of-function mutations in which gene cause cutaneous and uterine leiomyomata together with an aggressive renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"VHL","B":"MET","C":"FH","D":"TSC2","E":"WT1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell carcinoma syndrome is an autosomal dominant disease caused by loss-of-function mutations of FH, which encodes fumarate hydratase; it produces cutaneous and uterine leiomyomata and an aggressive renal cancer. VHL loss causes von Hippel-Lindau syndrome with bilateral clear cell carcinomas, activating MET mutations cause hereditary papillary carcinoma, TSC2 mutations underlie tuberous sclerosis with angiomyolipomas, and WT1 deletion predisposes to Wilms tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: familial renal cancers account for only about 4 percent of renal cancers and usually arise in younger patients. COMLEX: one-half to two-thirds of people with von Hippel-Lindau syndrome develop renal cysts and bilateral, often multiple, carcinomas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — germline drivers of renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which exposure is the most significant risk factor for renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Cigarette smoking","B":"Aniline dye work","C":"Phenacetin analgesics","D":"Schistosoma infection","E":"Cyclophosphamide therapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins identifies tobacco use as the most significant risk factor: cigarette smoking doubles the incidence of renal cell carcinoma, and pipe and cigar smokers are also more susceptible. Other risks include obesity, hypertension, unopposed estrogen therapy, exposure to asbestos, petroleum products, and heavy metals, and end-stage or acquired cystic kidney disease. Aniline dyes, Schistosoma haematobium, and cyclophosphamide are classic risks for bladder cancer, and phenacetin-containing analgesics cause analgesic nephropathy with papillary necrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the classic triad of costovertebral pain, palpable mass, and hematuria appears in only about 10 percent of cases. COMLEX: the most reliable clue is hematuria, which is often intermittent and microscopic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — clinical profile of renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Aniline dyes, Schistosoma haem"}},{"id":"sdl43_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy develops periorbital swelling 10 days after an upper respiratory infection. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood, serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and blood pressure is normal. A kidney biopsy shows normal glomeruli by light microscopy, diffuse foot process effacement, and no electron-dense deposits. Which of the following best explains his proteinuria?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies to phospholipase A2 receptor forming subepithelial complexes","B":"Planted streptococcal antigen forming subepithelial humps","C":"Autoantibodies to nephrin disrupting slit diaphragm cross-linking","D":"Galactose-deficient IgA1 deposited within the mesangium","E":"Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A young child with nephrotic syndrome, normal glomeruli by light microscopy, diffuse foot process effacement, and no deposits has minimal change disease. The current hypothesis is that circulating antibodies against nephrin, an essential slit diaphragm protein, interfere with nephrin cross-linking and cause rapid breakdown of the slit diaphragm. PLA2R antibodies, streptococcal antigens, and aberrantly glycosylated IgA1 all produce immune deposits, and anti-GBM antibodies cause linear GBM staining with crescentic disease, none of which fit normal light microscopy and absent deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: minimal change disease is the most frequent cause of nephrotic syndrome in children, with peak incidence at 2 to 6 years and onset sometimes after infection or immunization. COMLEX: expect a dramatic corticosteroid response in more than 90 percent of children, and the proteinuria is highly selective, mostly albumin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — matching light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy patterns to the primary nephrotic diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"PLA2R antibodies, streptococca","E":"PLA2R antibodies, streptococca"}},{"id":"sdl43_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old African American man has 2 months of leg edema, blood pressure of 158/98 mm Hg, urine protein of 5 g/day (reference range: 0–150 mg/day), microscopic hematuria, and a creatinine of 1.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). HIV testing is negative and he takes no medications. A biopsy shows sclerosis and hyalinosis involving part of the capillary tuft in some glomeruli, with foot process effacement in the remaining glomeruli. Corticosteroids produce no response. Variants in which gene most likely increased his risk of this disease?","choices":{"A":"COL4A5","B":"PKD1","C":"PKHD1","D":"UMOD","E":"APOL1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sclerosis of part of the tuft in a subset of glomeruli with hyalinosis defines FSGS, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States. Hypertension, hematuria, reduced GFR, and poor steroid response distinguish it from minimal change disease. Two sequence variants, G1 and G2, in APOL1 are strongly associated with chronic kidney disease and renal failure in individuals of African descent and with FSGS and collapsing glomerulopathy. COL4A5, PKD1, PKHD1, and UMOD are the genes of Alport syndrome, adult polycystic disease, childhood polycystic disease, and autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the APOL1 G1 and G2 risk alleles are common in people of African descent, apparently because they convey resistance to trypanosome infection. COMLEX: primary FSGS has a poor corticosteroid response, at least 50 percent of patients reach ESKD within 10 years, and it recurs in 25 to 50 percent of kidney transplants.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — ancestry-linked and genetic risk factors in the primary nephrotic diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man who smokes has 3 months of leg swelling. Urine protein is 6 g/day, serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and blood pressure is normal. A kidney biopsy shows diffusely thickened capillary walls with silver-staining spikes, granular IgG along the basement membrane, and subepithelial deposits on electron microscopy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Screen for underlying malignancy and other secondary causes","B":"Begin high-dose prednisone for minimal change disease","C":"Start plasma exchange for anti-GBM antibody disease","D":"Give empiric penicillin for poststreptococcal disease","E":"Order genetic testing for mutations in the nephrin gene"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diffusely thickened capillary walls with silver-staining spikes, granular IgG, and subepithelial deposits define membranous nephropathy. Robbins stresses that secondary causes must be ruled out first, because treating the underlying condition or stopping the offending drug can reverse the injury, and as many as 5 to 10 percent of adults with membranous nephropathy have an underlying malignant tumor, particularly carcinoma of the lung or colon or melanoma. The proteinuria is nonselective and usually does not respond well to corticosteroids, plasma exchange treats anti-GBM disease, and poststreptococcal disease is a nephritic process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: membranous nephropathy shows subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes on silver stain and is a hypercoagulable state with a risk of renal vein thrombosis. COMLEX: about 75 percent of cases are primary, remissions occur in up to 40 percent of patients, and the disease recurs in up to 40 percent of transplants.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — primary nephrotic diseases and how their treatment responses differ","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The proteinuria is nonselectiv"}},{"id":"sdl43_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis fails to improve; over 3 weeks he becomes severely oliguric, and his creatinine rises from 0.9 to 4.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.3–0.7 mg/dL). Biopsy shows crescents in most glomeruli, and immunofluorescence shows coarse granular IgG and C3 deposits. Which mechanism underlies this crescentic disease?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies against the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV","B":"ANCA-driven neutrophil injury with few deposits","C":"Immune complexes deposited in the capillary walls","D":"Autoantibodies to the phospholipase A2 receptor","E":"Complement-regulatory defects with C3-dominant deposits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins classifies crescentic glomerulonephritis as anti-GBM (type I), immune complex (type II), or pauci-immune (type III); immune complex cases, which include postinfectious, lupus, and IgA-related disease, show granular deposits by immunofluorescence. A small minority of children with poststreptococcal disease, perhaps less than 1 percent, become severely oliguric and develop this rapidly progressive form. Alpha-3 collagen antibodies give linear IgG, ANCA disease shows little or no immune deposition, PLA2R antibodies cause membranous nephropathy, and complement-regulatory defects give C3 staining without IgG.","boardPrep":"Step 1: about one-fifth of crescentic glomerulonephritis is anti-GBM disease without lung involvement, about one-quarter is immune complex mediated, and the majority is pauci-immune. COMLEX: crescents form from proliferating parietal epithelial cells and monocytes after GBM ruptures let fibrin into Bowman space.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — classification of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Alpha-3 collagen antibodies gi","D":"Alpha-3 collagen antibodies gi"}},{"id":"sdl43_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has hematuria with red cell casts and proteinuria of 1.2 g/day. Kidney biopsy shows segmental endocapillary proliferation, necrosis, and a few crescents involving 8 of 25 sampled glomeruli, with granular immune deposits; the other glomeruli are uninvolved. Which class best describes her renal lesion?","choices":{"A":"Class III with focal involvement","B":"Class IV with diffuse involvement","C":"Class II with mesangial proliferation","D":"Class V with membranous thickening","E":"Class VI with advanced sclerosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Eight of 25 glomeruli, about 32 percent, are involved, and Robbins defines class III (focal) disease by involvement of fewer than 50 percent of glomeruli. The lesions may be segmental or global and show endocapillary and mesangial proliferation, necrosis, and crescents, and red cell casts are common when the disease is active. Class IV lesions resemble class III but typically affect more than 50 percent of glomeruli, class II shows mesangial proliferation without capillary involvement, class V shows diffuse capillary wall thickening from subepithelial deposits, and class VI shows sclerosis of 90 percent or more of glomeruli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: some patients with class III disease progress to diffuse lupus nephritis, and active lesions can heal completely or scar. COMLEX: up to 50 percent of patients with SLE develop clinically significant renal involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 3 — lupus nephritis classes by extent and deposit location","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Class IV lesions resemble clas"}},{"id":"sdl43_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has 3 weeks of fatigue and oliguria, and his creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 5.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He has chronic sinus disease and pulmonary nodules on chest imaging. Urinalysis shows red cell casts and PR3-ANCA is positive. A kidney biopsy shows crescents in most glomeruli with segmental fibrinoid necrosis. Which additional finding is most likely on immunofluorescence and electron microscopy?","choices":{"A":"Linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","B":"Granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial humps","C":"Granular IgG and C1q with subendothelial deposits","D":"Mesangial IgA staining with mesangial deposits","E":"Scant immune deposits with GBM ruptures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sinus disease, pulmonary nodules, positive PR3-ANCA, and crescentic glomerulonephritis with segmental fibrinoid necrosis indicate pauci-immune crescentic GN as seen in granulomatosis with polyangiitis. By definition there is little or no immune reactant deposition by immunofluorescence, although electron microscopy shows GBM ruptures regardless of type, which allow leukocytes and plasma proteins into the urinary space to trigger crescent formation. Linear IgG, granular IgG with C3 and humps, granular IgG with C1q and subendothelial deposits, and mesangial IgA correspond to anti-GBM disease, poststreptococcal GN, lupus or MPGN, and IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: crescents are proliferating parietal epithelial cells with monocytes and macrophages in Bowman space, and pauci-immune disease is the majority of crescentic GN. COMLEX: about one-fifth of crescentic GN is anti-GBM disease and one-quarter is immune complex disease, and forms other than anti-GBM disease respond well to steroids and cytotoxic agents.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — comparing anti-GBM, immune complex, and pauci-immune RPGN by immunofluorescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has dark brown urine that began during a sore throat, lasted 3 days, and then cleared. He has had two similar episodes over the past year. Blood pressure and creatinine are normal, hearing is normal, and no relative has kidney disease. Urinalysis after the latest episode shows dysmorphic red cells and 1+ protein. Which set of biopsy findings is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial IgA and C3 with mesangial dense deposits","B":"Linear IgG along the basement membrane without deposits","C":"Granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial humps","D":"Granular IgG along capillary walls with subepithelial deposits","E":"Lamellated basement membrane with basketweave splitting"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Recurrent gross hematuria lasting several days after a respiratory infection, with normal hearing and no family history, is typical of IgA nephropathy. Immunofluorescence shows mesangial IgA, often with C3 and properdin, and electron microscopy shows electron-dense deposits predominantly in the mesangium. Linear IgG marks anti-GBM disease, subepithelial humps mark poststreptococcal GN, granular capillary wall IgG with subepithelial deposits marks membranous nephropathy, and basketweave lamellation marks Alport syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA nephropathy is the most common glomerulonephritis worldwide, with mesangial IgA and C3 and usually no C1q or C4. COMLEX: 15 to 40 percent progress to chronic renal failure over 20 years, and older onset, heavy proteinuria, hypertension, and extensive glomerulosclerosis predict progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 1 — IgA deposition patterns and the recurrent gross hematuria timeline","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has recurrent hematuria with red cell casts and mild proteinuria. He has bilateral sensorineural hearing loss and lens dislocation on eye examination. His maternal uncle began dialysis at age 35, and his mother has microscopic hematuria with normal renal function. Which electron microscopic finding is most likely on kidney biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Uniform thinning to about 200 nm with intact lamina densa","B":"Subepithelial humps with neutrophils in the capillary lumen","C":"Irregular thickening and thinning with basketweave lamination","D":"Subendothelial deposits with tram-track duplication","E":"Ribbonlike dense material permeating the lamina densa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hematuria with red cell casts, sensorineural hearing loss, lens dislocation, and a family history consistent with X-linked inheritance indicate Alport syndrome. Electron microscopy shows irregular foci of GBM thickening alternating with attenuation and pronounced splitting and lamination of the lamina densa, producing a basket-weave appearance. Uniform attenuation without lamination fits thin basement membrane disease, subepithelial humps fit poststreptococcal GN, tram-track duplication with subendothelial deposits fits MPGN, and ribbonlike dense deposits fit dense deposit disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alport syndrome results from type IV collagen defects, about 85 percent X-linked, with hematuria, sensorineural deafness, and eye lesions. COMLEX: males develop overt renal failure between 20 and 50 years of age, whereas female heterozygotes typically present with hematuria and preserved function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — type IV collagen ultrastructure across inherited basement membrane disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Uniform attenuation without la"}},{"id":"sdl43_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has had three episodes of gross hematuria, each beginning during an upper respiratory infection. Blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg, serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and proteinuria is 300 mg/day. Biopsy shows mesangial IgA and C3 deposits. He asks about his long-term outlook. According to Robbins, which statement is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Most patients need dialysis within 5 years","B":"Slow progression in 15 to 40 percent over 20 years","C":"The deposits clear completely within several months","D":"Nephrotic syndrome develops in most within a decade","E":"Rapidly progressive disease follows in about half"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins describes a highly variable course in IgA nephropathy: many patients keep normal renal function for decades, and slow progression to chronic renal failure occurs in 15 to 40 percent of cases over 20 years. Rapid progression to dialysis is not typical, and only 5 to 10 percent present with an acute nephritic syndrome, some with rapidly progressive disease. The mesangial deposits persist and episodes of hematuria typically recur every few months, and nephrotic syndrome is not the expected long-term outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 30 to 40 percent of patients have only microscopic hematuria, with or without proteinuria. COMLEX: onset in old age, heavy proteinuria, hypertension, and extensive glomerulosclerosis on biopsy signal a higher risk of progression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — prognostic modeling of glomerular hematuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man treated with a sulfonamide for a urinary tract infection develops fever, a maculopapular rash, and a creatinine rise from 0.9 to 2.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) 12 days into therapy. Urinalysis shows hematuria, mild proteinuria, and leukocyturia, and urine culture grows no bacteria. Which histologic finding is most likely on kidney biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Edematous interstitium with eosinophils and lymphocytes and tubulitis","B":"Neutrophilic tubular casts and neutrophilic tubulitis with cortical microabscesses","C":"Sloughed tubular epithelium with skip areas of tubular necrosis","D":"Hyaline arteriolar thickening with subcapsular glomerular scars","E":"Coarse polar corticomedullary scars with thyroidization of tubules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fever, rash, hematuria, mild proteinuria, and leukocyturia beginning 2 to 40 days after a sulfonamide, with a sterile urine culture, indicate drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis. The interstitium shows edema and infiltration by lymphocytes and macrophages, with eosinophils and neutrophils often in clusters, plus tubulitis. Neutrophilic tubular casts with microabscesses define acute pyelonephritis, sloughed necrotic epithelium with skip areas defines ATI, hyaline arteriolosclerosis with subcapsular scars defines benign nephrosclerosis, and polar scars with thyroidization define chronic pyelonephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs bind tubular components to create modified self antigens that evoke IgE or T cell mediated hypersensitivity. COMLEX: about 25 percent have rash and about 50 percent develop a rising creatinine, and 30 to 40 percent of acute interstitial nephritis has no identifiable drug.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — hypersensitivity markers that separate interstitial nephritis from suppurative infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man undergoes emergency repair of a ruptured aortic aneurysm with 45 minutes of hypotension. Over the next 9 days his urine output averages 250 mL/day while his BUN and potassium rise. On day 10 the urine volume climbs steadily to 2.8 L/day and his BUN begins to fall. Which of the following is the greatest hazard during this phase?","choices":{"A":"Salt and water overload from ongoing severe oliguria","B":"Water and electrolyte depletion from damaged tubules","C":"Life-threatening hyperkalemia from persistent severe oliguria","D":"Severe metabolic acidosis from failing renal acid excretion","E":"Permanent anuria from bilateral renal cortical necrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The steadily rising urine volume with a falling BUN marks the recovery phase of ATI, when urine output may reach up to 3 L per day. The tubules are still damaged, so large amounts of water, sodium, and potassium are lost in the flood of urine, creating a risk of water and electrolyte depletion. Salt and water overload, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis are hazards of the oliguric maintenance phase, and cortical necrosis belongs to thrombotic microangiopathy or severe ischemia rather than to recovery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATI phases are initiation at about 36 hours, maintenance with 40 to 400 mL per day, and recovery with up to 3 L per day, and recovery depends on intact basement membranes and epithelial regeneration. COMLEX: monitor volume status and electrolytes in the polyuric phase, and remember the peculiar vulnerability to infection at this stage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 3 — intrarenal feedback loops and the hazards of the recovery phase of tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia has had nocturia for 8 months. Ultrasonography shows bilateral hydronephrosis. Which functional abnormality most likely appeared first as a result of the obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Marked fall in glomerular filtration rate","B":"Nephrotic-range proteinuria with lipiduria","C":"Red cell casts with dysmorphic hematuria","D":"Impaired urine concentrating ability","E":"Metabolic alkalosis with renal sodium retention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In bilateral partial obstruction the earliest manifestation is inability to concentrate urine, reflected by polyuria and nocturia. High pelvic pressure is transmitted back through the collecting ducts and compresses the medullary vasculature, reducing inner medullary blood flow, so the initial functional alterations are largely tubular and the GFR falls only later. Nephrotic-range proteinuria and red cell casts are glomerular findings, and Robbins lists distal tubular acidosis and renal salt wasting, not alkalosis with sodium retention, among the tubular defects that can develop in bilateral partial obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstruction first causes tubular concentrating defects, with GFR decline later, and interstitial inflammation leads eventually to fibrosis. COMLEX: relief of obstruction in the first few weeks restores normal function, and a massive sodium-rich diuresis can follow relief of complete obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — mechanical, vascular, and functional progression of hydronephrosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High pelvic pressure is transm"}},{"id":"sdl43_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman has 2 days of fever, confusion, and petechiae. Hemoglobin is 7.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with schistocytes on the smear, platelet count is 16,000/μL (reference range: 150,000–400,000/μL), and creatinine is 1.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). She has had no diarrhea, and plasma ADAMTS13 activity is markedly reduced. Which of the following best explains the thrombi in her small vessels?","choices":{"A":"Shiga-like toxin activating endothelium and platelets","B":"Unopposed alternative pathway C3 convertase activity","C":"Urinary antithrombin III loss with hypercoagulability","D":"Immune complexes lodging in glomerular capillary walls","E":"Ultralarge von Willebrand multimers activating platelets"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fever, neurologic symptoms, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal dysfunction form the classic pentad of TTP, in which central nervous system involvement dominates and severe renal injury is uncommon. ADAMTS13 deficiency, most often from inhibitory autoantibodies, lets very large von Willebrand factor multimers accumulate, bind platelet glycoproteins, and spontaneously activate platelets, producing microthrombi. Shiga-like toxin causes typical HUS after diarrhea, unopposed alternative pathway activity underlies complement-mediated atypical HUS with normal ADAMTS13, antithrombin III loss is a hypercoagulable feature of nephrotic syndrome, and glomerular immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis rather than microangiopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schistocytes with severe thrombocytopenia, neurologic symptoms, and low ADAMTS13 indicate TTP. COMLEX: plasma exchange removes autoantibodies and supplies functional ADAMTS13, and it treats TTP successfully in more than 80 percent of patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — microthrombi, hemolysis, and thrombocytopenia across the thrombotic microangiopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman had a biopsy-proven glomerulonephritis 12 years ago that has been serologically quiescent for 8 years. Her GFR has now fallen to 40 percent of normal and continues to decline, with proteinuria and hypertension. A repeat biopsy shows global glomerulosclerosis, interstitial scarring, and tubular atrophy. Which of the following best explains the ongoing decline?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing antigen-driven immune complex deposition from the original disease","B":"Recurrent ascending bacterial infection with neutrophilic tubulitis","C":"Hyperfiltration of surviving nephrons driving sclerosis and fibrosis","D":"Compensatory nephron regeneration with falling glomerular capillary pressure","E":"Chronic obstruction from ureteral stones with progressive hydronephrosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once nephron loss reduces the GFR to about 30 to 50 percent of normal, progression to end-stage disease continues at a steady rate independent of the original insult. Surviving glomeruli undergo hypertrophy with increased blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure, which injures podocytes and drives progressive glomerulosclerosis, and tubulointerstitial fibrosis follows and correlates closely with functional decline. Ongoing immune injury would show serologic and histologic activity, and infection, capillary hypotension, and obstruction are not supported by the findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the final common pathway of chronic kidney disease shows glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and arteriosclerosis, with TGF-beta central to matrix deposition. COMLEX: renin-angiotensin system inhibitors lower intraglomerular hypertension and are the most successful intervention to slow progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — the shared mechanism of progression across chronic kidney diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ongoing immune injury would sh","B":"Ongoing immune injury would sh","D":"Ongoing immune injury would sh","E":"Ongoing immune injury would sh"}},{"id":"sdl43_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has severe hypertension for 6 months, a bruit over the left flank, and elevated plasma renin activity. Imaging shows a small left kidney and a normal-sized right kidney. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the narrowing that led to her hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Atheroma at the origin of the main artery","B":"Cholesterol atheroembolism of arcuate arteries","C":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis of small arteries","D":"Fibromuscular thickening of the arterial wall","E":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Severe hypertension, a flank bruit, elevated renin, and a small ischemic kidney indicate renal artery stenosis. Fibromuscular dysplasia, fibrous or fibromuscular thickening that may involve the intima, media, or adventitia, is more common in females and in younger age groups, typically the third and fourth decades. Atheromatous plaque at the renal artery origin causes 70 percent of stenosis but occurs mostly in older men, atheroemboli occlude arcuate and interlobular arteries after aortic procedures, and hyperplastic and hyaline arteriolosclerosis are small-vessel lesions of malignant and benign hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal artery stenosis causes hypertension through renin release from the ischemic kidney and angiotensin II, and almost all patients show a fall in pressure with angiotensin II blockade. COMLEX: unilateral disease is potentially curable by surgery, with cure rates of 70 to 80 percent in well-selected cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — renovascular hypertension and the renin-mediated vicious cycle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease has had slowly progressive azotemia for 12 years and long-standing hypertension. His family asks which complication is most likely to end his life. According to Robbins, what is the most common cause of death in adults with this disease?","choices":{"A":"Coronary or hypertensive heart disease","B":"Ruptured intracranial berry aneurysm","C":"Systemic or urinary tract infection","D":"Renal cell carcinoma arising in cyst walls","E":"Hepatic failure from liver cysts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins notes that patients may survive many years with azotemia slowly progressing to uremia, and that about 40 percent of adults ultimately die of coronary or hypertensive heart disease, 25 percent of infection, and 15 percent of a ruptured berry aneurysm or hypertensive intracerebral hemorrhage. Infection is therefore the second most common cause and aneurysm rupture is less frequent. Renal cell carcinoma is not listed among the leading causes, and the liver cysts present in about 40 percent of patients are usually asymptomatic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: subarachnoid hemorrhage from berry aneurysms accounts for death in 4 to 10 percent of individuals. COMLEX: this disease accounts for about 5 to 10 percent of end-stage kidney disease requiring dialysis or transplantation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — clinical course of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Infection is therefore the sec"}},{"id":"sdl43_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections has right flank pain. Urine pH is 8.0, urine culture grows a urease-producing Klebsiella species, and CT shows a large radiopaque calculus forming a cast of the right pelvicalyceal system. According to First Aid, which management is most appropriate for this stone?","choices":{"A":"Urine alkalinization with potassium citrate","B":"Allopurinol to reduce urate production","C":"Thiazide diuretic to lower urine calcium","D":"Clear the infection and remove the stone","E":"Chelation therapy with penicillamine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones form after infection with urease-positive organisms such as Proteus mirabilis, Staphylococcus saprophyticus, and Klebsiella, which hydrolyze urea to ammonia and alkalinize the urine, and they commonly grow as staghorn calculi. First Aid lists eradication of the underlying infection and surgical removal of the stone as treatment. Alkalinization and allopurinol are used for uric acid stones, thiazides reduce calcium stone recurrence, and penicillamine is a chelating agent for refractory cystine stones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: struvite stones are radiopaque, form at high urine pH, and show coffin-lid crystals. COMLEX: First Aid lists struvite as about 15 percent of stones, whereas Robbins gives 5 to 10 percent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — urine pH and crystal chemistry of renal calculi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Alkalinization and allopurinol"}},{"id":"sdl43_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man receiving chemotherapy for acute leukemia has a left ureteral stone that is seen on CT but not on a plain radiograph. Serum uric acid is 11.8 mg/dL (reference range: 3.5–7.2 mg/dL), and urine pH is 5.1. According to First Aid, which treatment best prevents further stones of this type?","choices":{"A":"Thiazide diuretic with a low-sodium diet","B":"Urine alkalinization with allopurinol","C":"Antibiotics with surgical stone removal","D":"Chelation therapy with penicillamine","E":"Loop diuretic to increase urine flow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uric acid stones form in acidic urine, are radiolucent, and are common in diseases with rapid cell turnover such as leukemia. First Aid lists alkalinization of the urine and allopurinol as treatment: raising urine pH increases uric acid solubility, and allopurinol lowers urate production. Thiazides with sodium restriction treat calcium stones, antibiotics with stone removal treat struvite stones, penicillamine is reserved for refractory cystine stones, and a loop diuretic raises urinary calcium without addressing uric acid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uric acid stones are visible on CT, invisible on plain film, and form rhomboid or rosette crystals. COMLEX: Robbins notes that more than half of patients with uric acid stones have neither hyperuricemia nor increased urinary uric acid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — uric acid stone chemistry and radiodensity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man who smokes has intermittent hematuria, flank pain, and a 6-kg weight loss. Hematocrit is 55% (reference range: 41–53%), and serum calcium is 12.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). CT shows a solitary 9-cm heterogeneous mass in the upper pole of the right kidney with a tumor column extending through the renal vein toward the inferior vena cava. Which molecular abnormality most likely drives this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Gain-of-function MET mutation with trisomy 7","B":"TFE3 gene translocation at Xp11.2","C":"Fumarate hydratase loss with aggressive papillary histology","D":"Multiple chromosome losses with extreme hypodiploidy","E":"Biallelic VHL inactivation with stabilized HIF-1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A solitary mass in a smoker with a tumor column in the renal vein extending toward the inferior vena cava fits clear cell RCC, the most common subtype at 70 to 80 percent, which characteristically invades the renal vein. Polycythemia and hypercalcemia are among the hormone-related syndromes of renal cell carcinoma. In 98 percent of these tumors one copy of chromosome 3 is deleted at 3p25.3, the site of VHL, and the second copy is disabled in up to 80 percent, so the VHL ubiquitin ligase fails to degrade HIF-1 and its levels stay high even under normoxia, driving VEGF and IGF-1. MET gain and trisomy 7 characterize papillary carcinoma, TFE3 translocation defines Xp11 carcinoma, fumarate hydratase loss underlies hereditary leiomyomatosis and RCC, and hypodiploidy characterizes chromophobe carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clear cell RCC arises with chromosome 3 and VHL loss and has polygonal clear cells rich in lipid and glycogen, and First Aid lists PTHrP, ectopic EPO, ACTH, and renin as paraneoplastic products. COMLEX: tobacco doubles the incidence, and the lungs, over 50 percent, and bones, 33 percent, are the most common metastatic sites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — molecular drivers and gross behavior of the renal cell carcinoma subtypes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with sickle cell trait has gross hematuria and right flank pain. CT shows an infiltrative mass centered in the renal medulla. Biopsy shows malignant cells forming glands within a prominent desmoplastic stroma. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","B":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma","C":"Renal medullary carcinoma","D":"Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","E":"Renal oncocytoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins describes collecting duct carcinoma as malignant glands enmeshed in a prominent desmoplastic stroma in a medullary location, and medullary carcinoma as a morphologically similar neoplasm seen in patients with sickle cell trait. Clear cell carcinoma forms a yellow cortical mass of clear cells with a delicate vascular network, papillary carcinoma shows papillae with foamy macrophages, chromophobe carcinoma shows pale cells with perinuclear halos, and oncocytoma shows mitochondria-rich eosinophilic cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: collecting duct carcinoma makes up about 1 percent or less of renal epithelial neoplasms and arises from medullary collecting duct cells. COMLEX: sickle cell trait also predisposes to renal papillary necrosis from sickling in the hypoxic, hypertonic medulla.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — renal tumor subtypes and their clinical settings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man who smokes is found to have a 9-cm clear cell renal cell carcinoma, and staging studies are ordered before nephrectomy. According to Robbins, which site is the most common location of distant metastasis from this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Brain","B":"Lungs","C":"Liver","D":"Adrenal glands","E":"Bones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal cell carcinoma tends to metastasize widely before it gives rise to local symptoms, and Robbins lists the lungs as the most common site of metastasis (more than 50 percent), followed by bones (33 percent), then regional lymph nodes, liver, adrenal glands, and brain. Bone is therefore the second most common site, and the liver, adrenal glands, and brain are less frequent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: average 5-year survival is about 70 percent, close to 100 percent without distant metastases, and about 60 percent with renal vein invasion or extension into perinephric fat. COMLEX: about 15 percent of new patients already have radiologic evidence of metastases at presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — clinical behavior and spread of renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Bone is therefore the second m","E":"Bone is therefore the second m"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl43_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has nephrotic syndrome with serum albumin of 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Biopsy shows subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes, and serum anti-PLA2R antibodies are positive. After 3 months of prednisone alone, his proteinuria has not improved. Which therapy is most appropriate next?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroid monotherapy","B":"Intravenous immunoglobulin","C":"Plasma exchange to remove antibodies","D":"Eculizumab complement blockade","E":"B-cell depletion with rituximab"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Subepithelial deposits with spikes and anti-PLA2R antibodies indicate primary membranous nephropathy, which resists corticosteroid monotherapy and is treated with B-cell depletion (rituximab) or combination immunosuppression. Corticosteroid monotherapy has already failed. Plasma exchange is used for anti-GBM disease, not membranous nephropathy. Eculizumab blocks complement in atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome. Intravenous immunoglobulin is not a membranous nephropathy treatment. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 43, membranous nephropathy steroid responsiveness); Robbins Ch. 20 (membranous nephropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: minimal change disease responds to steroids, FSGS responds poorly, and membranous nephropathy needs rituximab or combination therapy. COMLEX: screen for secondary causes such as malignancy in membranous nephropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 1 — podocytopathies are separated by LM, IF, and EM findings","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl43_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with SLE has hematuria, red cell casts, and a creatinine of 2.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Biopsy shows wire-loop lesions with proliferation in more than half of glomeruli and full-house immunofluorescence. Which serum complement pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low C3 and low C4","B":"Normal C3 and normal C4","C":"Low C3 and normal C4","D":"Normal C3 and low C4","E":"High C3 and high C4"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diffuse proliferative (class IV) lupus nephritis activates the classical complement pathway, consuming both C3 and C4. Low C3 with normal C4 reflects alternative pathway consumption, as in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Normal C3 and C4 fit pauci-immune ANCA disease. Normal C3 with low C4 does not fit classical pathway consumption, which lowers both. High C3 and C4 are acute-phase changes, not an immune complex nephritis pattern. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 43, class IV lupus nephritis complement profile); Robbins Ch. 20 (lupus nephritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low C3 plus low C4 means classical pathway (lupus), low C3 only means alternative pathway (PSGN), and normal complement means pauci-immune or anti-GBM disease. COMLEX: complement levels help track lupus nephritis activity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 1 — nephritic diseases are separated by IF pattern and complement levels","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Normal C3 and C4 fit pauci-imm","D":"Normal C3 with low C4 does not"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl43_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has had microscopic hematuria since childhood and now has proteinuria. Audiometry shows bilateral high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss, and his maternal uncle receives dialysis. Which eye finding is most associated with his disease?","choices":{"A":"Retinitis pigmentosa","B":"Bilateral aniridia","C":"Lisch nodules of iris","D":"Anterior lenticonus","E":"Cherry-red macula"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hematuria from childhood, sensorineural hearing loss, and an affected maternal uncle indicate X-linked Alport syndrome (COL4A5). Its ocular defects include anterior lenticonus, posterior cataracts, and corneal dystrophy. Retinitis pigmentosa occurs with nephronophthisis in Senior-Løken syndrome. Aniridia occurs in WAGR syndrome with Wilms tumor. Lisch nodules mark neurofibromatosis type 1. A cherry-red macula occurs in lysosomal storage diseases and central retinal artery occlusion. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 43, Alport syndrome extrarenal features); Robbins Ch. 20 (Alport syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alport syndrome is hematuria, hearing loss, and anterior lenticonus, with basket-weave GBM lamination. COMLEX: thin basement membrane disease has hematuria without hearing or eye findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 1 — Alport EM shows basket-weave lamination of the GBM","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl43_q4","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man is found confused after drinking antifreeze. He has a high anion gap acidosis, and his creatinine rises to 4.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) over 2 days. Urinalysis shows envelope-shaped crystals. Which renal histologic pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Patchy necrosis with tubulorrhexis","B":"Interstitial eosinophils with tubulitis","C":"Diffuse proximal tubule necrosis","D":"Medullary papillary necrosis","E":"Crescents filling Bowman space"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ethylene glycol is a nephrotoxin, and nephrotoxic acute tubular injury causes continuous necrosis along the proximal convoluted tubule, here with calcium oxalate crystals. Patchy necrosis with tubulorrhexis is the ischemic pattern. Interstitial eosinophils with tubulitis describe drug-induced interstitial nephritis. Crescents filling Bowman space describe rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. Medullary papillary necrosis follows ischemic causes such as diabetes or analgesic use. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 43, toxic vs ischemic acute tubular injury); Robbins Ch. 20 (acute tubular injury).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrotoxic ATI gives continuous proximal tubule necrosis, and ischemic ATI gives patchy necrosis with tubulorrhexis; both give muddy brown casts. COMLEX: calcium oxalate crystals with a high anion gap suggest ethylene glycol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 2 — ATN and AIN differ in triggers, urinalysis, and histology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl43_q5","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has a blood pressure of 232/134 mm Hg, headache, and blurred vision. His creatinine is 3.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and the kidney surface shows pinpoint petechial hemorrhages. Which arteriolar change is expected on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas in arterial walls","B":"Homogeneous hyaline arteriolar walls","C":"Atheromatous plaque at the ostium","D":"String-of-beads medial fibroplasia","E":"Onion-skin intimal hyperplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Blood pressure above 180/120 mm Hg with acute renal failure and a flea-bitten kidney indicates malignant nephrosclerosis, which shows hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis (onion-skin intimal thickening) with fibrinoid necrosis. Homogeneous hyaline arteriolar walls describe benign nephrosclerosis from long-standing milder hypertension. Atheromatous plaque at the ostium describes atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis. String-of-beads medial fibroplasia describes fibromuscular dysplasia in young women. Granulomas in arterial walls describe granulomatous vasculitis. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 43, malignant nephrosclerosis); Robbins Ch. 20 (malignant hypertension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyaline arteriolosclerosis means benign nephrosclerosis, and onion-skinning with fibrinoid necrosis means malignant hypertension. COMLEX: severe hypertension with end-organ injury is an emergency that needs controlled blood pressure lowering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 1 — ACE inhibitors and ARBs can precipitate AKI in bilateral renal artery stenosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl43_q6","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a 6-cm renal mass and new hypertension of 168/98 mm Hg. His hemoglobin, serum calcium, and morning cortisol are all normal. Imaging shows no renal artery narrowing. Which mechanism best explains his hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Tumor secretion of PTHrP","B":"Tumor secretion of renin","C":"Tumor secretion of EPO","D":"Tumor secretion of ACTH","E":"Compression of the renal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal cell carcinoma can release renin, causing hypertension as a paraneoplastic effect. Tumor secretion of PTHrP causes hypercalcemia, and his calcium is normal. Tumor secretion of EPO causes erythrocytosis, and his hemoglobin is normal. Tumor secretion of ACTH causes Cushing syndrome, and his cortisol is normal. Compression of the renal artery is excluded by imaging. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 39 and SDL 43, RCC paraneoplastic renin release); Robbins Ch. 20 (renal cell carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC can secrete renin, EPO, PTHrP, and ACTH, and it can cause Stauffer syndrome. COMLEX: new hypertension with a renal mass may resolve after nephrectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 2 — paraneoplastic syndromes of clear cell RCC","isHighYield":false}]};
